{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/react.review.md && nl -ba reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/react.fix.report.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t<!-- Reviewer: Codex gpt-6.2-astra, effort xhigh (observed in the session rollout, thread 01a1ecae-3654-7253-956b-adcdb3b22b87), read-only, cross-provider. Reviewed commit be123ee. -->\n     2\t\n     3\t**B for shipping `be123ee`.** The documented `import { Line } from '@axylo/react'` fails. The most consequential runtime defect is the gap between React’s layout and passive effects. A parent can receive a ref whose chart is still `null`, or append to a chart whose new controlled data has committed in React but has yet to reach the core.\n     4\t\n     5\t### Findings, most severe first\n     6\t\n     7\t1. **B · The package exports files that the build never produces. Ran and inspected.** Node returned `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND` for `@axylo/react/dist/index.js`. The declaration entry is missing too. The root build targets only `packages/axylo`, and the adapter’s TypeScript project has `noEmit`. The size test nevertheless passes at **984 B gzip** because it bundles `src/index.ts` in memory. With the installed esbuild, that direct entry retained `\"use client\"`; a bundle that re-exported `Line` from another entry lost it. Build the adapter JS and declarations, select the development hint appropriately, then import, type-check, server-render and measure the **packed export**. Assert the directive on the shipped entry. This blocks DX1, DX7, DX8 and DX10 release verification. [Package exports](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/package.json:8) · [Build](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/scripts/build.mjs:5) · [Size test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/size.test.ts:10)\n     8\t\n     9\t2. **M · The ref becomes observable before creation and before updates. Reasoned from the adapter and installed React 19.3.1.** `useImperativeHandle` publishes during layout. Creation and patching happen in passive effects. On mount, a callback ref or parent layout effect sees `handle.chart === null` and receives no notification when it becomes usable. On update, commit `data=B`, then call `append(row)` in the parent layout effect. The core receives `append` against A, followed by `update({data:B})`, which erases the row. A diagnostics read in that layout effect likewise describes A. The core obeys §1.5 call order throughout. Synchronize creation and accepted props in the child’s client layout phase before publishing the usable handle. Test this sequence without waiting for frames, including on Suspense reveal. This affects §1.7 streaming and DX5. [Adapter](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:53) · [React’s imperative-handle implementation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/node_modules/react-dom/cjs/react-dom-client.development.js:9111)\n    10\t\n    11\t3. **M · A committed handler change can still call the previous handler. Reasoned.** Commit `onDiagnostic=B` over A, then trigger a synchronous diagnostic through the ref from a parent layout effect. The stable forwarder still reads A. Removing `onHover` has the same window in which the removed callback can fire. Once passive effects run, present → absent → present works, and diagnostics from the adapter’s own `update` see the new handler. Publish the latest handlers in the child’s client layout phase before externally observable chart calls. Add replacement, removal and reinstatement tests that fire during the commit. This affects §1.7 handlers and §1.5 synchronous diagnostic delivery. [Forwarder and ref update](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:51)\n    12\t\n    13\t4. **M · Explicit `undefined` can fail to restore a default. Ran the diff and modeled the public ref sequence.** Mount with `title` absent, call `chart.update({title:'Live'})` through the ref, then render `title={undefined}`. `diff({}, {title:undefined})` returns `null`, so `Live` survives. The reverse own-key transition also returns `null`. The test explicitly requires both results. Compare own-key presence as well as values and send an own `title: undefined` on the transition. Test against a prior imperative update so the reset has an observable effect. This violates §1.5 patch semantics and §1.7 removal semantics. [Diff](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:28) · [Test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/patch.test.ts:45)\n    14\t\n    15\t5. **M · Structural comparison can overflow before core validation. Ran.** Two separately allocated field objects with the same `field` and a self-referential metadata property produced `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`. A structurally typed field variable can carry that extra property. Two mistakenly supplied React elements with cyclic props also overflowed; two ordinary ownerless elements compared equal. `diff` runs outside the `update` try block, so the adapter can take down the chart with its own exception. Bound comparison to supported option shapes or track visited pairs and treat unsupported objects, including React elements, as opaque. Add a regression that reaches the update path. This affects §1.7 field comparison and DX4 diagnostic delivery. [Comparator](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:39) · [Call site](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:87)\n    16\t\n    17\t6. **M · Filling a hole in an option array can silently discard the change. Ran.** `diff({y:Array(1)}, {y:['v']})` returned `null`. Reversing the arguments emitted a patch. `a.every(...)` skips the previous hole, so correcting a sparse wide `y` or `reference` list can leave the chart on its old state. Compare indexed presence and values symmetrically, and test a sparse-to-dense correction in both directions. This violates whole-value replacement in §1.5. [Array comparison](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:43)\n    18\t\n    19\t7. **M · Container attributes go to chart validation and disappear from the DOM. Ran SSR, `optionsOf` and TypeScript probes.** `id`, `aria-label` and `data-testid` were absent from the server div and present in core options. TypeScript rejected `id` and `onClick` but accepted the hyphenated JSX attributes. Under the specified AX150 behavior, an ordinary `data-*` attribute can therefore reject a strict chart. Omitting `id` also prevents the documented element-id fallback for diagnostic names. This produces the same omission on server and client; it does not require a hydration mismatch to fail. Define the supported host attributes, route them to the div on both sides, and preserve validation of genuine unknown chart options. §1.7 does not promise every div prop, but forwarding these attributes to chart validation has observable DX3/DX4 consequences. [Prop types](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:19) · [Div](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:100) · [Option routing](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:9)\n    20\t\n    21\t8. **M · Importing only `Line` retains all five chart factories. Ran an in-memory consumer bundle.** The output constructed all five components and imported all five core factories. With distinct stand-in implementations, all five survived bundling. Marking the component factory calls pure in memory reduced the retained implementations to `line`. `sideEffects: false` alone does not eliminate those calls inside a used module. Preserve pure annotations through the emitted distribution or split the component entries, and check a packed `Line`-only consumer. This affects the plan’s per-chart tree-shaking goal and DX7. [Component exports](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:106)\n    22\t\n    23\t9. **m · An equal replacement can incur a full traversal on every subsequent render. Ran.** Replace a large `colors` record or `reference` list with an equal new object, then keep that new object stable. The null-patch return leaves `state.applied` pointing to the older object. In Node, 20 comparisons of 20,000 entries took about 72 ms for `colors` and 25 ms for `reference`. That is comparator time alone; the cost of a real core update was unmeasured. Record the latest successfully compared props on a no-op, and reserve unbounded structural work for options that require it. Same-reference in-place mutations are also invisible, so the comparison assumes immutable option values. `colors` is a record in the public API. [No-op return](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:87) · [Traversal](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:45)\n    24\t\n    25\t10. **m · Object enumeration can lose a field change or produce the wrong reset value. Ran.** Two plain field specs with different non-enumerable `field` values compared equal. Removing an own `toString` option produced the inherited function as the patch value. `optionsOf` also copied inherited enumerable props. These paths bypass the intended §1.5 replacement/reset or DX3 unknown-option handling. Compare the documented properties of field specs, enumerate own props when routing, and use own-key checks or prototype-free dictionaries for patch lookup. [Option copying](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:15) · [Lookup](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:30)\n    26\t\n    27\t11. **m · The memoization hint can advise against a real data update. Ran.** Equal-length arrays with identical endpoints and a changing middle row returned `true` from `looksSame`. Equal dates reconstructed in endpoint rows and freshly allocated typed columns with identical values returned `false`. After three qualifying replacements, the warning asserts that the data was the same. The module-wide `hinted` flag then suppresses hints for other charts. Confirm content before issuing that claim, or word the hint as a sampling heuristic and specify its scope. The §1.7 hint is affected. Actual `data` patches still use identity. [Sampling](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:53) · [Warning](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:79)\n    28\t\n    29\t12. **m · Deferring an effect error to component state can lose it. Reasoned from React’s commit-error path.** The comment that an effect throw never reaches a boundary is incorrect for a synchronous effect setup. If the chart effect catches a strict error and a parent removes the child in the same passive-effect batch, the child can disappear before it renders `failure`; a surviving boundary then receives nothing from this path. Let synchronous setup/update exceptions propagate to React’s commit-error handling, and test original-object identity with a boundary that stays mounted. In ordinary recovery, the current code does throw the original object, the fallback unmounts the failed instance, and reset mounts a fresh one. Changing props alone cannot clear `failure` on that instance. [Error handling](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:49) · [React’s boundary capture](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/node_modules/react-dom/cjs/react-dom-client.development.js:20217)\n    30\t\n    31\t13. **m · A setup failure after successful creation has no cleanup path. Reasoned; conditional on core behavior.** If listener registration throws, including through an immediately invoked user listener, the effect exits before returning its disposer. The successfully created chart then has no owner to destroy it. §1.5 promises teardown when `destroy()` is called; it does not specify registration-time event delivery. Destroy the local chart before propagating a setup failure. Separately, a factory that allocates resources and then throws must roll them back itself because the adapter receives no handle. Neither case requires `on` or `destroy` to throw a *strict validation* error. [Creation and listener installation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:61)\n    32\t\n    33\t14. **m · The teardown test observes an already settled chart. Reasoned from the harness.** `render()` waits for frames and `ready()` before unmount. A mutation that lets a pending draw run after teardown can pass because the draw has already happened. The destroy spy stores a boolean, so double destruction also passes. The listener probe excludes detached nodes and all `once` listeners, and the spy retains chart elements. Count destroys, unmount before the pending frame, and assert against the saved handle and scheduled callback. The current test establishes less than the DX5 teardown and leak criteria. [Waits](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/react.browser.test.ts:55) · [Spy](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/spy-axylo.ts:26) · [Probe](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/probe.ts:69)\n    34\t\n    35\t### Test coverage and mutations the seven reported checks missed\n    36\t\n    37\t| Tests examined | What their assertions distinguish, and what can still pass |\n    38\t|---|---|\n    39\t| `optionsOf`, `diff`, `equal`, `looksSame` | The examples distinguish ordinary additions, changes, removal of defined values, whole nested replacements, function changes and handler filtering. Deep-equality assertions can accept a copied `data` value. The property uses production `equal` as its oracle and compares indexed values, so it can share the comparator’s defect and miss own-key differences. Add independent presence, identity and symmetry assertions. [Patch tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/patch.test.ts:13) |\n    40\t| Five browser lifecycle cases, StrictMode, independence | The cases check factory kind, title updates, destruction and isolation between two charts. A render-phase write of `latest.current`, a duplicate destroy, or a stale saved handle can pass. Exercise an abandoned transition, an actual key change, and a saved getter across cleanup. [Lifecycle cases](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/react.browser.test.ts:82) |\n    41\t| Inline options, removed title, removed tooltip, memoization hint | The inline test counts updates. Title checks the own reset key and generated name. Tooltip checks restored behavior. None exercises removal of required fields. The hint test would accept a warning one or two replacements late because it checks only after three more renders. Assert on each replacement and include changing middle rows. [Prop cases](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/react.browser.test.ts:135) |\n    42\t| Handlers, ref, strict errors | Hover is exercised after settling. A broken `select` or `zoom` forwarder, stale layout-phase callback, or broken diagnostic removal can pass. The ref test always appends to a ready chart. The boundary test checks message text and remount counts; wrapping the error or advancing `applied` before a rejected update can pass because recovery creates a new instance. [Handler, ref and error cases](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/react.browser.test.ts:213) |\n    43\t| Server, hydration and size | The five SSR cases establish empty markup and zero factory calls. The class/style case checks exact HTML. The directive assertion reads source. The hydration control detects deliberately altered markup, but successful hydration never checks reuse of the original div and both sides use source. All these tests can pass with a broken package entry. Save the server node for an identity assertion and run the distribution checks through the package name. [Server cases](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/server.test.ts:23) · [Hydration](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/react.browser.test.ts:312) · [Size](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/size.test.ts:12) |\n    44\t| Type fixtures | I removed all 11 suppressions **in memory**. Every marked line failed. The misspellings, text value, donut options and scatter category produced the intended diagnostics. The two wide-`y` cases first complain about widened `string[]`; using `as const` isolates the forbidden combination. Every directive can mask another error on its line. Pair each negative case with the same otherwise-valid expression. JSX inferred rows and typed columns. `createElement(Line, …)` and `memo(Line)` lost row inference; specializing `Line<Row>` worked. [Type fixtures](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/types.check.tsx:32) |\n    45\t\n    46\t### Verification and contract limits\n    47\t\n    48\t`npm run typecheck` passed in the current checkout, including `test/types.check.tsx`. I ran the adapter’s Node suite with filesystem writes disabled, native config loading, threads, and both Vitest caches disabled. Its final lines were:\n    49\t\n    50\t```text\n    51\tTest Files  3 passed (3)\n    52\t     Tests  25 passed (25)\n    53\t  Start at  04:25:38\n    54\t  Duration  518ms (transform 65%, import 24%, tests 10%, worker 2%)\n    55\t```\n    56\t\n    57\tI changed no files. Browser behavior, Fast Refresh and React 18 were assessed by reading. Only React 19.3.1 ran. I did not inspect the changing core implementation. The adapter assumes the target’s `axylo` class is sufficient when React rewrites `className`. The browser spy also calls extracted `update` and `destroy` methods without their receiver; §1.5 allows a conforming implementation to use `this`. Preserve the receiver in the spy. The manifest has no direct `react-dom` peer, which matches the adapter’s imports; the workspace lockfile still lists that peer and should be synchronized. A consumer still needs a DOM renderer to mount or server-render the components. [Host class](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:98) · [Spy](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/spy-axylo.ts:26) · [Lockfile](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/package-lock.json:2271)\n    58\t\n    59\tNo core contract change is needed for these fixes. The fixed public `Chart` exposes `zoom()` and `on('zoom', …)` for **every** chart. I verified that a donut ref accepts both, while donut JSX rejects `zoom` and `onZoom`, and `donutRef.chart.update({zoom:true})` is rejected. A chart-specific handle that excludes those methods would require a deliberate public API decision. [Public handle](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/public.ts:137)\n    60\t\n    61\t### Areas examined and found sound\n    62\t\n    63\t- **Committed renders.** Reasoned. Creation, diffing and writes to `applied` occur in effects. An abandoned render publishes no options, callback change or patch.\n    64\t- **Normal lifecycle.** Reasoned. StrictMode deliberately creates twice with cleanup between setups. A key change disposes the old instance. A Fast Refresh remount or effect replay pairs successful setup with cleanup. The adapter’s normal path clears `live` before destruction. A caller who explicitly destroys the raw chart can still cause later adapter updates to reach it; §1.5 makes those calls no-ops.\n    65\t- **Suspense and ref identity.** Reasoned for installed React 19. A Suspense fallback disconnects the layout ref while the existing chart’s passive effect can remain connected. Reveal can publish a new wrapper around that chart. With an unchanged ref, ordinary rerenders keep the wrapper stable. A saved getter reads `null` after actual passive cleanup; during StrictMode replay it can subsequently see the replacement chart.\n    66\t- **Strict rejection and recovery.** Reasoned. A thrown `update` leaves `applied` at the last successful options, consistent with §1.5 rollback. The latest handler ref can already reflect the rejected render. Boundary remount gets fresh state. A strict `append` through the raw ref throws synchronously to its caller, and an event-handler caller must catch it there.\n    67\t- **Ordinary patches.** Ran. Removing previously defined `data`, `x`, `y` and `onDiagnostic` emitted own keys with `undefined`. Missing required fields then reach core validation. Event props use subscriptions, with absent handlers becoming no-ops after synchronization. The diagnostic forwarder is installed for a supplied callback and cleared on removal.\n    68\t- **Ordinary comparison.** Ran. Dense nested values replace whole options. Dates compare by timestamp, including two invalid dates. `NaN` compares equal and signed zeros differ. Functions, maps, typed arrays and class instances compare by identity. Data replacement uses identity; only the development hint inspects row endpoints or column references.\n    69\t- **Source SSR and option types.** Ran. All five components rendered an empty div with deterministic class/style, a null server ref and zero factory calls. Each JSX prop type uses its corresponding public options type, including area stacking, bar layout, scatter field restrictions and donut fields. By inspection, the hooks and `forwardRef` used here are available in React 18.\n     1\t<!-- Author: Claude Opus 5.5 (requested effort high), Agent-tool subagent a5529f23e59e816c4, resumed for the fix pass. Saved by the coordinator from the hand-back. -->\n     2\t\n     3\t## @axylo/react fix pass: report\n     4\t\n     5\tI fixed all 14 findings. For findings 1–8 and 10–14 I wrote a test first, saw it fail against the be123ee adapter for the reviewer's reason, then fixed the code. Finding 9 has a test but no one-line fix. All tests in packages/react now pass. The adapter now ships from a build: the production `dist/index.js` is **1427 B gzipped** (gzip level 9; 3377 B before gzip). Nothing is committed.\n     6\t\n     7\t**Command for the root build script**, after the core build because declarations need `packages/axylo/dist/index.d.ts`:\n     8\t```\n     9\tnode packages/react/build.mjs\n    10\t```\n    11\tThe root `typecheck` script already ends with `&& tsc -p packages/react`, and it passes. `tsc -p packages/react` works without dist.\n    12\t\n    13\t### Runs I made\n    14\t\n    15\t**Before the fix:**\n    16\t- New `patch.test.ts` cases against the old patch.ts: `Tests 8 failed | 14 passed (22)`.\n    17\t  - Errors seen: `{ title: undefined }` expected but got null; `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`; `Array(1)` to `['v']` gave a null patch; inherited props copied (`[ 'data', 'inherited' ]`); host props reached options.\n    18\t- New browser tests against the be123ee adapter: `Tests 13 failed | 18 passed (31)`.\n    19\t  - Errors seen: `expected 10 to be 11` (append erased); `Set{ 'diagnostic:A' }` instead of `'diagnostic:B'`; `[ 'zoom:A' ]` instead of `'zoom:B'`; `expected 5 to be 2` (repeated traversal); container attributes missing; `[0,0,0,1,1,1]` instead of `[0,0,0,2,2,2]` (hint per instance); `caught.length 0` instead of 1; `destroys [0]` instead of `[1]`.\n    20\t\n    21\t**After the fix:**\n    22\t- `npx tsc -p packages/react`: clean. It is also clean with dist removed.\n    23\t- `npm run typecheck`: passes.\n    24\t- `npx vitest run packages/react`: `Test Files 2 passed (2)`, `Tests 34 passed (34)`, Duration 1.15s.\n    25\t- `AXYLO_BROWSER=1 npx vitest run packages/react`, run twice: `Test Files 1 passed (1)`, `Tests 31 passed (31)`, Duration 16.76s.\n    26\t- Full `npm test`: `Tests 7 failed | 620 passed (627)`. All 7 are outside my package: six in `packages/axylo/test/theme/palette.test.ts` and one timing test in `packages/axylo/test/data/normalize.test.ts`.\n    27\t- **Finding 8 mutation:** with the production file built using `minify: true`, \"keeps only the line chart (conditions: default)\" fails. Restored afterwards, and the file passes again.\n    28\t\n    29\t### Per finding\n    30\t\n    31\t1. **No dist (B): fixed.**\n    32\t   - New `packages/react/build.mjs` and `tsconfig.build.json` produce:\n    33\t     - `dist/index.js`: production, `__DEV__` false, so the hint is removed.\n    34\t     - `dist/index.dev.js`: development, with the hint.\n    35\t     - `dist/index.d.ts`.\n    36\t   - This follows the core's convention: the files are chosen through a `development` export condition, not `NODE_ENV`. package.json exports are now `types`, `development` and `default`.\n    37\t   - Both files start with exactly one `'use client';` and contain no `__DEV__`.\n    38\t   - Tests in `test/dist.test.ts`, which builds first: \"starts with the 'use client' directive and has no bare __DEV__\" (both files); \"the production file drops the development hint…\"; \"the production file is at most 2 KiB gzipped\"; \"the declarations are what a consumer type-checks against\" (runs tsc on `test/consumer/consumer.tsx`, which imports `@axylo/react`; it fails when dist is absent); and SSR through `@axylo/react` and `dist/index.js`.\n    39\t   - I deleted the old `server.test.ts` and `size.test.ts`, which tested src.\n    40\t2. **Ref before create / call order (M): fixed.**\n    41\t   - Create, patch and handler publication now run in `useLayoutEffect` on the client. They are declared before `useImperativeHandle`, so they run before it.\n    42\t   - On the server `useEffect` is used, so React 18 gives no SSR warning (not verified: React 18 is not installed).\n    43\t   - Destroy runs in a passive cleanup, so a Suspense hide keeps the chart.\n    44\t   - Tests: \"on mount, a callback ref and the parent layout effect see a created chart\"; \"a parent layout effect that appends after a data change appends to the new data\"; \"diagnostics read in a parent layout effect describe the props of that commit\"; \"after a Suspense fallback, the revealed chart is the same one and matches the revealing commit\".\n    45\t3. **Stale handler during commit (M): fixed.** `latest` is written in the layout effect.\n    46\t   - Tests: \"onDiagnostic replaced, removed and reinstated takes effect within the commit\" and \"onZoom replaced, removed and reinstated…\". Both fire through the ref from the parent's layout effect.\n    47\t   - Also \"an abandoned transition applies nothing…\", a guard against writes during render.\n    48\t4. **Explicit undefined (M): fixed.** Presence now counts, and the patch carries an own `title: undefined`.\n    49\t   - I corrected the wrong-requirement test into \"sends the transition between an absent prop and an explicit undefined, as an own key\".\n    50\t   - Browser test: \"title={undefined} after a title set through the ref restores the generated name\".\n    51\t5. **Comparator overflow (M): fixed.**\n    52\t   - Comparison now tracks the pairs it has already visited.\n    53\t   - React elements (`$$typeof`), class instances and anything not plain are compared by identity.\n    54\t   - `equal` catches internally, so a throwing getter means \"unequal\".\n    55\t   - Test: \"never throws, and treats values it does not understand by identity\". It covers cyclic field specs, mutually recursive objects, a throwing getter and cyclic React elements.\n    56\t6. **Sparse arrays (M): fixed.** Index presence is compared, and the comparison is symmetric. Test: \"sees a hole filled or opened in an option array, in both directions\".\n    57\t   - The property test now uses an oracle independent of `equal`, which tells holes, undefined and absence apart and checks symmetry.\n    58\t7. **Host attributes (M): fixed as you decided.**\n    59\t   - `id`, `className`, `style` and `data-*` go to the div on server and client and never reach the options.\n    60\t   - `aria-*` reaches core validation; the reason is in a comment in `patch.ts`.\n    61\t   - Types: `ContainerProps` with `id` and `` `data-${string}` ``.\n    62\t   - Tests: the optionsOf unit test; dist SSR \"id, className, style and data-* render on the container…\"; browser \"id, className, style and data-* reach the container, never the chart; aria-* is reported as an unknown option\" (a strict chart with `data-testid` is not rejected, and `aria-label` produces AX150).\n    63\t8. **Tree-shaking (M): fixed.** The five definitions carry `/* @__PURE__ */`.\n    64\t   - esbuild's whitespace minification drops those marks. So the production file minifies identifiers and syntax only, which is why it is 1427 B rather than 1203 B.\n    65\t   - Test: \"keeps only the line chart\", for the default and `development` conditions. It bundles a consumer that imports only `Line`, with marker stand-ins for the core.\n    66\t9. **Repeated traversal (m): fixed.** `applied` now advances on a no-op too. Test: \"an equal replacement object is compared once, not on every later render\", using a proxy that counts key reads.\n    67\t   - I did not limit how deep structural comparison goes for any option. Options are assumed immutable, and a comment in `diff` says so.\n    68\t10. **Enumeration (m): fixed.** Comparison uses own keys including non-enumerable ones (`Reflect.ownKeys`), `optionsOf` copies own props only, and patch lookups check own keys. Tests: \"compares own properties, enumerable or not, and never reads inherited ones\" and \"copies own props only\".\n    69\t11. **Hint (m): fixed.** It is now per chart instance and worded as a sample (\"same length and the same first and last rows (only those are compared)\").\n    70\t    - Endpoint dates are compared by time, and typed columns by length and endpoints.\n    71\t    - Tests: the looksSame unit test, and browser \"the memoization hint: once per chart, on the third endpoint-equal replacement, worded as a sample\". That test asserts after each replacement, with a middle row changing.\n    72\t12. **Error path (m): fixed.**\n    73\t    - **Observed on React 19.3.1:** a throw from the layout effect reaches the boundary as the original object (`caught === spy.errors` holds, by identity), on create and on update. So I removed the state-and-rethrow path.\n    74\t    - Test: \"a strict rejection reaches the boundary even when the parent removes the chart right after the commit\". The old code lost the error there.\n    75\t13. **Setup failure (m): fixed.** The chart is destroyed before the error from `on` propagates. Test: \"a setup failure after the chart exists destroys it before the error propagates\".\n    76\t14. **Teardown test strength (m): fixed.**\n    77\t    - The spy now counts destroys, holds charts and elements through `WeakRef`, and calls `update` and `destroy` with the handle as `this`.\n    78\t    - The probe now tracks `once` listeners too.\n    79\t    - New tests: \"unmounting before the first frame cancels it: nothing is drawn after destroy, destroy runs once\"; \"a key change destroys the old chart once and creates a new one\"; a saved handle reads `null` after unmount; hydration checks that the server-rendered div is reused.\n    80\t    - Also added \"the latest onSelect is called on click\".\n    81\t\n    82\t### For you to decide or relay\n    83\t- **Core (not mine):** after destroy, the core leaves listeners on its own removed elements (keyboard proxy `input`, toolbar, legend and control buttons). They cannot fire. The StrictMode test lists them and asserts that every such element is one of the core's `axylo-*` elements, none of them React's container. Strict cleanliness would need a change in `src/dom`.\n    84\t- **Core behaviour (not mine):** a click right after hovering lands on the floating tooltip, so no `select` fires. The select test uses `tooltip: false` for that reason.\n    85\t- **Trade-off:** the Suspense decision means the chart survives being hidden. An Activity or Offscreen hide, which disconnects passive effects, destroys it, and the reveal creates a new chart from the latest props.\n    86\t- **Not verified:** React 18 (only 19.3.1 is installed) and Fast Refresh.\n    87\t- `package-lock.json` still lists the `react-dom` peer. That file is not mine.\n    88\t- `packages/react/test/browser/.out/` (the generated test bundle) is still not covered by `.gitignore`; dist is covered by the existing `dist/` rule.\n    89\t\n    90\tAll files are in /home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/:\n    91\t- new: `build.mjs`, `tsconfig.build.json`, `test/dist.test.ts`, `test/consumer/consumer.tsx`, `test/consumer/tsconfig.json`\n    92\t- changed: `src/index.ts`, `src/patch.ts`, `package.json`, `tsconfig.json` (excludes `test/consumer`), `test/patch.test.ts`, `test/types.check.tsx` (each negative case now has a valid twin; wide `y` uses `as const`), `test/browser/app.tsx`, `test/browser/probe.ts`, `test/browser/spy-axylo.ts`, `test/browser/react.browser.test.ts`\n    93\t- deleted: `test/server.test.ts`, `test/size.test.ts`\n    94\t\n    95\tThe core under `packages/axylo/src` was being edited during my runs. None of my browser failures came from the core.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/bench.review.md && nl -ba reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/bench.fix.report.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t<!-- Reviewer: Codex gpt-6.2-sol, effort max (observed in the session rollout, thread 01a1ecaf-0ff2-7513-8792-7690dd671450), read-only, cross-provider. -->\n     2\t\n     3\t**Decision 0002 should be provisional.** The M1 data supports keeping SVG as the working default for the line-chart slice while retaining Canvas. It does not establish a shipping winner, a P3 pass, or a reason to remove Canvas.\n     4\t\n     5\tThis was a read-only review. I ran the Node tests, replayed history through the CLI, and ran counterexamples in Node. Browser behavior was assessed from source and recorded runs. The test glob also includes a suite that rebuilds `bench/dist` and launches Chrome, so I ran `node --test --test-isolation=none bench/test/stats.test.mjs`.\n     6\t\n     7\t```text\n     8\tℹ tests 12\n     9\tℹ suites 0\n    10\tℹ pass 12\n    11\tℹ fail 0\n    12\tℹ cancelled 0\n    13\tℹ skipped 0\n    14\tℹ todo 0\n    15\tℹ duration_ms 57.184433\n    16\t```\n    17\t\n    18\t## Findings\n    19\t\n    20\t1. **B · Decision 0002 treats exploratory results as a shipping decision.** **Ran Node on history and inspected the plan.** The 3×120 and 100,000-point create intervals include zero, the DPR 1 dashboard interval falls inside the session noise band, and the million-point comparison has four pairs. The 10,000-point create and DPR 2 dashboard intervals allow improvements as small as **0.16 ms** and **6.28 ms**, respectively. M1 gives verdicts on 149 Canvas–SVG metric comparisons, many correlated, with no adjustment for selecting favorable results. The decision also lacks declared minimum gains and regression tolerances, evidence that tight-budget workloads are safe, and the byte, fidelity and complexity checks required by §5. **Fix.** Keep the default provisional. Declare primary endpoints and thresholds before rerunning. Use simultaneous or multiplicity-adjusted inference for claims across workloads, and require the remaining §5 checks before shipping or removing a renderer. [Decision 0002](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md:9) · [M1 results](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md:77) · [Shipping rules](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/04-plan-performance.md:124)\n    21\t\n    22\t2. **B · The streaming and resize results cannot establish that no frame was late.** **Ran Node on raw history and synthetic timestamps.** P3’s initial chart and settle have a separate window. The stream counter starts before scheduling the worker feed and ends when a page rAF callback sees the final draw record. It includes dispatch, append, deferred main-thread work and the harness’s tracking. It divides that total by submitted samples, then takes the median across runs. That matches the plan’s aggregate-per-update definition. If P3’s “median” means the median cost of individual updates, it is a different statistic. The worker’s 60 Hz timer is independent, but a draw record read in rAF precedes presentation, and coalesced samples are credited at the same callback. Some M1 runs counted 299 intervals for 300 samples. A synthetic 150 ms interval straddling the resize window boundary yielded **zero** late intervals. **Fix.** Define the intended P3 statistic explicitly, count a full window including boundary-spanning intervals, and validate which sequence actually reached presentation. Scope the current zero to the recorded callback intervals. [Streaming window](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:130) · [Frame summaries](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/stats.js:117) · [P3](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:243) · [Decision 0002](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md:25)\n    23\t\n    24\t3. **B · The production profiles cannot attribute the 100,000-point bottleneck.** **Ran Node on the raw profiles and inspected the mapper.** Production leaves **73.1% Canvas and 71.0% SVG** of busy time unmapped. GC is identifiable at **16.8% and 22.7%**. The **37.4% and 43.2%** attributed to normalization and **1.7% and 2.6%** attributed to drawing come from development builds. The deepest Axylo frame gets the sample even when a native callee is the leaf, so this is neither JS self time nor inclusive time for each ancestor. It cannot recover unreported inlined frames, attribute root GC to an allocator, or measure off-thread raster. The window also includes the settle period, and there is no uPlot profile to explain the gap. **Fix.** Archive the exact production maps, report self and inclusive samples separately, and join stage spans with presentation and raster traces. Keep normalization as a hypothesis from the dev runs. [Profile window](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/driver.mjs:147) · [Profile attribution](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/profile-summary.mjs:94) · [100,000-point profiles](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md:492) · [Decision 0002](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md:39)\n    25\t\n    26\t4. **B · Admission accepts charts whose drawing has lost fixture features.** **Ran Node against the installed Chart.js decimator and the real admission function.** On the 100,000-point gappy fixture at 800 px, the tuned decimator retained 2,981 points and 60 of 5,000 NaNs. I found **144 adjacent retained finite pairs** spanning a missing value in the original input. Admission passed because it reads the held dataset. The historical tuned run also reports 2,981 drawn points and admits it. A synthetic readback with correct extents, counts and timestamps and no `missing` field passed too, with no drawing required to construct it. The domain checks catch the Phase 1 pinned-axis failure, but extent containment does not prove that the extreme or gaps were drawn. **Fix.** Require missing-value readback and validate the rendered marks or selected source indices for gap breaks, extremes and displayed windows. Sample presented frames against untimed references. [Admission predicate](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/admission.js:24) · [Chart.js held-data readback](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/chartjs-common.js:24) · [Recorded gappy comparison](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/history/20261118T010745Z-first-1x100000-gaps-uplot-vs-chartjs-tuned-2d3a.json) · [Harness admission claim](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/README.md:3)\n    27\t\n    28\t5. **B · The resize CPU result cannot pin the cost on raster, and the memory claim drops its denominator.** **Ran Node on history and inspected process sampling.** The 100,000-point resize’s **1,035 → 1,770 ms** is whole-renderer CPU. It does not identify a raster stage, and the SwiftShader string identifies the WebGL probe rather than the Canvas 2D or SVG raster path. The resize CPU trade-off, DPR 2 timing and process-memory differences need a headed GPU-backed rerun before transfer. “Roughly half” applies to the **DPR 2 increase above data-only renderer PSS** at 34.23 → 17.71 MiB. Absolute renderer PSS was 74.65 → 56.21 MiB, and the DPR 1 increase was 23.11 → 18.38 MiB. P7 is still unmeasured. The million-point heap and process deltas and `width × height × 4` estimate cannot identify Axylo-owned bytes. The harness does no replacement-peak measurement or 100/300/1,000-cycle leak and listener check. **Fix.** Trace stage and process ownership on the headed runs, label the PSS quantities precisely, and measure owned buffers and lifecycle retention separately. [Process sampling](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/driver.mjs:188) · [PSS results](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md:341) · [P7 method](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/04-plan-performance.md:54) · [Decision 0002](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md:29)\n    29\t\n    30\t6. **M · Replaying a recorded comparison silently removes its noise band.** **Ran the CLI.** `node bench/analyze.mjs 20261118T023718Z-page-3x120-x24-axylo-canvas-vs-axylo-svg-41e7` printed the original two noise IDs while calling the **−22.01 ms** task result **better**. Supplying those same IDs explicitly with `--noise` restored **inconclusive**, as published. The CLI initializes `noise` to `null` and recomputes the verdict while retaining the old label. **Fix.** Print the stored analysis by default. On reanalysis, resolve and validate the recorded bands or explicitly request and label a zero-only analysis. Print the band actually used per metric. [Analyzer](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/analyze.mjs:12) · [Formatter](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/report.mjs:13) · [Published dashboard result](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md:154)\n    31\t\n    32\t7. **M · Failed work can win the speed comparison.** **Ran two Node counterexamples.** A stream with the correct final held window but two sequences never acknowledged by the draw reader passed admission after the watchdog. Eight such candidate runs beat eight complete baseline runs **0.50 vs 2.00 ms/update**, with interval **[−1.50, −1.50]**. Separately, a 20-block run where B timed out 14 times and took 5 ms on the six survivors beat A at 10 ms on every run. Failures are reported separately, but neither the inner stream watchdog nor differential exclusion prevents a primary speed verdict. **Fix.** Give unfinished streams a failure outcome. Make completion and deadline reliability gate speed, and publish survivor-only timing as conditional. Track adverse-run rates too. In a third probe, two candidate runs with 60 late intervals each still gave the same median count and **[0, 0]** interval as a clean baseline. [Stream watchdog](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:187) · [Driver admission](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/driver.mjs:80) · [Complete-pair filter](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/stats.js:214)\n    33\t\n    34\t8. **M · The create workloads are proxies for P1, P2 and P5.** **Inspected source and ran Node on history.** The CDP start snapshot precedes a fresh task and the first create call. The end follows all adapter readiness promises, one more rAF callback and a 500 ms settle. The window includes adapter conversion, chart creation, main-thread script, style, layout and any main-thread paint work charged during it. Fixture generation, checksum, adapter load/setup, empty-container layout and forced memory collections are outside. `readyFrameMs` is sampled in the callback before paint. Work on other threads or after the settle has no coverage. Axylo also scans the held frame through `describe()` inside the window, once on create and on each stream append’s readiness, even though create does not need streaming visibility. P2 uses the right typed-column sizes, with 8 and 4 pairs. P1/P5 use synthetic walks named `s0`–`s2`, whereas the named `trend` has real label lengths and a seven-day gap. **Fix.** Use the named fixture, the specified 20 runs, a constant-time commit hook, and sampled screenshots and traces to validate readiness and settle boundaries. [Create window](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:89) · [Preparation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:356) · [Axylo instrumentation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/axylo-common.js:76) · [Named `trend` fixture](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/research/harness/src/data.js:9)\n    35\t\n    36\t9. **M · Rebuilding from current source can measure SVG against itself.** **Ran the adapters against a Node spy and traced the renderer branch.** Canvas advertises `renderer=canvas` while omitting `__renderer` from the call. Current source selects SVG unless passed `'canvas'`. The browser test asserts the echoed configuration string, so it can pass with both charts using SVG. This is a rerun defect. The M1 create records show two chart canvases per Canvas chart and zero for SVG. **Fix.** Explicitly select both renderers and require actual renderer identity in the committed readback. Test that identity after a production rebuild. [Adapter create](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/axylo-common.js:61) · [Current renderer selection](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/chart.ts:72) · [Browser test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/test/admission.test.mjs:85)\n    37\t\n    38\t10. **M · Noise admission permits mismatched controls and a loaded machine.** **Ran the CLI and inspected history.** The loader accepted the **DPR 1** A/A bands on the **DPR 2** dashboard, changing its task verdict from better to inconclusive. It checks workload and serialized parameters only. It can accept finite intervals from fewer than six pairs, mismatched adapters, builds, settle times or hosts. The replay CLI only warns about a Chrome mismatch. M1 unions percentile intervals from one session for only two workload shapes. That is not a distribution of repeatability across three independent sessions. The recorded load average reached 6.8 while other work ran, with no gate on representative refresh, power, contention or drift. **Fix.** Match controls by build, environment and workload, require sufficient complete pairs across independent sessions, and predeclare what makes a session invalid. Leave uncalibrated metrics exploratory. [Noise loader](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/refs.mjs:23) · [Machine record](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/driver.mjs:42) · [M1 noise calibration](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md:54)\n    39\t\n    40\t11. **M · Six-pair percentile intervals can give a confident answer in the wrong direction.** **Ran Node.** A is always 10 ms. B is 5 ms with probability 0.49 and 11 ms otherwise, so its true median is 11 ms. With five 5s and one 11, the harness returned **better, [−5, −2] ms**. Five or six 5s occur **10.03%** of the time, and both cases give the wrong direction. The implementation takes 10,000 whole-pair resamples and the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles for `median(B) − median(A)`. The bootstrap seed is fixed at 12345. `--seed` controls block order only, and an invalid order seed silently becomes zero in the PRNG. **Fix.** Validate and record both seeds. Use the pilot to set sample sizes and check coverage on tied, skewed and discrete metrics. Calibrate inference to the chosen estimand and the balanced design before permitting verdicts. [Bootstrap](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/stats.js:13) · [Seed handling](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/run.mjs:47) · [PRNG](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/fixtures.js:8)\n    41\t\n    42\t12. **M · Equal AB/BA counts leave differential carry-over unidentified.** **Ran synthetic blocks and inspected isolation.** Every first run took 10 ms. Either adapter took 4 ms after A and 12 ms after B, with no adapter effect in the same starting state. Four AB and four BA blocks still called B better with **[−6, −2] ms**. The real runner uses fresh pages and contexts, so there is no same-page chart carry-over. It reuses one Chrome process and the host, and admission always runs A before B. There is no symmetric warm-up or analysis by order. The bootstrap also resamples across both orders without preserving their fixed balance. **Fix.** Define cold and warm states, standardize warm-up, inspect order-specific estimates, and isolate browser processes if a counterbalanced pilot detects carry-over. Preserve order strata in inference. [Run order](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/run.mjs:113) · [Context isolation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/driver.mjs:132) · [Bootstrap sampling](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/stats.js:26)\n    43\t\n    44\t13. **M · Resize and theme can admit incomplete visual responses.** **Reasoned from source and checked the recorded counters.** A chart fixed at the initial 1,200 px can pass resize admission because the box returns to 1,200 px and only the widest graphic is checked afterward. uPlot’s `ready` promise is the already-resolved initial draw promise. A busy adapter can also receive fewer resize steps because width advances by elapsed time. For theme, changing one stroke can satisfy the alternating-signature test while other marks stay stale. The timed counters instrument Canvas calls and DOM mutations, and between-toggle canvas readbacks add asymmetric work. Both M1 renderers recorded six mutations per toggle. Zero Canvas calls for SVG does not establish that SVG required no runtime update or repaint. **Fix.** Validate intermediate and final committed geometry and expected colors per mark. Measure counters and pixel sampling in separate runs, and compare presentation latency alongside task cost. [Resize end check](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:313) · [uPlot readiness](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/uplot.js:35) · [Theme timing](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:239) · [M1 theme results](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md:190)\n    45\t\n    46\t14. **M · Required budget workloads and decision-sensitive cases are missing.** **Inspected workload dispatch and enumerated history in Node.** P4 has no real pointer moves on the 100,000-point chart, matching-readout hook, or input-to-presentation p95. P8 has no 40-chart compact workload. `--charts 40` creates ordinary charts. The exact P1/P5 `trend` workloads and the P7 ownership and lifecycle runs are also missing. P6 has a separate size script, but no packed-entry comparison or Canvas-retention byte result in M1. Dense or clustered scatter, 10×100,000, and pan and zoom have no renderer measurements. Resize and theme **were measured** in headless Chrome, and one million points **was measured** with four pairs. The plan’s row/irregular-x variants, mixed dashboard, larger streaming windows, replace, keys/table, busy page, other chart types, cold load, browser matrix, forced colors, print and browser zoom lack recorded M1 evidence. **Fix.** Add the missing budget probes and prioritize the absent or underpowered cases that could reverse the renderer choice. [Workload dispatch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/refs.mjs:5) · [Performance budgets](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:239) · [Planned workloads](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/04-plan-performance.md:18) · [M1 acceptance scope](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:204) · [Size script](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/scripts/size.mjs:1)\n    47\t\n    48\t15. **M · The cross-library numbers compare different reader experiences.** **Inspected adapters and checked history in Node.** Inputs, outer CSS boxes, viewport and requested DPR are shared. Plot geometry and fonts differ. uPlot reserves 30 px for its legend and supplies sizes, strokes, millisecond units and a resize observer where needed. It offers a cursor and live legend without Axylo’s tooltip and accessibility layer. Chart.js uses its own canvas font and has tooltips in both configurations, but the adapter provides no equivalent accessibility layer. Its starter configuration includes a time adapter, markers and animations. The declared tuned one changes parsing, input shape, decimation, markers, animation and tooltip hit behavior. Axylo M1 used the minified script-tag bundle with the benchmark hook enabled and no accessibility opt-out. The M1 write-up omits the specific feature differences behind the uPlot gap. The no-op is a harness floor, measured earlier at about 1.26 ms for small create and 0.17 ms/update. **Fix.** Publish the feature matrix alongside starter results, add a matched-reader-task comparison with aligned fonts and animation, and keep the no-op as a contemporaneous control. [Page sizing](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.html:7) · [uPlot options](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/uplot.js:3) · [Chart.js starter](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/chartjs.js:1) · [Chart.js tuned](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/chartjs-tuned.js:1) · [Axylo options](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/axylo-common.js:50)\n    49\t\n    50\t16. **M · History records comparisons but does not detect regressions.** **Inspected the runner, analyzer and CI.** The runner compares adapter references served from one snapshot. The analyzer replays one record. Neither builds the merge base and candidate together, retries a flagged PR, confirms against the last release, or handles a Chrome or host change by rerunning both sides. A sequence of sub-tolerance regressions can accumulate indefinitely without a release comparison. CI invokes tests and a size report, with no paired performance job or `--check` on size. **Fix.** Run two immutable build artifacts in the same balanced job. Add the screening retry, nightly reference-machine confirmation and a last-release comparison. Rebaseline by rerunning both builds on a changed environment. [Runner build selection](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/run.mjs:102) · [CI](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/.github/workflows/ci.yml:27) · [Regression plan](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/04-plan-performance.md:139)\n    51\t\n    52\t17. **M · The report’s provenance can change without the measurements changing.** **Ran Node on the 39 listed records and the live manifest.** They share the same recorded production bundle hash but report three live HEADs, `709fcbf7`, `e6e0e195` and `b3c1a4fa`. The report displays the first HEAD and loads the build timestamp from the current `dist/manifest.json`, which already describes a different bundle. The build hashes the source file after copying it, and the server serves a shared directory a concurrent benchmark build can replace. The report generator also chooses the first matching profile from the directory without matching its artifact to the session. **Fix.** Seal a session-specific artifact directory and hash the files actually served. Record source provenance at build time and generate reports solely from pinned run records, manifests and profile IDs. [Snapshot build](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/build.mjs:36) · [Server root](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/driver.mjs:15) · [Report metadata](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/results/make-m1-report.mjs:38) · [Profile selection](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/results/make-m1-report.mjs:161)\n    53\t\n    54\t18. **m · Several test names assert more than the tests do.** **Ran the pure tests and inspected coverage.** “Widens with noise” has no quieter control. The constant-shift case cannot distinguish the requested estimator from a mean or a median of paired differences. In Node, `A=[1,101,102]`, `B=[100,1,103]` gave a difference of medians of **−1**, while the median paired difference is **+1**. The positive admission fixture builds its readback from the same `expectedView()` under test. There is no direct coverage for analyzer replay, `loadNoise` compatibility, formatted verdict consistency, stream/resize/theme summaries, memory-unavailable paths, profile mapping or actual renderer identity. **Fix.** Add those cases using independent expected values and retain the existing negative admission and lifecycle tests. [Statistics tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/test/stats.test.mjs:24) · [Positive fixture test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/test/stats.test.mjs:69) · [Browser test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/test/admission.test.mjs:85)\n    55\t\n    56\t## Checks that held\n    57\t\n    58\t- **Scheduling and isolation.** Source inspection confirmed an independent worker clock, equal scheduled AB/BA counts, and a fresh page and context for each run.\n    59\t- **Basic statistics.** Node confirmed whole-pair resampling, per-run summaries, and the stated three-way classification and minimum-pair rule on their tested inputs.\n    60\t- **History.** Source and records confirmed distinct outcomes, ordered raw stream samples, and atomic checkpoints that leave interrupted sessions identifiable.\n    61\t- **Profile accounting.** In the recorded profiles, GC samples were rooted separately, idle samples were excluded from busy shares, and unavailable production mapping was labeled.\n    62\t\n    63\t## Keep, weaken, withdraw\n    64\t\n    65\t- **Keep** the M1 raw contrasts and intervals, the inconclusive calls at 3×120, 100,000 and 1,000,000 points, and decision 0002’s retention of the Canvas switch.\n    66\t- **Weaken** “SVG by default” to a provisional choice for the tested line slice. Scope the dashboard memory result to incremental renderer PSS at DPR 2, the frame result to observed callbacks, and the uPlot timing gap to the configurations measured.\n    67\t- **Withdraw** the claims that raster explains the resize CPU gap, that simplification bounds every chart by width alone, that the SVG theme change needs no runtime work, and that production normalization and GC explain the 100,000-point gap or settle the performance budgets. The recorded heap, DOM and layout results also contradict the claim that only resize CPU favored Canvas.\n     1\t<!-- Author: Claude Opus 5.5 (requested effort high), Agent-tool subagent a6075efd5cba2d5e5. Saved by the coordinator from the hand-back. -->\n     2\t\n     3\t## Report: bench harness fixes for the 18 review findings\n     4\t\n     5\tEvery finding in your list (items 1–11) is fixed or partly fixed, each with tests. Both suites pass. All browser numbers below come from smoke runs on a loaded machine and are not evidence. I wrote nothing to `bench/results/*.md` or the decision documents. All edits are in `bench/**`; nothing is committed or staged.\n     6\t\n     7\tThree things you should know first:\n     8\t\n     9\t1. **The M1 dashboard comparison measured no charts.** Every SVG run in `20261118T023718Z-page-3x120-x24-axylo-canvas-vs-axylo-svg-41e7` has `svgPaths: 0`. At 230×115 tiles Axylo's plot is below the 120×80 minimum of spec L8, so it shows its text summary instead of marks. The Canvas runs presumably drew nothing either. Decision 0002 uses the DPR 2 dashboard memory result, which comes from these runs. The new drawn-geometry check rejects such runs (\"the chart drew no line strokes\"). I changed the dashboard tiles to 340×200 in 4 columns, and the 24-chart resize grid to 4 columns of 200 px; Axylo draws marks there.\n    10\t2. **The verdict rule is now 20 complete pairs** (plan 3.3: \"20 runs for full comparisons\"; I read that as 20 per configuration). It was 6. Even at 20 pairs, the coverage tool measured a false \"better\" 5.0% of the time on the reviewer's distribution (2,000 trials). This is stated in the README; I did not invent a new method to fix it.\n    11\t3. **Someone else ran the harness while I was editing it.** Two aborted records appeared at 04:50 (`bench/history/20261118T035036Z-…-956e.json`, `…035037Z…-5708.json`; old command line with `--no-build`), plus two artifacts in `bench/artifacts/`. They are not mine, so I left them. A Chrome process still running belongs to another agent's vitest browser run; I did not touch it.\n    12\t\n    13\t## Per finding\n    14\t\n    15\t| # | State | Covered by | Ran myself vs. expect |\n    16\t|---|---|---|---|\n    17\t| 9 renderer | fixed | `test/axylo-adapter.test.mjs` (spy), `test/drawn.test.mjs`, browser test after a fresh production rebuild, `test/adapters/axylo-wrong-renderer.js` | Ran: both adapters always pass `__renderer`. The renderer is read from the committed chart's elements and must match, in probe and timed runs. A canvas-labelled adapter drawing SVG is rejected. |\n    18\t| 6 replay | fixed | `test/noise.test.mjs` (runs the CLI, including the reviewer's exact M1 command) | Ran: the stored analysis prints by default, and that record shows `inconclusive` as published. `--reanalyze` re-resolves the band or stops with exit 2; `--zero-only` is explicit and labelled. The band used is printed per metric. |\n    19\t| 7 failed work | fixed | `test/reliability.test.mjs` (the reviewer's three counterexamples), browser `uplot-stalls` | Ran: an unfinished stream gets outcome `incomplete`. Failures label every timing \"conditional\". More failures or more late-frame runs on the candidate block \"better\". Failure rates are reported. |\n    20\t| 4 drawn | fixed | `test/drawn.test.mjs` (literal fixtures), browser test | Ran: `missing` is required. uPlot and Chart.js report the indices they drew; bridges, points at missing values, coverage gaps and lost extremes are rejected. Axylo is checked from captured strokes at pixel resolution in the untimed probe run. Tuned Chart.js on 1×100,000 with gaps is rejected (\"185 drawn segments join retained neighbours across missing source values\"); uPlot and both Axylo renderers are admitted. Partly: Axylo gaps under 2 px and Axylo timed runs are not checked (no library hook); uPlot's sub-pixel gap clipping is invisible to its index readback. |\n    21\t| 2 lateness | fixed | `test/reliability.test.mjs` | Ran: intervals that cross a window boundary are counted (the 150 ms case counts). P3 uses exactly 300 intervals from the first due time (smoke: 300 every run). The statistic is stated in code and README. Not done: checking that a frame was actually presented (no traces). |\n    22\t| 10, 11, 12 | fixed / partly | `test/noise.test.mjs`, `test/inference.test.mjs`, `tools/coverage.mjs` | Ran: a noise band must match workload, parameters, settle, DPR, Chrome, headless/headed, host and the hashes of the files it served, with ≥20 pairs; a mismatch is an error. Fewer than 3 sessions is labelled exploratory. Both seeds are validated and recorded. The bootstrap keeps the AB/BA strata; per-order estimates are printed (reviewer's carry-over case: −6 vs −2). Warm-up is symmetric. Not done: separate browser processes, a load or session-validity gate. |\n    23\t| 17 provenance | fixed | `test/provenance.test.mjs` | Ran: each session gets its own read-only artifact. Axylo is compiled from a snapshot of the sources, with every input hashed, and HEAD and uncommitted changes recorded at build time. The server checks each file's hash as it serves it and records what it sent. `make-m1-report.mjs` now reads only the records and a pinned profile list, and needs `--out`; I regenerated it to scratch only. |\n    24\t| 3 profiles | fixed | `test/profile.test.mjs` | Ran: source maps live in the artifact; self and inclusive samples are reported separately; the settle period is a separate profile. A smoke production profile was fully mapped (M1 left 71–73% unmapped). Not done: stage spans, raster traces, a uPlot profile. |\n    25\t| 8 create | fixed | `test/provenance.test.mjs` (trend checked value for value against `research/harness/src/data.js`), adapter spy test | Ran: P1/P5 use `--fixture trend`; the default is 20 blocks; Axylo's stream draw record no longer calls `describe()`. Not done: screenshot or trace validation of readiness. |\n    26\t| 14 workloads | fixed / partly | browser test \"new workloads run end to end\" | Ran: `hover` (P4), `compact` (P8), `mem` and `lifecycle` (P7), `scatter` (10k/50k/200k, `--clustered`), `--size 10x100000`, and `zoom` through `chart.zoom()`. Axylo is **not admitted** on hover (see library needs). Not done: P6, row input, irregular x, mixed page, W-replace/keys/busy/load. |\n    27\t| 13, 15, 16, 18 | fixed / partly | browser tests (`uplot-fixed-width`, `axylo-stale-mark`), `test/summaries.test.mjs`, README | Ran: resize uses a fixed width sequence and checks geometry at the narrowest width too. uPlot readiness waits for its next draw. Theme checks every mark's colour; counters run separately. The feature matrix is in the README. `--base`/`--candidate` two-build mode refuses different harness files; `--screen --tolerance` reruns once and flags on a second failure. Not done: presentation latency for theme, nightly and last-release jobs, CI. |\n    28\t| 1, 5 | not addressed | – | Outside your list. Finding 5 needs a headed GPU rerun. |\n    29\t\n    30\t## Test runs (machine under load)\n    31\t\n    32\tPure tests:\n    33\t```\n    34\tℹ tests 67\n    35\tℹ pass 67\n    36\tℹ fail 0\n    37\t```\n    38\tBrowser tests:\n    39\t```\n    40\t✔ new workloads run end to end: hover (P4), zoom, compact (P8), scatter, mem and lifecycle (P7) (11433.858273ms)\n    41\tℹ tests 14\n    42\tℹ pass 14\n    43\tℹ fail 0\n    44\tℹ duration_ms 81944.577955\n    45\t```\n    46\t\n    47\t## Commands\n    48\t\n    49\t- Pure tests: `node --test bench/test/*.test.mjs` (builds one artifact from `packages/axylo/src`, so it can fail while the core is broken).\n    50\t- Browser tests: `node --test --test-concurrency=1 bench/test/browser/*.test.mjs`\n    51\t- Smoke comparison: `node bench/run.mjs --baseline axylo-canvas --candidate axylo-svg --workload first --size 3x120 --blocks 2 --settle 200 --history-dir <scratch dir>`\n    52\t- Coverage check: `node bench/tools/coverage.mjs`\n    53\t\n    54\t## What I need from the library\n    55\t\n    56\t- A drawn-index readback in `__bench.describe()` (the source indices the simplifier kept per series), so Axylo can be checked index-exactly, in timed runs, and for gaps under 2 px.\n    57\t- **Hover:** on a 10,000-point chart the readout showed 4 distinct data over 40 moves (indices 407, 2826, 5244, 7662), so P4 cannot admit Axylo. Please tell me whether that is a bug or intended.\n    58\t- Optionally, renderer kind in the production `__bench` hook (the DOM readback works without it).\n    59\t- At the current source, `.axylo-summary` is visible in the L8 fallback. That looks intended, but it is why the dashboard tiles drew nothing.\n    60\t\n    61\t## What I need from the plan\n    62\t\n    63\tDeclared tolerances and smallest worthwhile improvements per workload (screening requires `--tolerance` until then), a slope threshold for the P7 lifecycle gate, tile sizes for P5 and P8, and confirmation that \"20 runs\" means per configuration.\n    64\t\n    65\t## Where the spec and the plan disagree\n    66\t\n    67\tP3: the spec says \"Main-thread task time per update; Median\". The plan defines the metric as task time divided by updates, one number per run, which I implemented (median over runs). The spec could also mean the median of individual updates' costs, which TaskDuration cannot separate.\n    68\t\n    69\tOther places where the spec is silent and I chose: P8 is 40 tiles of 160×48, each showing one series of `trend` (spec AX131 suggests one series per compact chart). For P4, Chart.js starter only shows a tooltip when the pointer hits a point marker, so it is not admitted for hover.\n    70\t\n    71\tMain files: `bench/build.mjs`, `bench/run.mjs`, `bench/analyze.mjs`, `bench/src/stats.js`, `bench/src/admission.js`, `bench/README.md`. The old `bench/dist` is stale and no longer used; I left it.\n    72\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba docs/04-plan-performance.md && rg -n '\"'^##|'\"\\\\bP[1-8]\\\\b|React' docs/02-product-spec.md docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba docs/04-plan-performance.md && rg -n '\"'^##|'\"\\\\bP[1-8]\\\\b|React' docs/02-product-spec.md docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Phase 3 plan: continuous performance work\n     2\t\n     3\tRevision 2, 2026-11-18. Revised after three independent reviews ([ledger](../reviews/2026-11-18/ledger.md)).\n     4\t\n     5\tThe repeated improvement work starts when the TypeScript release from [03-plan-typescript-release.md](03-plan-typescript-release.md) works end to end, and that release is its baseline. The **measurement harness is built earlier, in milestone M0**, because the release's own budgets (P1–P7 in [02-product-spec.md](02-product-spec.md)) cannot be checked without it and two design decisions in M1 depend on its numbers.\n     6\t\n     7\t## 1. What Phase 1 established, and what it did not\n     8\t\n     9\t| Observation | What it supports | What it does not support |\n    10\t|---|---|---|\n    11\t| uPlot, plain JavaScript on Canvas 2D, was the fastest library at every size in the harness. | Tuned TypeScript sets a high bar. Any other technology has to beat that, not a naive implementation. | A specific per-update cost. The first run timed only the synchronous part of each call, and uPlot defers its redraw; see the corrected figures in 01, section 5.3. |\n    12\t| Chart.js and ECharts were one to two orders of magnitude faster in their documented fast configuration than by default. | Whole configurations differ enormously; look for avoided work before low-level tuning. | Attribution to any single option. Several options changed at once, including ones that change what the reader sees. |\n    13\t| Recharts and Nivo, which create a DOM node per point by default, were the slowest. | Node count is worth watching. | That SVG is slow. A single SVG path per series was among the fastest. |\n    14\t| Two WebGL configurations ran on a software rasterizer in headless Chrome. | Nothing about WebGL. | Any conclusion about GPU paths, which need a headed browser on real hardware. |\n    15\t\n    16\tThe Phase 1 timings rank configurations by order of magnitude on one machine. They are not the baseline for this plan; the M0 harness produces that.\n    17\t\n    18\t## 2. Workloads\n    19\t\n    20\tEach workload is a page in `bench/` that takes a library adapter, so the same page runs Axylo and the comparison libraries. Every adapter is admitted only after a check that it shows the right thing: the visible x and y range, the newest and oldest timestamp, the window length, and one injected extreme value are read back and compared with the fixture. Phase 1's D3 adapter failed exactly this check after the fact.\n    21\t\n    22\t| ID | Workload | Sizes and shapes | What a reader would notice | Budget |\n    23\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n    24\t| W-first | Create one line chart | 3×120; 1×10k; 1×100k; 1×1M; 10×100k. Rows and typed columns measured separately. Dense, gappy (5% missing in runs) and irregular-x variants | Delay before the chart appears | P1, P2 |\n    25\t| W-page | Create a dashboard | 24 × `trend`; and a mixed page: 10 line, 6 bar, 4 area, 4 donut | Dashboard load | P5 |\n    26\t| W-stream | Append on an independent 60 Hz clock, with sequence numbers | Window 600; 10k; 100k | Stutter; the display falling behind the data | P3 |\n    27\t| W-replace | Replace all data (a filter change) | 10k; 100k; and all 24 charts at once | Delay after changing a filter | – |\n    28\t| W-hover | Move the pointer to known data points, 200 moves | 10k; 100k; 1M | Tooltip lag | P4 |\n    29\t| W-keys | Hold an arrow key across the plot; open the table | 10k; 1M | Keyboard lag; table delay | – |\n    30\t| W-zoom | Zoom in and out 60 steps, then pan | 100k; 1M | Jerky zoom | – |\n    31\t| W-resize | Animate container width 1,200 → 400 → 1,200 px over 2 s | 24 charts; 1×100k | Jank while a sidebar opens | – |\n    32\t| W-busy | The mixed page with one chart streaming, one table open, a resize in progress and a filter change arriving | – | Everything at once, as on a real dashboard | – |\n    33\t| W-types | Stacked bars 12×50 and 5×2,000; stacked area 5×10k; donut 8; scatter 10k uniform and 10k clustered | – | Delay before the chart appears | – |\n    34\t| W-load | Cold page load with the script-tag build and one chart, on a named network profile | – | Time until the first chart is visible | – |\n    35\t| W-mem | Memory, as defined in 3.1 | – | Tab memory; slow leak | P7 |\n    36\t\n    37\tWorkloads without a budget get one when their first baseline exists. Sizes are the ones named in the spec; there is no usage telemetry and none is planned, so they are revised from issue reports, with the reason recorded.\n    38\t\n    39\t## 3. How to measure\n    40\t\n    41\t### 3.1 Metrics\n    42\t\n    43\tEvery workload has one primary metric with a defined start, a defined end state that can be verified, and a statistic.\n    44\t\n    45\t| Metric | Start | End | How |\n    46\t|---|---|---|---|\n    47\t| Time to first correct chart | The create call | The first presented frame in which the chart shows the fixture's data: readiness is signalled by the adapter (Axylo exposes a revision counter for this in benchmark builds), and the signal is validated on sampled runs against a screenshot compared with a reference | `performance.mark`, a `requestAnimationFrame` after readiness; screenshot sampling in separate, non-timed runs |\n    48\t| Main-thread task time | Same | A fixed settle window after readiness | Chrome DevTools Protocol `Performance.getMetrics` (task, script, layout and style durations). This counts work wherever the library schedules it: synchronous, microtask, timer or animation frame |\n    49\t| Update cost when streaming | First append | Last append displayed | Main-thread task time divided by updates; plus, per sequence number, the delay until the frame that displays it, the backlog, and the count of coalesced updates |\n    50\t| Frame pacing | – | – | Intervals between animation-frame callbacks, summarized per run: 95th and 99th percentile, and the count over 1.5 refresh intervals (25 ms at 60 Hz, 12.5 ms at 120 Hz). The refresh rate is recorded |\n    51\t| Input to display | A real pointer or key event dispatched at a known datum, timestamped | The first presented frame in which the readout shows that datum | A lightweight hook in benchmark builds, validated against Chrome traces on sampled runs. The Event Timing API is not used for this: it does not report `pointermove` or `wheel` |\n    52\t| Long stalls | – | – | Long Animation Frames entries, which report frames of 50 ms and more. Used for stalls only, not for the one-frame budgets |\n    53\t| GPU and raster time | – | – | Chrome tracing on selected headed runs |\n    54\t| Memory | – | – | Four separate numbers, never mixed: bytes in buffers Axylo owns (counted directly); JavaScript heap after forced collection (CDP); process memory including canvas backing stores (browser task manager figures via CDP where available); peak during a data replacement. Baselines: a blank page, and the page holding only the input data. Leaks: heap after 100, 300 and 1,000 create–destroy cycles following warm-up, gated on slope, plus a count of remaining listeners and observers |\n    55\t| Bytes | – | – | Minified, gzip and brotli size of each entry point from the packed distribution, CSS included; parse and compile time from a trace |\n    56\t\n    57\tWhere a browser lacks an instrument, the result file says \"not available\" for that browser. It is never filled with a number from another metric.\n    58\t\n    59\t### 3.2 Environment\n    60\t\n    61\t| Dimension | Values |\n    62\t|---|---|\n    63\t| Browsers | Chrome and Edge stable, Firefox stable, headed, with a real GPU. Safari on macOS by hand at releases. Playwright's WebKit build in CI, reported under its own name, not as Safari. |\n    64\t| Hardware profiles | \"Desktop\": the reference machine, recorded with CPU, GPU, OS, display refresh rate and power settings. \"Throttled\": 4× CPU throttling through CDP, Chromium only. \"Phone\": one real mid-range Android phone, used at baseline calibration and at releases, when the device is available; when it is not, the result says so. |\n    65\t| Display | Fixed 1,440×900 window; device pixel ratio 1 and 2. |\n    66\t| Build | Production build of every library; pinned versions; adapter and fixture hashes in the result file. |\n    67\t| Isolation | Fresh browser context per run; no extensions; machine otherwise idle. |\n    68\t| Runnable combinations | Desktop × {Chrome, Firefox, WebKit}; Throttled × Chrome; Phone × Chrome. Nothing else is claimed. |\n    69\t\n    70\tThe reference machine restarts often and has no permanent temporary storage, so every run writes its raw samples and environment record to `bench/history/` in the repository as it goes. A run cut short by a restart is kept, labelled interrupted, and never merged with a later one.\n    71\t\n    72\t### 3.3 Statistics\n    73\t\n    74\t- **Design:** baseline and candidate run in randomized, balanced blocks (AB and BA in equal numbers) inside one session, so drift and order effects fall on both.\n    75\t- **Unit of analysis:** the run. Frames inside one run share a browser, a thermal state and a history, so they are summarized per run before any interval is computed.\n    76\t- **Summary:** median of per-run values with a paired bootstrap 95% interval for the difference; tail and count metrics get their own intervals from the same per-run values.\n    77\t- **Noise:** an A-versus-A distribution, built from repeated blocks across at least three sessions, gives the noise band for each workload. A single A–A difference is not a noise floor; it can be near zero by chance.\n    78\t- **Sample sizes** are set from a pilot in M0 that measures each workload's variance and run time. Until then: 20 complete pairs for a verdict, that is 20 runs of each configuration. With fewer, the result is reported as exploratory, with no verdict. (The first harness used six pairs; a reviewer showed that six can give a confident answer in the wrong direction about one time in ten on a skewed metric.)\n    79\t- **Three outcomes:** better, worse, or inconclusive. \"No significant difference\" is reported as inconclusive, never as \"no regression\".\n    80\t- **Scope of a number:** a result holds for its commit, browser version and machine. Comparisons against stored history are valid only on the same machine and browser version; otherwise both sides are rebuilt and rerun together.\n    81\t\n    82\t### 3.4 Comparison libraries\n    83\t\n    84\t| Library | Configurations | Role |\n    85\t|---|---|---|\n    86\t| uPlot | Default | Speed reference. |\n    87\t| Chart.js | Starter default; feature-equivalent tuned | The most deployed. |\n    88\t| ECharts | Starter default; feature-equivalent tuned | Canvas with built-in large-data modes. |\n    89\t| Highcharts | Default; Boost | SVG with a WebGL path; evaluation use only, subject to its license terms for publishing benchmarks. |\n    90\t| Recharts | Starter default; tuned | What most React users have. |\n    91\t| Nivo | SVG line; Canvas line | A node-per-point library with a canvas alternative. |\n    92\t| D3 | One SVG path per series, with current axes | A control for \"SVG without per-point nodes\". |\n    93\t\n    94\tFor each library, up to three configurations are published where they exist: the starter default, the least configuration that completes the same reader task, and a tuned configuration that keeps the same visible features (axes, legend, tooltip). Every option that differs from the default is listed with the documentation page that recommends it. A causal claim about one option needs a run that changes only that option. Node counts and the active renderer are recorded during the run.\n    95\t\n    96\tAxylo is compared with a comparably equipped library as well as with uPlot, since uPlot does less. Versions are pinned and bumped once a quarter in a commit of their own. Comparison numbers are published with method, versions and configuration files, including the ones Axylo loses.\n    97\t\n    98\t## 4. The improvement loop\n    99\t\n   100\tOne iteration, written up as a short record in `bench/experiments/NNNN-title.md`:\n   101\t\n   102\t1. **Pick the target.** A workload failing its budget comes first. Otherwise rank by what readers meet most: missed frame deadlines in frequent interactions (hover, streaming, resize) before a rare wait, and a 40 ms stall that happens on every dashboard before a second saved on a million-point chart.\n   103\t2. **Find the bottleneck.** Record a profile of that workload on the production build with source maps. Attribute time to stages (normalize, lookup, layout, scene, mark drawing, DOM text, raster) with `performance.measure` spans available in benchmark builds. Record each stage's share of the workload's critical path.\n   104\t3. **State a hypothesis that can fail,** with a number: \"Layout takes 41% of W-page because every tick label is measured twice; caching by font and string will cut W-page by at least 25%.\"\n   105\t4. **Build the smallest experiment** behind a flag or on a branch, with no refactoring mixed in.\n   106\t5. **Measure** candidate against baseline on the target workload and on every other tracked workload.\n   107\t6. **Decide** by section 5 and write the record, including when the hypothesis was wrong. Negative results stay in the repository.\n   108\t7. **Review.** Someone other than the author of the change checks the measurement and the record before it merges.\n   109\t\n   110\tLikely order of attack. Each is a hypothesis, not a commitment:\n   111\t\n   112\t| Order | Area | Why it is likely to matter |\n   113\t|---|---|---|\n   114\t| 1 | Work avoided: explicit invalidation, no relayout when tick labels do not change, simplification bounded by pixels and runs | The large gaps in Phase 1 were between whole configurations that did or did not avoid work. |\n   115\t| 2 | Allocation in hot paths: typed columns end to end, reused buffers, no per-point objects | Streaming and hover run every frame. |\n   116\t| 3 | Drawing command volume: one path per series, batched state changes | uPlot's approach; cheap to try. |\n   117\t| 4 | Text: measurement cache, batched DOM reads and writes, reused label elements | 24 charts multiply every text cost. |\n   118\t| 5 | Lookup: binary search on sorted x; a grid index for scatter that degrades gracefully on clustered data | Bounds hover and keyboard cost. |\n   119\t| 6 | Partial redraw: a separate layer for cursor and focus | Removes full redraws from hover. |\n   120\t| 7 | Startup: module structure, lazy code for zoom and the table | Affects every page. |\n   121\t| 8 | Off-main-thread drawing | High integration cost; only if 1–7 leave a budget failing. |\n   122\t| 9 | WebGL or WebGPU marks | Only if raster time is the measured bottleneck at sizes in the workload table. |\n   123\t\n   124\t## 5. What makes a gain worth shipping\n   125\t\n   126\tEach tracked workload has, written down before any candidate is measured: its primary metric, the smallest improvement worth having in absolute terms, and the largest regression tolerated. All of the following must hold.\n   127\t\n   128\t| Condition | Rule |\n   129\t|---|---|\n   130\t| The gain is real and large enough | The lower bound of the 95% interval for the improvement is above the workload's smallest worthwhile improvement. Excluding zero is not enough. |\n   131\t| The gain is perceptible or structural | It moves a workload across a budget, removes missed frame deadlines (shown with an interval over runs, not one frame in one run), or saves at least the workload's declared minimum in milliseconds. A percentage alone does not qualify: 5% of 0.1 ms is nothing. |\n   132\t| Nothing else pays for it | No other tracked workload's interval shows a regression beyond its tolerance, in each browser where it can be measured. Inconclusive on a workload with a tight budget triggers a rerun, not a pass. |\n   133\t| Bytes are accounted for | The size budget still holds. A change that adds more than 0.5 KiB gzipped needs a workload crossing a budget. |\n   134\t| Correctness is unchanged | Scene snapshots identical, or every difference reviewed. All fidelity property tests pass, in particular F2 and F5. |\n   135\t| Complexity is paid for | A new code path, mode or public option needs a workload crossing a budget or a gain at least four times the smallest worthwhile improvement. Otherwise the simpler code stays. |\n   136\t\n   137\tThresholds a reader can perceive rank above percentages: one frame for interaction and animation, 100 ms for a response to input, 1 s for a chart to appear.\n   138\t\n   139\t### 5.1 Declared endpoints (provisional until the pilot)\n   140\t\n   141\tWritten on 2026-11-18, before any candidate was measured with the corrected harness, so that results cannot choose their own thresholds.\n   142\t\n   143\t**Per workload.** The primary metric is the one its budget names (spec section 5). The smallest worthwhile improvement is 10% of the budget's limit; the largest tolerated regression is 5% of it. Neither may be smaller than the upper edge of that workload's A-versus-A noise band: where the band is wider, the band is the threshold and the workload is listed as too noisy to gate at its nominal tolerance.\n   144\t\n   145\t| Budget | Workload in the harness | Primary metric | Smallest worthwhile | Tolerated regression |\n   146\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n   147\t| P1 | `first`, fixture `trend` | Task time, median over runs | 1 ms | 0.5 ms |\n   148\t| P2 | `first`, 1 × 100,000 | Same | 6 ms | 3 ms |\n   149\t| P2 | `first`, 1 × 1,000,000 | Same | 25 ms | 12.5 ms |\n   150\t| P3 | `stream`, window 600 | Task time per update: one value per run (task time in the window divided by updates), median over runs | 0.2 ms | 0.1 ms |\n   151\t| P3 | Same | Runs with any interval later than 25 ms | Fewer runs with late intervals | No more runs with late intervals than the baseline |\n   152\t| P4 | `hover`, 1 × 100,000 | Input to the frame showing the matching readout, 95th percentile per run, median over runs | 1.7 ms | 0.8 ms |\n   153\t| P5 | `page`, 24 tiles of 340 × 200 px, fixture `trend` | Total task time, median over runs | 20 ms | 10 ms |\n   154\t| P8 | `compact`, 40 tiles of 160 × 48 px, one series each | Same | 8 ms | 4 ms |\n   155\t| P7 | `mem`, 1 × 1,000,000 | Bytes owned beyond the caller's columns | 10% of the two columns | 5% of the two columns |\n   156\t| P7 | `lifecycle` | Heap after forced collection, cycles 1,500–2,000 against 2,000–2,500 | n/a | Growth above 1% between the two windows |\n   157\t\n   158\t**For the mark renderer (decision 0002).** Primary endpoints: P1, P2 at 100,000, P3 (both metrics), P5, P8, and three workloads that could reverse the choice: `scatter` at 50,000 points (first draw), `first` at 10 × 100,000, and `zoom` (task time per step at 1 × 100,000). The scatter, ten-series and zoom endpoints have no budget; their thresholds are 10% and 5% of the slower renderer's median in the pilot, fixed before the comparison runs. Because nine endpoints are judged together, each interval is computed at 99.4% (Bonferroni), not 95%.\n   159\t\n   160\tThe rule, in order:\n   161\t1. A renderer that is worse beyond tolerance on any primary endpoint, and better beyond the smallest worthwhile improvement on none, loses.\n   162\t2. If each renderer wins somewhere, the default goes to the one that wins on more of P1, P3, P5 and P8 (the everyday cases), and the other is kept for the chart type where it wins only if its gain there is at least four times the smallest worthwhile improvement (section 5, \"complexity is paid for\").\n   163\t3. If nothing separates them, SVG stays and canvas is removed from the 1.0 bundle only if the size report shows it costs more than 0.5 KiB gzipped; otherwise both stay behind the switch.\n   164\t\n   165\tA session counts only if the machine was otherwise idle (one-minute load average under 1.0 at the start of every block) and Chrome's version did not change during it. The GPU-dependent workloads (resize, pixel ratio 2, process memory) are not endpoints here because this machine cannot measure them; they stay listed as open in the decision.\n   166\t\n   167\t## 6. Catching regressions\n   168\t\n   169\t| When | What runs | Compared with | Action |\n   170\t|---|---|---|---|\n   171\t| Every pull request | Smoke set: W-first at 3×120 and 1×100k, W-stream at 600, W-hover at 100k, bytes. Chromium on a shared CI runner. | The merge base, built and run in the same job in balanced blocks | Screening only. A result worse than the workload's tolerance triggers one automatic rerun; two in a row flag the pull request. A size-budget breach fails it. Inconclusive is reported as such. |\n   172\t| On flag, and nightly | Full workload set on the reference machine, runnable combinations from 3.2 | The merge base or previous night, and the last release, each rebuilt and run in the same session | Confirms or clears a flag. Opens an issue with the commit range for any workload outside its tolerance against the last release. |\n   173\t| Cumulative drift | Each nightly also compares with the last release | – | Small regressions that each pass per-change screening are caught when their sum crosses the tolerance. |\n   174\t| Before a release | Full set, plus Edge on Windows, Safari by hand, the phone profile when available, and all comparison libraries | Budgets P1–P7 and the previous release | A failed budget blocks the release, or the budget is changed in the spec with the measurements and a written reason. Never silently. |\n   175\t| Quarterly | Comparison library versions bumped | – | Publish the updated comparison. |\n   176\t\n   177\tResults are appended to `bench/history/` as one JSON file per run with an immutable run id, commit, lockfile hash, adapter and fixture hashes, browser versions, machine record, and ordered raw samples, with crashes, errors and timeouts recorded as distinct outcomes. A page in the documentation site charts that history with Axylo itself.\n   178\t\n   179\tPerformance tests follow the same honesty rules as functional tests: a budget is not relaxed to make a run pass, a slow workload is reported as failing until it is fixed, and a comparison Axylo loses is published as measured.\n   180\t\n   181\t## 7. Where Rust could be justified, and what it would take\n   182\t\n   183\tNo Rust experiment starts before TypeScript 1.0 has shipped and its bottlenecks have been measured.\n   184\t\n   185\t### 7.1 Candidates\n   186\t\n   187\tA Rust experiment is worth running only for a stage that is a measured bottleneck on a tracked workload and is compute-bound on numeric data. Stages that mostly call browser APIs cannot gain: each call crosses the JavaScript–WebAssembly boundary and the browser does the work either way.\n   188\t\n   189\t| Stage | Could Rust help? | Reasoning |\n   190\t|---|---|---|\n   191\t| Simplification and aggregation over typed columns (per-column extremes, binning, cumulative sums) at 1M+ points | Possibly | Tight numeric loops over contiguous memory; SIMD is available. JavaScript engines already compile such loops well. |\n   192\t| Spatial index build and query for scatter at 1M+ points | Possibly | Memory layout control matters; no browser API involved. |\n   193\t| Parsing large text inputs into columns | Unlikely | Strings must be copied into WebAssembly memory and decoded; the copy often costs as much as the parse. |\n   194\t| Layout, ticks, label fitting | No | Small inputs; dominated by text measurement, a browser call. |\n   195\t| Canvas 2D or DOM drawing | No | One boundary crossing per command. |\n   196\t| Rendering to a pixel buffer or to WebGL/WebGPU from Rust | Only as a separate product decision | It replaces the renderer, brings its own text rendering, and does nothing for the DOM-based accessibility layer. |\n   197\t\n   198\t### 7.2 Preconditions for starting\n   199\t\n   200\t1. **The arithmetic allows a win.** If the stage is a fraction *f* of the workload's critical path and Rust makes it *s* times faster, the workload improves by at most *f* × (1 − 1/*s*), before any overhead. A stage at 30% needs a threefold speedup to reach 20%; a stage at 50% needs 1.7 times. The experiment starts only when a measured *f* and a defensible *s* (from a prototype of the kernel alone) give at least 25%, leaving room for transfer and startup.\n   201\t2. **The workload matters.** It fails its budget, or it is the largest remaining wait, at a size in the workload table.\n   202\t3. **TypeScript is already tuned for that stage** (typed arrays, no allocation in the loop, pixel-bounded work), and a reviewer other than the author has confirmed it from a profile.\n   203\t4. **A written hypothesis with a number and a time ceiling.**\n   204\t\n   205\t### 7.3 Evidence required to adopt\n   206\t\n   207\tThe comparison is always end to end, on the workload, against the tuned TypeScript build, with section 3's method.\n   208\t\n   209\t| Question | Evidence required |\n   210\t|---|---|\n   211\t| Is the workload faster for the reader? | The lower bound of the 95% interval for the improvement in the workload's primary metric is at least 20%, and the absolute saving is at least the workload's declared minimum, in Chrome, Firefox and Safari or WebKit. A function-level microbenchmark is not evidence. |\n   212\t| What does startup cost? | Fetch, compile and instantiate time of the module, cold and warm, on desktop and throttled profiles, included in W-load and in the first render of the affected workload. Break-even stated for a page that draws one such chart once (cold) and for one that redraws it repeatedly (warm). |\n   213\t| What does data transfer cost? | Time and peak memory to move columns into WebAssembly memory and results back, included in the end-to-end number. If columns live in WebAssembly memory to avoid copies, the effect on `update`, `append` and P7 is measured too. |\n   214\t| Where is the crossover? | The data size above which the Rust path wins end to end. It must be at or below sizes in the workload table. |\n   215\t| Do small charts pay? | W-first at 3×120, W-page and W-load must not regress beyond tolerance, so the module loads lazily and only above the crossover. The byte, correctness and tail-latency gates of section 5 apply to the module as to any change. |\n   216\t| What happens while it loads, and when it cannot? | `line()` stays synchronous. The TypeScript path draws until the module is ready; the switch happens on a later revision and is invisible. Tested: fetch failure, instantiation blocked by a Content Security Policy without `wasm-unsafe-eval`, `update` and `destroy` during loading, and a stale result arriving after newer data. In every case the chart is correct and no error reaches the page. |\n   217\t| Does it run everywhere Axylo runs? | Script-tag build, Vite, webpack, Next.js. No reliance on threads, which need cross-origin isolation that embedded dashboards rarely have. |\n   218\t| Are both paths the same chart? | Differential tests feed identical inputs to both and compare the selected source indexes, gaps, order and ties exactly, and floating-point results within a stated tolerance; the F2 and F5 property tests run on the Rust path; rendered output is compared within a stated pixel tolerance. |\n   219\t| What does it cost to keep? | Recorded: the toolchain every contributor and CI needs; a native Windows build verified on Windows, not by cross-compiling; lazy bytes; CI minutes; lines of Rust; a named owner for rollback; and the permanent duty to maintain two implementations of one stage. |\n   220\t\n   221\t### 7.4 Decision\n   222\t\n   223\tAdopt only if every row passes. The adoption record names the workload, the crossover size and the conditions for reversal:\n   224\t\n   225\t- tuned TypeScript comes within 10% on a later browser release, checked at each quarterly comparison;\n   226\t- the differential tests cannot be kept green without disproportionate effort;\n   227\t- among charts large enough to be eligible, the fallback path is the one that usually runs.\n   228\t\n   229\tIf the experiment misses, the record is kept with its numbers, the tuned TypeScript stays, and the idea is not reopened without a new profile.\n   230\t\n   231\tA large win on the isolated stage with a small win on the workload is the expected failure. It does not show the profile was wrong: with a stage at 30% of the path, even a perfect kernel saves 30% before copies and startup take their share. The record for such a result measures where the remainder went (transfer, compilation, contention, a bottleneck that moved) before anything else is tried.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:5:This plan builds the product defined in [02-product-spec.md](02-product-spec.md). Criterion IDs (F1, L1, A2, DX4, P1 …) refer to that spec; finding IDs (R5, D2 …) refer to [01-landscape.md](01-landscape.md).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:9:## 1. Public API\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:13:### 1.1 Shape\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:35:| Plain options object, not a builder or a JSX tree | The same object works in the React adapter. Recharts needed eight components for the trend task; the information content was four fields. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:38:### 1.2 Data: one logical table, three forms\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:63:### 1.3 Fields\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:90:### 1.4 Common options\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:107:### 1.5 Handle and commit contract\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:130:### 1.6 Diagnostics\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:149:### 1.7 React adapter\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:167:## 2. Architecture\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:169:### 2.1 Pipeline\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:190:### 2.2 Invalidation\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:204:### 2.3 Rendering\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:210:M1 builds both mark renderers behind the same scene and compares them on: P1, P2, P3, P5, bytes, memory, a runtime theme toggle, forced colors, print, and browser zoom. The scene is renderer-neutral either way, so the choice is contained.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:216:### 2.4 Document structure\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:239:### 2.5 Repository\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:255:## 3. Milestones\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:259:### M0. Foundations (ceiling: 5 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:268:### M1. A vertical slice, and the two open decisions (ceiling: 12 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:274:- First measurements of P1, P2 and P5 with the accessibility layer present.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:280:- Provisional budgets P1, P2, P5 and the size targets are recalibrated from the slice and recorded.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:284:### M2. Faithful data, on the line chart (ceiling: 8 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:296:### M3. Reading the chart (ceiling: 10 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:299:- React adapter prototype, used to test the commit contract (1.5, 1.7).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:309:### M4. Accessibility, complete on the line chart (ceiling: 10 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:322:### M5. Bar, area, donut, scatter and the compact variant (ceiling: 18 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:331:- Compact variant: criteria C1–C5; a page of 40 adds no tab stops and meets P8.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:336:### M6. Updates and scale (ceiling: 10 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:344:- Budgets P1–P5 and P7 measured with the Phase 3 method, recalibrated once if M1's provisional values were wrong, then frozen with a written reason for each.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:348:### M7. Distribution and documentation (ceiling: 8 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:353:- The packed tarball installed into three clean fixtures (npm with a bundler, script tag, React): the first chart works in ten lines or fewer in each (DX1).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:355:- Size measured on the packed distribution for line-only, full, script-tag and React cases, CSS included (DX7).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:359:### M8. Release (ceiling: 10 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:370:## 4. Test strategy\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:386:## 5. Risks\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:391:| DOM text costs too much with many charts on a page. | P5 is measured in M1. A fallback to canvas-drawn tick labels would give up user text spacing and forced-colors adaptation for those labels, so it must pass the same L1 and A6 tests before it is allowed. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:393:| The million-point budget cannot be met with exact lookup and gap-preserving simplification. | The headline claim is already limited to measured workloads (spec, section 1). If P2 fails after tuning, the budget is changed in the spec with the measurements, not hidden. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:398:## 6. What waits until after 1.0\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:7:## 1. What Axylo is\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:11:It is a framework-neutral TypeScript library under the MIT license, with a thin React adapter.\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:15:### 1.1 Why someone would choose it\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:22:| Recharts | The React default | A palette, a title, labels that fit, formatted numbers and dates without code; 100,000 points without seconds of blocking; the same chart outside React |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:29:### 1.2 Who it is for\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:35:### 1.3 Principles\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:46:## 2. Scope of the first release\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:48:### 2.1 Chart types\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:65:### 2.2 Workflows\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:76:### 2.3 Supported environments\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:82:| React | 18 and 19, through `@axylo/react`. |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:86:## 3. Acceptance criteria: the reader's experience\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:90:### 3.1 Faithful representation\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:107:### 3.2 Legibility\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:120:### 3.3 Interaction\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:131:### 3.4 Accessibility\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:148:### 3.5 Diagnostic registry\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:188:### 3.6 Type matrix\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:200:### 3.7 Compact variant\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:214:### 3.8 What the evidence will and will not show\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:218:## 4. Acceptance criteria: the developer's experience\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:222:| DX1 | The first chart takes ten lines or fewer, including import, by npm, by script tag and in React. No required option besides data and fields. | Three CI tests that render the documented snippet from the packed distribution | D1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:226:| DX5 | Three methods change a chart: `update` changes options, `append` adds rows, `zoom` sets the view. They apply in call order. Validation and diagnostics are synchronous; drawing is deferred to the next frame; marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer always switch to a new state together. `destroy()` releases everything and cancels pending work. No `resize()` exists. | Sequence tests: replace then append then zoom in one turn; a rejected `strict` update leaves the last valid chart; destroy before the frame; 1,000 create–destroy cycles show no heap growth trend (P7) | D3, R16 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:228:| DX7 | Zero runtime dependencies (reconsidered only with a measured comparison). Size targets, provisional until M1 measures a working slice: 40 KiB gzipped for everything, 25 KiB for a page that uses only `line`, 2 KiB for the React adapter, CSS included. | size-limit in CI on the packed distribution | D4 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:229:| DX8 | Importing in Node without a DOM does not throw, and `validate()` runs there. Works under React StrictMode, including setup, cleanup and setup again. Works under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline` or `unsafe-eval`, served with real headers. Labels and formatter output are inserted as text, never as HTML. A target that matches no element throws an error that names the selector. | CI tests | D1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:235:## 5. Performance budgets\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:241:| P1 | `trend` (3×120), default options, reference desktop | Main-thread task time from the call until the chart is complete | Median of 20 runs | 10 ms | Not comparable across libraries below about 35 ms with the Phase 1 method |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:242:| P2 | One series, 100,000 and 1,000,000 points, typed columns | Same | Median of 20 | 60 ms and 250 ms | See 01, section 5.3: uPlot and tuned Chart.js are the fastest measured |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:243:| P3 | 600-point window, one appended point per display frame at 60 Hz, driven by an independent clock | Main-thread task time per update (per run: task time in the window divided by the number of updates); and frames later than 25 ms | Median over runs; count per 300 frames | 2 ms; 0 | See 01, section 5.3 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:244:| P4 | Pointer moved across a 100,000-point chart | Time from input to the frame showing the matching readout | 95th percentile | One frame (16.7 ms at 60 Hz) | Not measured in Phase 1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:245:| P5 | 24 `trend` charts created on one page, each tile 340 × 200 px | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 200 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:246:| P6 | Download | Minified and gzipped bytes, CSS included | Exact | DX7 | 01, section 1.4 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:247:| P8 | 40 compact charts created on one page, each 160 × 48 px with one series | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 80 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:248:| P7 | Memory | Bytes owned by Axylo beyond the caller's columns for 1,000,000 points; heap trend over 1,000 create–destroy cycles | Measured after forced collection | At most 1× the size of the caller's two columns; no upward slope | Not measured in Phase 1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:256:## 6. Decisions and the findings behind them\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:277:| React adapter in 1.0 | Recharts leads npm installs among chart-level libraries. | Kept, and built early to test the core API. Downloads show where charts are written, not that Axylo will be wanted there. |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:281:## 7. Changes this revision rejected\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:290:## 8. Decisions still open\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:296:| Canvas or SVG paths for marks? | Decide from the M1 comparison: P1, P2, P3, P5, bytes, memory, theme toggle, forced colors, print. | M1 record. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
