{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '50,110p' reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/bench.review.md && cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/bench.fix.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"16. **M · History records comparisons but does not detect regressions.** **Inspected the runner, analyzer and CI.** The runner compares adapter references served from one snapshot. The analyzer replays one record. Neither builds the merge base and candidate together, retries a flagged PR, confirms against the last release, or handles a Chrome or host change by rerunning both sides. A sequence of sub-tolerance regressions can accumulate indefinitely without a release comparison. CI invokes tests and a size report, with no paired performance job or `--check` on size. **Fix.** Run two immutable build artifacts in the same balanced job. Add the screening retry, nightly reference-machine confirmation and a last-release comparison. Rebaseline by rerunning both builds on a changed environment. [Runner build selection](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/run.mjs:102) · [CI](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/.github/workflows/ci.yml:27) · [Regression plan](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/04-plan-performance.md:139)\n\n17. **M · The report’s provenance can change without the measurements changing.** **Ran Node on the 39 listed records and the live manifest.** They share the same recorded production bundle hash but report three live HEADs, `709fcbf7`, `e6e0e195` and `b3c1a4fa`. The report displays the first HEAD and loads the build timestamp from the current `dist/manifest.json`, which already describes a different bundle. The build hashes the source file after copying it, and the server serves a shared directory a concurrent benchmark build can replace. The report generator also chooses the first matching profile from the directory without matching its artifact to the session. **Fix.** Seal a session-specific artifact directory and hash the files actually served. Record source provenance at build time and generate reports solely from pinned run records, manifests and profile IDs. [Snapshot build](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/build.mjs:36) · [Server root](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/driver.mjs:15) · [Report metadata](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/results/make-m1-report.mjs:38) · [Profile selection](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/results/make-m1-report.mjs:161)\n\n18. **m · Several test names assert more than the tests do.** **Ran the pure tests and inspected coverage.** “Widens with noise” has no quieter control. The constant-shift case cannot distinguish the requested estimator from a mean or a median of paired differences. In Node, `A=[1,101,102]`, `B=[100,1,103]` gave a difference of medians of **−1**, while the median paired difference is **+1**. The positive admission fixture builds its readback from the same `expectedView()` under test. There is no direct coverage for analyzer replay, `loadNoise` compatibility, formatted verdict consistency, stream/resize/theme summaries, memory-unavailable paths, profile mapping or actual renderer identity. **Fix.** Add those cases using independent expected values and retain the existing negative admission and lifecycle tests. [Statistics tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/test/stats.test.mjs:24) · [Positive fixture test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/test/stats.test.mjs:69) · [Browser test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/test/admission.test.mjs:85)\n\n## Checks that held\n\n- **Scheduling and isolation.** Source inspection confirmed an independent worker clock, equal scheduled AB/BA counts, and a fresh page and context for each run.\n- **Basic statistics.** Node confirmed whole-pair resampling, per-run summaries, and the stated three-way classification and minimum-pair rule on their tested inputs.\n- **History.** Source and records confirmed distinct outcomes, ordered raw stream samples, and atomic checkpoints that leave interrupted sessions identifiable.\n- **Profile accounting.** In the recorded profiles, GC samples were rooted separately, idle samples were excluded from busy shares, and unavailable production mapping was labeled.\n\n## Keep, weaken, withdraw\n\n- **Keep** the M1 raw contrasts and intervals, the inconclusive calls at 3×120, 100,000 and 1,000,000 points, and decision 0002’s retention of the Canvas switch.\n- **Weaken** “SVG by default” to a provisional choice for the tested line slice. Scope the dashboard memory result to incremental renderer PSS at DPR 2, the frame result to observed callbacks, and the uPlot timing gap to the configurations measured.\n- **Withdraw** the claims that raster explains the resize CPU gap, that simplification bounds every chart by width alone, that the SVG theme change needs no runtime work, and that production normalization and GC explain the 100,000-point gap or settle the performance budgets. The recorded heap, DOM and layout results also contradict the claim that only resize CPU favored Canvas.<!-- Author: Claude Opus 5.5 (requested effort high), Agent-tool subagent a6075efd5cba2d5e5. Saved by the coordinator from the hand-back. -->\n\n## Report: bench harness fixes for the 18 review findings\n\nEvery finding in your list (items 1–11) is fixed or partly fixed, each with tests. Both suites pass. All browser numbers below come from smoke runs on a loaded machine and are not evidence. I wrote nothing to `bench/results/*.md` or the decision documents. All edits are in `bench/**`; nothing is committed or staged.\n\nThree things you should know first:\n\n1. **The M1 dashboard comparison measured no charts.** Every SVG run in `20261118T023718Z-page-3x120-x24-axylo-canvas-vs-axylo-svg-41e7` has `svgPaths: 0`. At 230×115 tiles Axylo's plot is below the 120×80 minimum of spec L8, so it shows its text summary instead of marks. The Canvas runs presumably drew nothing either. Decision 0002 uses the DPR 2 dashboard memory result, which comes from these runs. The new drawn-geometry check rejects such runs (\"the chart drew no line strokes\"). I changed the dashboard tiles to 340×200 in 4 columns, and the 24-chart resize grid to 4 columns of 200 px; Axylo draws marks there.\n2. **The verdict rule is now 20 complete pairs** (plan 3.3: \"20 runs for full comparisons\"; I read that as 20 per configuration). It was 6. Even at 20 pairs, the coverage tool measured a false \"better\" 5.0% of the time on the reviewer's distribution (2,000 trials). This is stated in the README; I did not invent a new method to fix it.\n3. **Someone else ran the harness while I was editing it.** Two aborted records appeared at 04:50 (`bench/history/20261118T035036Z-…-956e.json`, `…035037Z…-5708.json`; old command line with `--no-build`), plus two artifacts in `bench/artifacts/`. They are not mine, so I left them. A Chrome process still running belongs to another agent's vitest browser run; I did not touch it.\n\n## Per finding\n\n| # | State | Covered by | Ran myself vs. expect |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 9 renderer | fixed | `test/axylo-adapter.test.mjs` (spy), `test/drawn.test.mjs`, browser test after a fresh production rebuild, `test/adapters/axylo-wrong-renderer.js` | Ran: both adapters always pass `__renderer`. The renderer is read from the committed chart's elements and must match, in probe and timed runs. A canvas-labelled adapter drawing SVG is rejected. |\n| 6 replay | fixed | `test/noise.test.mjs` (runs the CLI, including the reviewer's exact M1 command) | Ran: the stored analysis prints by default, and that record shows `inconclusive` as published. `--reanalyze` re-resolves the band or stops with exit 2; `--zero-only` is explicit and labelled. The band used is printed per metric. |\n| 7 failed work | fixed | `test/reliability.test.mjs` (the reviewer's three counterexamples), browser `uplot-stalls` | Ran: an unfinished stream gets outcome `incomplete`. Failures label every timing \"conditional\". More failures or more late-frame runs on the candidate block \"better\". Failure rates are reported. |\n| 4 drawn | fixed | `test/drawn.test.mjs` (literal fixtures), browser test | Ran: `missing` is required. uPlot and Chart.js report the indices they drew; bridges, points at missing values, coverage gaps and lost extremes are rejected. Axylo is checked from captured strokes at pixel resolution in the untimed probe run. Tuned Chart.js on 1×100,000 with gaps is rejected (\"185 drawn segments join retained neighbours across missing source values\"); uPlot and both Axylo renderers are admitted. Partly: Axylo gaps under 2 px and Axylo timed runs are not checked (no library hook); uPlot's sub-pixel gap clipping is invisible to its index readback. |\n| 2 lateness | fixed | `test/reliability.test.mjs` | Ran: intervals that cross a window boundary are counted (the 150 ms case counts). P3 uses exactly 300 intervals from the first due time (smoke: 300 every run). The statistic is stated in code and README. Not done: checking that a frame was actually presented (no traces). |\n| 10, 11, 12 | fixed / partly | `test/noise.test.mjs`, `test/inference.test.mjs`, `tools/coverage.mjs` | Ran: a noise band must match workload, parameters, settle, DPR, Chrome, headless/headed, host and the hashes of the files it served, with ≥20 pairs; a mismatch is an error. Fewer than 3 sessions is labelled exploratory. Both seeds are validated and recorded. The bootstrap keeps the AB/BA strata; per-order estimates are printed (reviewer's carry-over case: −6 vs −2). Warm-up is symmetric. Not done: separate browser processes, a load or session-validity gate. |\n| 17 provenance | fixed | `test/provenance.test.mjs` | Ran: each session gets its own read-only artifact. Axylo is compiled from a snapshot of the sources, with every input hashed, and HEAD and uncommitted changes recorded at build time. The server checks each file's hash as it serves it and records what it sent. `make-m1-report.mjs` now reads only the records and a pinned profile list, and needs `--out`; I regenerated it to scratch only. |\n| 3 profiles | fixed | `test/profile.test.mjs` | Ran: source maps live in the artifact; self and inclusive samples are reported separately; the settle period is a separate profile. A smoke production profile was fully mapped (M1 left 71–73% unmapped). Not done: stage spans, raster traces, a uPlot profile. |\n| 8 create | fixed | `test/provenance.test.mjs` (trend checked value for value against `research/harness/src/data.js`), adapter spy test | Ran: P1/P5 use `--fixture trend`; the default is 20 blocks; Axylo's stream draw record no longer calls `describe()`. Not done: screenshot or trace validation of readiness. |\n| 14 workloads | fixed / partly | browser test \"new workloads run end to end\" | Ran: `hover` (P4), `compact` (P8), `mem` and `lifecycle` (P7), `scatter` (10k/50k/200k, `--clustered`), `--size 10x100000`, and `zoom` through `chart.zoom()`. Axylo is **not admitted** on hover (see library needs). Not done: P6, row input, irregular x, mixed page, W-replace/keys/busy/load. |\n| 13, 15, 16, 18 | fixed / partly | browser tests (`uplot-fixed-width`, `axylo-stale-mark`), `test/summaries.test.mjs`, README | Ran: resize uses a fixed width sequence and checks geometry at the narrowest width too. uPlot readiness waits for its next draw. Theme checks every mark's colour; counters run separately. The feature matrix is in the README. `--base`/`--candidate` two-build mode refuses different harness files; `--screen --tolerance` reruns once and flags on a second failure. Not done: presentation latency for theme, nightly and last-release jobs, CI. |\n| 1, 5 | not addressed | – | Outside your list. Finding 5 needs a headed GPU rerun. |\n\n## Test runs (machine under load)\n\nPure tests:\n```\nℹ tests 67\nℹ pass 67\nℹ fail 0\n```\nBrowser tests:\n```\n✔ new workloads run end to end: hover (P4), zoom, compact (P8), scatter, mem and lifecycle (P7) (11433.858273ms)\nℹ tests 14\nℹ pass 14\nℹ fail 0\nℹ duration_ms 81944.577955\n```\n\n## Commands\n\n- Pure tests: `node --test bench/test/*.test.mjs` (builds one artifact from `packages/axylo/src`, so it can fail while the core is broken).\n- Browser tests: `node --test --test-concurrency=1 bench/test/browser/*.test.mjs`\n- Smoke comparison: `node bench/run.mjs --baseline axylo-canvas --candidate axylo-svg --workload first --size 3x120 --blocks 2 --settle 200 --history-dir <scratch dir>`\n- Coverage check: `node bench/tools/coverage.mjs`\n\n## What I need from the library\n\n- A drawn-index readback in `__bench.describe()` (the source indices the simplifier kept per series), so Axylo can be checked index-exactly, in timed runs, and for gaps under 2 px.\n- **Hover:** on a 10,000-point chart the readout showed 4 distinct data over 40 moves (indices 407, 2826, 5244, 7662), so P4 cannot admit Axylo. Please tell me whether that is a bug or intended.\n- Optionally, renderer kind in the production `__bench` hook (the DOM readback works without it).\n- At the current source, `.axylo-summary` is visible in the L8 fallback. That looks intended, but it is why the dashboard tiles drew nothing.\n\n## What I need from the plan\n\nDeclared tolerances and smallest worthwhile improvements per workload (screening requires `--tolerance` until then), a slope threshold for the P7 lifecycle gate, tile sizes for P5 and P8, and confirmation that \"20 runs\" means per configuration.\n\n## Where the spec and the plan disagree\n\nP3: the spec says \"Main-thread task time per update; Median\". The plan defines the metric as task time divided by updates, one number per run, which I implemented (median over runs). The spec could also mean the median of individual updates' costs, which TaskDuration cannot separate.\n\nOther places where the spec is silent and I chose: P8 is 40 tiles of 160×48, each showing one series of `trend` (spec AX131 suggests one series per compact chart). For P4, Chart.js starter only shows a tooltip when the pointer hits a point marker, so it is not admitted for hover.\n\nMain files: `bench/build.mjs`, `bench/run.mjs`, `bench/analyze.mjs`, `bench/src/stats.js`, `bench/src/admission.js`, `bench/README.md`. The old `bench/dist` is stale and no longer used; I left it.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,166p' docs/04-plan-performance.md && sed -n '199,255p' docs/02-product-spec.md && cat AGENTS.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Phase 3 plan: continuous performance work\n\nRevision 2, 2026-11-18. Revised after three independent reviews ([ledger](../reviews/2026-11-18/ledger.md)).\n\nThe repeated improvement work starts when the TypeScript release from [03-plan-typescript-release.md](03-plan-typescript-release.md) works end to end, and that release is its baseline. The **measurement harness is built earlier, in milestone M0**, because the release's own budgets (P1–P7 in [02-product-spec.md](02-product-spec.md)) cannot be checked without it and two design decisions in M1 depend on its numbers.\n\n## 1. What Phase 1 established, and what it did not\n\n| Observation | What it supports | What it does not support |\n|---|---|---|\n| uPlot, plain JavaScript on Canvas 2D, was the fastest library at every size in the harness. | Tuned TypeScript sets a high bar. Any other technology has to beat that, not a naive implementation. | A specific per-update cost. The first run timed only the synchronous part of each call, and uPlot defers its redraw; see the corrected figures in 01, section 5.3. |\n| Chart.js and ECharts were one to two orders of magnitude faster in their documented fast configuration than by default. | Whole configurations differ enormously; look for avoided work before low-level tuning. | Attribution to any single option. Several options changed at once, including ones that change what the reader sees. |\n| Recharts and Nivo, which create a DOM node per point by default, were the slowest. | Node count is worth watching. | That SVG is slow. A single SVG path per series was among the fastest. |\n| Two WebGL configurations ran on a software rasterizer in headless Chrome. | Nothing about WebGL. | Any conclusion about GPU paths, which need a headed browser on real hardware. |\n\nThe Phase 1 timings rank configurations by order of magnitude on one machine. They are not the baseline for this plan; the M0 harness produces that.\n\n## 2. Workloads\n\nEach workload is a page in `bench/` that takes a library adapter, so the same page runs Axylo and the comparison libraries. Every adapter is admitted only after a check that it shows the right thing: the visible x and y range, the newest and oldest timestamp, the window length, and one injected extreme value are read back and compared with the fixture. Phase 1's D3 adapter failed exactly this check after the fact.\n\n| ID | Workload | Sizes and shapes | What a reader would notice | Budget |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| W-first | Create one line chart | 3×120; 1×10k; 1×100k; 1×1M; 10×100k. Rows and typed columns measured separately. Dense, gappy (5% missing in runs) and irregular-x variants | Delay before the chart appears | P1, P2 |\n| W-page | Create a dashboard | 24 × `trend`; and a mixed page: 10 line, 6 bar, 4 area, 4 donut | Dashboard load | P5 |\n| W-stream | Append on an independent 60 Hz clock, with sequence numbers | Window 600; 10k; 100k | Stutter; the display falling behind the data | P3 |\n| W-replace | Replace all data (a filter change) | 10k; 100k; and all 24 charts at once | Delay after changing a filter | – |\n| W-hover | Move the pointer to known data points, 200 moves | 10k; 100k; 1M | Tooltip lag | P4 |\n| W-keys | Hold an arrow key across the plot; open the table | 10k; 1M | Keyboard lag; table delay | – |\n| W-zoom | Zoom in and out 60 steps, then pan | 100k; 1M | Jerky zoom | – |\n| W-resize | Animate container width 1,200 → 400 → 1,200 px over 2 s | 24 charts; 1×100k | Jank while a sidebar opens | – |\n| W-busy | The mixed page with one chart streaming, one table open, a resize in progress and a filter change arriving | – | Everything at once, as on a real dashboard | – |\n| W-types | Stacked bars 12×50 and 5×2,000; stacked area 5×10k; donut 8; scatter 10k uniform and 10k clustered | – | Delay before the chart appears | – |\n| W-load | Cold page load with the script-tag build and one chart, on a named network profile | – | Time until the first chart is visible | – |\n| W-mem | Memory, as defined in 3.1 | – | Tab memory; slow leak | P7 |\n\nWorkloads without a budget get one when their first baseline exists. Sizes are the ones named in the spec; there is no usage telemetry and none is planned, so they are revised from issue reports, with the reason recorded.\n\n## 3. How to measure\n\n### 3.1 Metrics\n\nEvery workload has one primary metric with a defined start, a defined end state that can be verified, and a statistic.\n\n| Metric | Start | End | How |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Time to first correct chart | The create call | The first presented frame in which the chart shows the fixture's data: readiness is signalled by the adapter (Axylo exposes a revision counter for this in benchmark builds), and the signal is validated on sampled runs against a screenshot compared with a reference | `performance.mark`, a `requestAnimationFrame` after readiness; screenshot sampling in separate, non-timed runs |\n| Main-thread task time | Same | A fixed settle window after readiness | Chrome DevTools Protocol `Performance.getMetrics` (task, script, layout and style durations). This counts work wherever the library schedules it: synchronous, microtask, timer or animation frame |\n| Update cost when streaming | First append | Last append displayed | Main-thread task time divided by updates; plus, per sequence number, the delay until the frame that displays it, the backlog, and the count of coalesced updates |\n| Frame pacing | – | – | Intervals between animation-frame callbacks, summarized per run: 95th and 99th percentile, and the count over 1.5 refresh intervals (25 ms at 60 Hz, 12.5 ms at 120 Hz). The refresh rate is recorded |\n| Input to display | A real pointer or key event dispatched at a known datum, timestamped | The first presented frame in which the readout shows that datum | A lightweight hook in benchmark builds, validated against Chrome traces on sampled runs. The Event Timing API is not used for this: it does not report `pointermove` or `wheel` |\n| Long stalls | – | – | Long Animation Frames entries, which report frames of 50 ms and more. Used for stalls only, not for the one-frame budgets |\n| GPU and raster time | – | – | Chrome tracing on selected headed runs |\n| Memory | – | – | Four separate numbers, never mixed: bytes in buffers Axylo owns (counted directly); JavaScript heap after forced collection (CDP); process memory including canvas backing stores (browser task manager figures via CDP where available); peak during a data replacement. Baselines: a blank page, and the page holding only the input data. Leaks: heap after 100, 300 and 1,000 create–destroy cycles following warm-up, gated on slope, plus a count of remaining listeners and observers |\n| Bytes | – | – | Minified, gzip and brotli size of each entry point from the packed distribution, CSS included; parse and compile time from a trace |\n\nWhere a browser lacks an instrument, the result file says \"not available\" for that browser. It is never filled with a number from another metric.\n\n### 3.2 Environment\n\n| Dimension | Values |\n|---|---|\n| Browsers | Chrome and Edge stable, Firefox stable, headed, with a real GPU. Safari on macOS by hand at releases. Playwright's WebKit build in CI, reported under its own name, not as Safari. |\n| Hardware profiles | \"Desktop\": the reference machine, recorded with CPU, GPU, OS, display refresh rate and power settings. \"Throttled\": 4× CPU throttling through CDP, Chromium only. \"Phone\": one real mid-range Android phone, used at baseline calibration and at releases, when the device is available; when it is not, the result says so. |\n| Display | Fixed 1,440×900 window; device pixel ratio 1 and 2. |\n| Build | Production build of every library; pinned versions; adapter and fixture hashes in the result file. |\n| Isolation | Fresh browser context per run; no extensions; machine otherwise idle. |\n| Runnable combinations | Desktop × {Chrome, Firefox, WebKit}; Throttled × Chrome; Phone × Chrome. Nothing else is claimed. |\n\nThe reference machine restarts often and has no permanent temporary storage, so every run writes its raw samples and environment record to `bench/history/` in the repository as it goes. A run cut short by a restart is kept, labelled interrupted, and never merged with a later one.\n\n### 3.3 Statistics\n\n- **Design:** baseline and candidate run in randomized, balanced blocks (AB and BA in equal numbers) inside one session, so drift and order effects fall on both.\n- **Unit of analysis:** the run. Frames inside one run share a browser, a thermal state and a history, so they are summarized per run before any interval is computed.\n- **Summary:** median of per-run values with a paired bootstrap 95% interval for the difference; tail and count metrics get their own intervals from the same per-run values.\n- **Noise:** an A-versus-A distribution, built from repeated blocks across at least three sessions, gives the noise band for each workload. A single A–A difference is not a noise floor; it can be near zero by chance.\n- **Sample sizes** are set from a pilot in M0 that measures each workload's variance and run time. Until then: 20 complete pairs for a verdict, that is 20 runs of each configuration. With fewer, the result is reported as exploratory, with no verdict. (The first harness used six pairs; a reviewer showed that six can give a confident answer in the wrong direction about one time in ten on a skewed metric.)\n- **Three outcomes:** better, worse, or inconclusive. \"No significant difference\" is reported as inconclusive, never as \"no regression\".\n- **Scope of a number:** a result holds for its commit, browser version and machine. Comparisons against stored history are valid only on the same machine and browser version; otherwise both sides are rebuilt and rerun together.\n\n### 3.4 Comparison libraries\n\n| Library | Configurations | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| uPlot | Default | Speed reference. |\n| Chart.js | Starter default; feature-equivalent tuned | The most deployed. |\n| ECharts | Starter default; feature-equivalent tuned | Canvas with built-in large-data modes. |\n| Highcharts | Default; Boost | SVG with a WebGL path; evaluation use only, subject to its license terms for publishing benchmarks. |\n| Recharts | Starter default; tuned | What most React users have. |\n| Nivo | SVG line; Canvas line | A node-per-point library with a canvas alternative. |\n| D3 | One SVG path per series, with current axes | A control for \"SVG without per-point nodes\". |\n\nFor each library, up to three configurations are published where they exist: the starter default, the least configuration that completes the same reader task, and a tuned configuration that keeps the same visible features (axes, legend, tooltip). Every option that differs from the default is listed with the documentation page that recommends it. A causal claim about one option needs a run that changes only that option. Node counts and the active renderer are recorded during the run.\n\nAxylo is compared with a comparably equipped library as well as with uPlot, since uPlot does less. Versions are pinned and bumped once a quarter in a commit of their own. Comparison numbers are published with method, versions and configuration files, including the ones Axylo loses.\n\n## 4. The improvement loop\n\nOne iteration, written up as a short record in `bench/experiments/NNNN-title.md`:\n\n1. **Pick the target.** A workload failing its budget comes first. Otherwise rank by what readers meet most: missed frame deadlines in frequent interactions (hover, streaming, resize) before a rare wait, and a 40 ms stall that happens on every dashboard before a second saved on a million-point chart.\n2. **Find the bottleneck.** Record a profile of that workload on the production build with source maps. Attribute time to stages (normalize, lookup, layout, scene, mark drawing, DOM text, raster) with `performance.measure` spans available in benchmark builds. Record each stage's share of the workload's critical path.\n3. **State a hypothesis that can fail,** with a number: \"Layout takes 41% of W-page because every tick label is measured twice; caching by font and string will cut W-page by at least 25%.\"\n4. **Build the smallest experiment** behind a flag or on a branch, with no refactoring mixed in.\n5. **Measure** candidate against baseline on the target workload and on every other tracked workload.\n6. **Decide** by section 5 and write the record, including when the hypothesis was wrong. Negative results stay in the repository.\n7. **Review.** Someone other than the author of the change checks the measurement and the record before it merges.\n\nLikely order of attack. Each is a hypothesis, not a commitment:\n\n| Order | Area | Why it is likely to matter |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Work avoided: explicit invalidation, no relayout when tick labels do not change, simplification bounded by pixels and runs | The large gaps in Phase 1 were between whole configurations that did or did not avoid work. |\n| 2 | Allocation in hot paths: typed columns end to end, reused buffers, no per-point objects | Streaming and hover run every frame. |\n| 3 | Drawing command volume: one path per series, batched state changes | uPlot's approach; cheap to try. |\n| 4 | Text: measurement cache, batched DOM reads and writes, reused label elements | 24 charts multiply every text cost. |\n| 5 | Lookup: binary search on sorted x; a grid index for scatter that degrades gracefully on clustered data | Bounds hover and keyboard cost. |\n| 6 | Partial redraw: a separate layer for cursor and focus | Removes full redraws from hover. |\n| 7 | Startup: module structure, lazy code for zoom and the table | Affects every page. |\n| 8 | Off-main-thread drawing | High integration cost; only if 1–7 leave a budget failing. |\n| 9 | WebGL or WebGPU marks | Only if raster time is the measured bottleneck at sizes in the workload table. |\n\n## 5. What makes a gain worth shipping\n\nEach tracked workload has, written down before any candidate is measured: its primary metric, the smallest improvement worth having in absolute terms, and the largest regression tolerated. All of the following must hold.\n\n| Condition | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| The gain is real and large enough | The lower bound of the 95% interval for the improvement is above the workload's smallest worthwhile improvement. Excluding zero is not enough. |\n| The gain is perceptible or structural | It moves a workload across a budget, removes missed frame deadlines (shown with an interval over runs, not one frame in one run), or saves at least the workload's declared minimum in milliseconds. A percentage alone does not qualify: 5% of 0.1 ms is nothing. |\n| Nothing else pays for it | No other tracked workload's interval shows a regression beyond its tolerance, in each browser where it can be measured. Inconclusive on a workload with a tight budget triggers a rerun, not a pass. |\n| Bytes are accounted for | The size budget still holds. A change that adds more than 0.5 KiB gzipped needs a workload crossing a budget. |\n| Correctness is unchanged | Scene snapshots identical, or every difference reviewed. All fidelity property tests pass, in particular F2 and F5. |\n| Complexity is paid for | A new code path, mode or public option needs a workload crossing a budget or a gain at least four times the smallest worthwhile improvement. Otherwise the simpler code stays. |\n\nThresholds a reader can perceive rank above percentages: one frame for interaction and animation, 100 ms for a response to input, 1 s for a chart to appear.\n\n### 5.1 Declared endpoints (provisional until the pilot)\n\nWritten on 2026-11-18, before any candidate was measured with the corrected harness, so that results cannot choose their own thresholds.\n\n**Per workload.** The primary metric is the one its budget names (spec section 5). The smallest worthwhile improvement is 10% of the budget's limit; the largest tolerated regression is 5% of it. Neither may be smaller than the upper edge of that workload's A-versus-A noise band: where the band is wider, the band is the threshold and the workload is listed as too noisy to gate at its nominal tolerance.\n\n| Budget | Workload in the harness | Primary metric | Smallest worthwhile | Tolerated regression |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| P1 | `first`, fixture `trend` | Task time, median over runs | 1 ms | 0.5 ms |\n| P2 | `first`, 1 × 100,000 | Same | 6 ms | 3 ms |\n| P2 | `first`, 1 × 1,000,000 | Same | 25 ms | 12.5 ms |\n| P3 | `stream`, window 600 | Task time per update: one value per run (task time in the window divided by updates), median over runs | 0.2 ms | 0.1 ms |\n| P3 | Same | Runs with any interval later than 25 ms | Fewer runs with late intervals | No more runs with late intervals than the baseline |\n| P4 | `hover`, 1 × 100,000 | Input to the frame showing the matching readout, 95th percentile per run, median over runs | 1.7 ms | 0.8 ms |\n| P5 | `page`, 24 tiles of 340 × 200 px, fixture `trend` | Total task time, median over runs | 20 ms | 10 ms |\n| P8 | `compact`, 40 tiles of 160 × 48 px, one series each | Same | 8 ms | 4 ms |\n| P7 | `mem`, 1 × 1,000,000 | Bytes owned beyond the caller's columns | 10% of the two columns | 5% of the two columns |\n| P7 | `lifecycle` | Heap after forced collection, cycles 1,500–2,000 against 2,000–2,500 | n/a | Growth above 1% between the two windows |\n\n**For the mark renderer (decision 0002).** Primary endpoints: P1, P2 at 100,000, P3 (both metrics), P5, P8, and three workloads that could reverse the choice: `scatter` at 50,000 points (first draw), `first` at 10 × 100,000, and `zoom` (task time per step at 1 × 100,000). The scatter, ten-series and zoom endpoints have no budget; their thresholds are 10% and 5% of the slower renderer's median in the pilot, fixed before the comparison runs. Because nine endpoints are judged together, each interval is computed at 99.4% (Bonferroni), not 95%.\n\nThe rule, in order:\n1. A renderer that is worse beyond tolerance on any primary endpoint, and better beyond the smallest worthwhile improvement on none, loses.\n2. If each renderer wins somewhere, the default goes to the one that wins on more of P1, P3, P5 and P8 (the everyday cases), and the other is kept for the chart type where it wins only if its gain there is at least four times the smallest worthwhile improvement (section 5, \"complexity is paid for\").\n3. If nothing separates them, SVG stays and canvas is removed from the 1.0 bundle only if the size report shows it costs more than 0.5 KiB gzipped; otherwise both stay behind the switch.\n\nA session counts only if the machine was otherwise idle (one-minute load average under 1.0 at the start of every block) and Chrome's version did not change during it. The GPU-dependent workloads (resize, pixel ratio 2, process memory) are not endpoints here because this machine cannot measure them; they stay listed as open in the decision.\n\n\n### 3.7 Compact variant\n\n`compact: true` on line, area and bar draws the marks and nothing else: no axes, gridlines, legend, toolbar, table control, zoom or tooltip. It is for a small tile where the page already shows the number and the label.\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by |\n|---|---|---|\n| C1 | A compact chart is a static image with a full text alternative: `role=\"img\"`, an accessible name (as in A1, including AX140 when none is given) and the generated summary as its description (series, x range, minimum, maximum, last value, omissions). It has no tab stop and no pointer-only behavior, so nothing is available to a mouse that is not available to a keyboard. | Accessibility-tree snapshot; a page of 40 has zero added tab stops |\n| C2 | The fidelity rules still hold: zero baseline for bar and area (F1), gaps (F2), diagnostics (F3), simplified drawing (F5), sorting (F11), no motion (I5), scheme from the page (L4). | The same fixtures as the full charts |\n| C3 | It fills its container at any size from 24×16 CSS px up; with no definite height it is 32 px tall. | Size tests |\n| C4 | With more than one series, lines differ by dash as well as color, since there is no legend; AX131 suggests one series per compact chart. | Two-series fixture |\n| C5 | When values were left out (F12), the description says so and a small marker with a text equivalent appears in the corner. | `dirty` fixture in compact mode |\n\nA compact chart does not give access to every value; the summary is its alternative. An author who needs every value reachable uses the full chart.\n\n### 3.8 What the evidence will and will not show\n\nAutomated checks establish structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast, text. They do not establish that a screen-reader user finds a chart understandable. The only evidence for that in 1.0 is A3's rehearsal by the developer on five reader and browser pairs, recorded as rehearsal. Axylo is built by one person with no access to study participants, so no claim of usability testing with disabled users is made, and the conformance table says so in those words. Reviewers proposed participant testing as a release gate; it is recorded here as a known limit instead.\n\n## 4. Acceptance criteria: the developer's experience\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| DX1 | The first chart takes ten lines or fewer, including import, by npm, by script tag and in React. No required option besides data and fields. | Three CI tests that render the documented snippet from the packed distribution | D1 |\n| DX2 | One logical table, three ways to pass it: long rows, wide rows, typed columns. The three forms of each fixture produce the same normalized data and the same diagnostics, with row indexes in the caller's own form. | Equivalence tests | D2 |\n| DX3 | With typed rows, a misspelled field, a text field on a value axis, wide `y` combined with `series`, and an option that does not apply to the chart type all fail to compile. At run time, for untyped rows, the same mistakes raise AX101, AX106 and AX150. | [docs/api/examples.ts](api/examples.ts) compiles and each marked mistake fails (already checked with TypeScript 7.0.2); runtime tests per code | D5 |\n| DX4 | Diagnostics follow the registry in section 3.5: stable code, chart name, field and rows where they apply, the fix, and a documentation page per code. | Message snapshots; link checker | R5, D5 |\n| DX5 | Three methods change a chart: `update` changes options, `append` adds rows, `zoom` sets the view. They apply in call order. Validation and diagnostics are synchronous; drawing is deferred to the next frame; marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer always switch to a new state together. `destroy()` releases everything and cancels pending work. No `resize()` exists. | Sequence tests: replace then append then zoom in one turn; a rejected `strict` update leaves the last valid chart; destroy before the frame; 1,000 create–destroy cycles show no heap growth trend (P7) | D3, R16 |\n| DX6 | Colors, font and spacing are CSS custom properties with documented names; the chart inherits `font-family` and `color`. `colors` pins a color to a series name so the same series has the same color on every chart. There is no theme object and no positional palette option. | Theming tests, including a 24-tile page where series subsets differ | R10 |\n| DX7 | Zero runtime dependencies (reconsidered only with a measured comparison). Size targets, provisional until M1 measures a working slice: 40 KiB gzipped for everything, 25 KiB for a page that uses only `line`, 2 KiB for the React adapter, CSS included. | size-limit in CI on the packed distribution | D4 |\n| DX8 | Importing in Node without a DOM does not throw, and `validate()` runs there. Works under React StrictMode, including setup, cleanup and setup again. Works under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline` or `unsafe-eval`, served with real headers. Labels and formatter output are inserted as text, never as HTML. A target that matches no element throws an error that names the selector. | CI tests | D1 |\n| DX9 | Semantic versioning. A deprecated API keeps working for at least one minor release and six months. At most one major version a year. The previous release's documented examples still compile and run against each release candidate. | Release policy; API report diff; upgrade test | D6 |\n| DX10 | Every runnable example in the documentation is compiled and rendered in CI from the packed distribution. | Docs build | D1 |\n| DX11 | MIT license for everything, including zoom and accessibility. Third-party notices are verified at release. | Repository; release check | D7 |\n| DX12 | A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence. | Scripted rehearsal at each release | D1, 1.2 |\n\n## 5. Performance budgets\n\nThese numbers are provisional. They are design targets taken from Phase 1's order-of-magnitude measurements, and they become release gates only after the benchmark harness built in M0 has measured a working Axylo slice in M1 and the limits have been recalibrated and frozen (plan, M1 and M6). Each budget names its workload, metric and statistic so that two people measuring the same build get the same verdict.\n\n| ID | Workload | Metric | Statistic | Provisional limit | Phase 1 reference |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| P1 | `trend` (3×120), default options, reference desktop | Main-thread task time from the call until the chart is complete | Median of 20 runs | 10 ms | Not comparable across libraries below about 35 ms with the Phase 1 method |\n| P2 | One series, 100,000 and 1,000,000 points, typed columns | Same | Median of 20 | 60 ms and 250 ms | See 01, section 5.3: uPlot and tuned Chart.js are the fastest measured |\n| P3 | 600-point window, one appended point per display frame at 60 Hz, driven by an independent clock | Main-thread task time per update (per run: task time in the window divided by the number of updates); and frames later than 25 ms | Median over runs; count per 300 frames | 2 ms; 0 | See 01, section 5.3 |\n| P4 | Pointer moved across a 100,000-point chart | Time from input to the frame showing the matching readout | 95th percentile | One frame (16.7 ms at 60 Hz) | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n| P5 | 24 `trend` charts created on one page, each tile 340 × 200 px | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 200 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n| P6 | Download | Minified and gzipped bytes, CSS included | Exact | DX7 | 01, section 1.4 |\n| P8 | 40 compact charts created on one page, each 160 × 48 px with one series | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 80 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n| P7 | Memory | Bytes owned by Axylo beyond the caller's columns for 1,000,000 points; heap trend over 1,000 create–destroy cycles | Measured after forced collection | At most 1× the size of the caller's two columns; no upward slope | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n\nThree rules hold whatever the numbers become:\n\n1. Budgets apply to default options. Finding D8 showed libraries that are fast only for authors who know which options to set.\n2. Budgets are absolute, on named hardware profiles. Comparison libraries, uPlot above all, are measured in the same runs and published beside Axylo, but a ratio to another library is not a gate: it would reward leaving out work that library does not do.\n3. The accessibility layer is on in every measured configuration.\n\n# Working in this repository\n\nAxylo is a browser charting library. The product is specified in `docs/02-product-spec.md`; the build plan is `docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md`. Read the sections your task names before writing code.\n\n## Rules for every change\n\n- **Contracts are fixed.** `packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts` and `packages/axylo/src/public.ts` are the interfaces between modules. Do not edit them. If your work needs a change there, stop that part, finish what you can, and describe the exact change you need in your report.\n- **Stay in your files.** Each task lists the directories it owns. Do not create, edit or delete anything else, including configuration and other modules. Several agents work in this checkout at the same time.\n- **No runtime dependencies.** Use the platform (`Intl`, typed arrays). Dev dependencies already installed: TypeScript, Vitest, fast-check, esbuild, Playwright (system Chrome), axe-core. The network is not available; do not try to install anything.\n- **No DOM at import time.** A module that needs the DOM takes it as a parameter or touches it inside a function. Pure stages (data, scales, formatting, layout, scene) must run in Node.\n- **Hot paths use typed arrays.** No object per data point, no array of arrays per data point, no copying of large columns that the contracts say are borrowed.\n- **Never guess silently.** Anything that cannot be drawn as given is reported through the `DiagnosticSink` with the code from spec section 3.5.\n- **Tests come from the spec.** Expected values are derived from the specification and the fixture data, never by running the implementation and pasting its output. Use fast-check property tests for invariants (\"for any data …\"). A test you cannot make pass is reported as failing; do not weaken it or change its expected value.\n- **TypeScript strict, no `any`** in exported signatures. `__DEV__` is a build-time boolean (true in tests).\n- **Comments say why**, not what. Names are plain words.\n- **Do not commit.** The coordinator commits.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\nnpm run typecheck      # tsc over the library, its tests, and the API examples in docs/api\nnpm test               # unit and property tests (Node)\nnpm run build          # packages/axylo/dist\nnpm run test:browser   # browser tests against the built bundle, in system Chrome\n```\n\n## Your report\n\nEnd with a short report: what you built (files), what you verified and how (paste the last lines of the test run), what is not done or not verified, any contract change you need, and any place where the spec was ambiguous and what you assumed. Keep \"ran and saw\" apart from \"expect\".\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
