{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/palette.review.md reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/palette.fix.report.md'","aggregated_output":"<!-- Reviewer: Codex gpt-6.2-astra, effort xhigh (observed in the session rollout, thread 01a1ecda-c27e-7eb2-ac8e-cf8cea9b67d2), read-only, cross-provider. Reviewed commit 51e1be5. -->\n\n**All 16 shipped colours pass the numerical A5 palette requirement on their respective default backgrounds.** The lowest contrast is **3.041417:1**. The smallest full-precision OKLab distance is **0.100387**. I found no error larger than rounding in the author’s opaque-colour arithmetic or in the measured tables.\n\n**The chart does not meet A5 as a whole at `51e1be5`.** Area markers disappear from the legend and tooltip. I also reproduced a rendered fill below the decision’s 0.06 threshold and found a two-scheme counterexample to the claimed necessity of the lightness spread.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. **B · Eight area markers become one shape in the legend and tooltip. Ran.** I passed eight distinct marker cues through the committed `seriesSwatch` and `createSwatch` functions. All eight area swatches emitted the identical square path `M5 1H15V11H5Z`. Both the legend and tooltip use this path. A5 explicitly requires the area’s shape marker to be repeated in both places. The palette tests still pass. Draw the series marker on the area chip and verify its identity against the plotted marker in an eight-series chart. The same integration check should exercise contrast-mode dashes, applicable bar patterns, 1 px background separators, and the grayscale crossing-line fixture A5 names. [Area swatch selection](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:111) · [Square path](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/swatch.ts:93) · [A5](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:141)\n\n2. **M · The 25% fill guarantee fails on an 8-bit raster. Ran.** For light slots 4–5, ideal source-over on white gives **0.060916**. Drawing the same fills in an in-memory Skia canvas produced `#d7c8e0` and `#eed7d4`, **0.059002** apart. The test uses the correct encoded-sRGB blend for an ideal isolated fill. Its precision exceeds that of the raster it describes. The background also matters when one translucent area covers another. For scale, all eight isolated light fills at 25% have only **1.275–1.596:1** contrast against white. An outline can carry the graphical information there. Keep the analytic test, add pixel checks for the renderer and backing colours the decision promises, and allow enough margin for those checks. The 0.06 limit belongs to decision 0003. [Fill test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/theme/palette.test.ts:136) · [Decision table](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:47)\n\n3. **M · Three pairs fall below 0.10 in the committed simulated preview pixels. Ran.** I sampled the interiors of the eight donut slices in both PNGs. The normal rows reproduce all 16 shipped hex values exactly. After the preview’s simulation and 8-bit output, light protan slots 6–8 measure **0.098343**, dark tritan slots 2–5 measure **0.099183**, and dark tritan slots 1–8 measure **0.099631**. All sampled channels agree with independently simulating and then rounding to the nearest byte. The author’s full-precision simulation of the original mark colours is correct. A5 asks for rendered marks to be scored and leaves the simulation’s output precision unspecified. Define the acceptance pipeline explicitly. A claim that the simulated screenshots themselves clear 0.10 needs more margin. [Decision’s preview claim](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:28) · [Scoring pipeline](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/research/palette/color.mjs:65)\n\n4. **M · Neither the three deep light colours nor the medium-dark first blue is forced by the six constraints. Ran.** The counterexample below raises the lowest light-scheme OKLab L from **0.39111 to 0.48158**, narrows its range from **0.28951 to 0.20679**, and raises dark slot 1 from **L 0.54972, 3.64:1** to **L 0.81504, 10.28:1**. It keeps all three tokens, both backgrounds, full-severity simulation, and all six thresholds. Its magenta is more saturated, which is a genuine design trade-off. Correct the necessity claim at line 146. The explanation that the simulations simply flatten OKLab `a` or `b` also overstates the model. For the shipped light blue, simulated L ranges from **0.6430 to 0.6913** around its original **0.6590**. The projections act in linear RGB. [Geometric argument](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:107) · [Claim about relaxing both limits](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:146)\n\n5. **M · The family-order explorer can discard a feasible order after a timeout. Reasoned from code; ran the final and what-if grid checks.** The first-four precheck caches `!!search(..., 2, four).best`. A two-second timeout with no incumbent becomes `false` and skips every later permutation with that same first-four set. The order loop also prints only successes. Preserve three outcomes, and prune only after a completed infeasibility proof. The underlying branch-and-bound lower bound and forward checking are sound for its sorted candidate domains. I reproduced both shipped solutions to completion, in **64 light and 35,088 dark search nodes**. That proves each fixed search. The final light boxes were centred on an earlier answer, the dark search fixes that one light solution, and the fitter imposes **3.02:1** and **28°** where the tests allow **3:1** and **30°**. [Explorer precheck](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/research/palette/explore.mjs:59) · [Search bounds](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/research/palette/fit.mjs:71) · [Final config provenance](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/research/palette/configs/fit-final.json:2)\n\n6. **M · The stroke statistic is reproducible, but “always fully covered” and “what the eye averages” exceed the evidence. Ran and reasoned.** My independent CPU rasterization reproduced the reported Canvas stroke rows. At DPR 1, **one of 576 sampled columns** lacked a fully covered pixel for each colour. Light orange’s least-covered core reached **96.62%**, giving **2.924:1**. Its core p10 and median both round to **3.04:1**, just as published. The weighted statistic uses `Σcoverage² / Σcoverage`, drops coverage at or below 0.02, and omits untouched background. It describes this path and sampling rule. WCAG 1.4.11 evaluates the contrast of the graphical information needed to understand the content against adjacent colours and permits assessing the specified stroke colour despite antialiasing. Neither WCAG nor A5 defines a coverage-weighted average as the 3:1 test. A5 still calls for rendered-mark checks, and L2 calls for computed-style and rendered-pixel checks. Report the percentile, qualify the perceptual interpretation, and retain the thin-line result as a legibility measurement. [Stroke calculation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/research/palette/strokes.mjs:59) · [Decision conclusion](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:100) · [WCAG guidance](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/non-text-contrast.html)\n\n7. **m · The record cross-check permits materially false entries. Ran.** In memory I changed the recorded light protan closest pair to **1–1**, dark blue’s OKLCH to **[0, 0, 0]**, its recorded weighted stroke median to **99**, and light blue’s contrast from **3.12 to 3.16**. All **23 palette-file tests passed**. Pair identities, OKLCH and stroke records go unchecked, and the contrast assertion allows nearly 0.05 error for a two-decimal record. `score.mjs` also imports an existing stroke record without checking its colours or backgrounds. Compare fields at their published precision, validate the stroke inputs before copying them, and narrow the README’s cross-check claim to the fields actually verified. [Record assertions](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/theme/palette.test.ts:145) · [Stroke record import](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/research/palette/score.mjs:35)\n\n8. **m · A binding minimum does not imply that almost any hand edit breaks a constraint. Ran.** I changed one channel by one byte in every valid direction, **95 edits** in total. **36/48 light** and **32/47 dark** edits still met all six numerical constraints, including the muted token. A stale record catches a changed hex independently of those constraints. Report the tight pairs and their margins. The measurements support those statements directly. [Decision’s maintenance claim](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:79)\n\n9. **m · The palette-history section mixes measured defaults, authored examples and recollection. Ran and checked the landscape.** In the installed `d3-scale-chromatic` 3.1.1 Tableau 10, **5/10** colours fall below 3:1 on white. Red is slot **3**, green is slot **5**, and teal separates them. The red–green deutan distance really is **0.006640**. Observable 10’s yellow, pastel blue and pink measure **1.91, 1.95 and 2.19:1**. R7 has eight displayed six-slice rows. Recharts’ colours are authored example colours, D3’s chart is authored, uPlot has no default palette, and ECharts was read from options. The seven displayed library palette rows have respectively **5, 4, 4, 0, 1, 6 and 4** colours below 3:1 for Chart.js, ECharts, Highcharts, ApexCharts, Plotly, Nivo and MUI X. ApexCharts repeats slot 1 at slot 6. Cite the versioned arrays for Tableau and Observable. Identify a specific Carbon, Okabe–Ito or Tol array before claiming its measured extrema or failures. [Decision’s comparisons](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:155) · [Landscape R7](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/01-landscape.md:220)\n\n## Independent arithmetic\n\nI implemented the reference calculation in Node using **linear sRGB → D65 XYZ → LMS → cube roots → OKLab**. This gives an independent route around the author’s combined linear-sRGB-to-LMS matrix. I derived the last transform by inverting the OKLab-to-cube-root-LMS matrix. OKLCH uses `hypot(a,b)` and `atan2(b,a)`. The difference is the ordinary Euclidean norm in OKLab, with white at **L = 1** and **no factor of 100**.\n\nFor a normalized sRGB channel \\(u\\), I used \\(u/12.92\\) through 0.04045 and \\(((u+0.055)/1.055)^{2.4}\\) above it. Encoding uses \\(12.92x\\) through 0.0031308 and \\(1.055x^{1/2.4}-0.055\\) above it. WCAG luminance uses linear-channel weights **0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722**. Contrast is \\((Y_\\mathrm{lighter}+0.05)/(Y_\\mathrm{darker}+0.05)\\).\n\nI am confident of those transfer, luminance, contrast, polar-coordinate and distance constants from memory. I know the OKLab transform structure and Machado model, but cannot vouch from memory for every decimal in their matrices. I independently checked **all 27 Machado endpoint coefficients** against the three 11-level tables in the locally installed Plotly 4.3.0 bundle. Every coefficient matched. The rows I applied to **linear** RGB at severity **1.0**, with the result clipped to `[0,1]` before OKLab, were:\n\n```text\nprotan   .152286  1.052583 −.204868    .114503  .786281  .099216   −.003882 −.048116 1.051998\ndeutan   .367322   .860646 −.227968    .280085  .672501  .047413   −.011820  .042940  .968881\ntritan  1.255528  −.076749 −.178779   −.078411  .930809  .147602    .004733  .691367  .303900\n```\n\nI compared all **120 unordered pairs of the 16 colours under all four visions**, including the **224 within-scheme comparisons** that determine A5. Maximum absolute discrepancy against `color.mjs` was **7.92×10⁻⁹** in distance, **3.38×10⁻⁸** in an OKLab coordinate, **0.000019°** in hue, and **zero** in contrast. The inverse transform recovered each shipped linear channel within **2.75×10⁻¹⁰**. Neither implementation has a material numerical error here.\n\nA5 names normal vision and the three **-opias**. Severity 1.0 is an appropriate interpretation, consistent with R7. A5 specifies no particular model and no sweep of anomalous-trichromacy severities. Reducing severity would change the question and can even reduce separation. At tritan severity **0.9**, dark slots 3–6 measure **0.099563**, versus **0.100668** at 1.0.\n\n## The six constraints and their mutation tests\n\nI executed the committed test bodies with Vitest’s `expect`, changing inputs only in memory. Each mutation below failed the named test.\n\n| Test | Requirement and independent result, light / dark | Mutation caught |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | A5 opaque contrast on `#ffffff` / `#16181d`, minimum **3.041417 / 3.643024** against 3 | Set light slot 1 to white. Contrast **1:1**. |\n| 2 | A5 minimum **0.10** over all 28 pairs in each scheme, including repeats, under each of four visions | Set light slot 6 to `#027cad`. Normal still gives **0.115811**. Tritan slots 1–6 give **0.099947**, which would print as 0.100. |\n| 3 | Decision-only **0.14** for the first four, measured **0.140598 / 0.145611**. The 0.10 checks for prefixes 3, 5 and 6 follow from test 2. | Swap light slots 3 and 5. The full set is identical. The new first four contain the orange–red tritan pair at **0.102505**. |\n| 4 | Decision-only maximum **30°** shortest hue arc, measured **18.4915°** for slate and **7.8359°** for the chromatic slots | Swap dark slots 1 and 2. Slot 1 shifts **160.15°**. A separate **358.859° / 1.256°** case correctly passed at **2.397°**. |\n| 5 | Decision-only normal-vision **0.10** from focus, reference and muted. Minima **0.108078 / 0.100768**, **0.148372 / 0.105580**, **0.105871 / 0.100945** | In three runs, set light slot 6 equal to each token. Each failed at **zero**. |\n| 6 | Decision-only **0.06** for seven consecutive normal-vision pairs over the scheme background. Ideal minima at 25% **0.060916 / 0.060399**, at 80% **0.193912 / 0.173528** | Reorder both schemes to **4,2,3,1,6,5,7,8**. Light slots originally numbered 1 and 6 become neighbours at **0.033**. Among the six numerical constraints, only test 6 fails. |\n\nThe test thresholds for **1 and 2** come from A5. The extra limits are explicit fitter inputs in decision 0003. The commit establishes that the palettes were fitted to those inputs. It does not establish when the extra limits were chosen. Ring closure and deficiency-simulated fills are recorded separately. I reproduced the three sub-0.06 normal-vision 25% closures and the documented **0.023–0.046** range for 25% consecutive neighbours under deficiencies.\n\nEvery prefix row in the decision record reproduced at its printed precision, including the closest pair identities. Values below are in **normal / protan / deutan / tritan** order.\n\n| Slots | Light | Dark |\n|---|---|---|\n| First 3 | .316 / .169 / .281 / .254 | .258 / .236 / .187 / .283 |\n| First 4 | .299 / .169 / .201 / .141 | .230 / .146 / .187 / .152 |\n| First 5 | .116 / .102 / .119 / .103 | .114 / .122 / .107 / .101 |\n| First 6 | .116 / .102 / .118 / .103 | .114 / .122 / .102 / .101 |\n| All 8 | .116 / .100 / .101 / .103 | .114 / .101 / .101 / .101 |\n\nI also triggered the distinct-count test with a duplicate, the OKLab reference test with a changed matrix coefficient, the record test with a one-byte hex edit, and the L2 token test by setting the axis to the background. The four stylesheet tests each failed when I respectively removed a series declaration, set `color-scheme` on the root, removed the structure marker, or inserted an unmatched brace. The previous palette failed **six numerical test cases**, plus the two expected stale-record checks.\n\n## Feasibility counterexample\n\nThese two palettes work **together**, with the shipped backgrounds and tokens.\n\n```text\nLight  #3791ff #d28204 #2b6d48 #881bbd #c70f09 #1f7fae #fe1ce0 #959396\nDark   #63d2fc #f9c309 #78aa6f #bf37be #e42129 #4574fa #ee5ec3 #7e7c80\n```\n\n| Independently measured | Light | Dark |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Lowest contrast | 3.028060 | 3.809938 |\n| Minimum Δ across all pairs and visions | 0.102001 | 0.100212 |\n| First-four minimum across visions | 0.148624 | 0.142296 |\n| Largest cross-scheme hue shift | 29.965134° | 29.965134° |\n| Nearest focus / reference / muted | .107341 / .119952 / .100781 | .113824 / .107331 / .113049 |\n| Ideal neighbour Δ at 25% / 80% | .062831 / .207713 | .067636 / .172076 |\n| OKLab L range | .48158–.68837 | .58920–.84182 |\n\nThe counterexample also cleared the two raster checks discussed above. Its rendered 25% fill minima were **0.061675 / 0.066833**, and its smallest distances after rounding the simulated colours to bytes were **0.101856 / 0.100043**.\n\nI independently enumerated the published `whatif-no-deep-light.json` domains with a bitset search. Their sizes were **430, 907, 467, 1,130, 1,668, 498, 1,331 and 454**. I found no solution on that grid at **0.10/0.14**, **0.09/0.14**, **0.09/0.12**, or even **0.08/0.12**. The counterexample uses colours outside those hand-selected boxes. This reconciles the author’s unsuccessful runs with the existence of a palette meeting the original limits. The fitter’s usual steps are **0.01 L, 0.01 C, 2° hue**. The family explorer uses **3°** hue and considers **99** eligible family sets. I did not reproduce the historical timed **34/12/53** split.\n\n## Verification and colour verdicts\n\nNo files were created, edited or deleted. No contract change is needed. I ran the theme test bodies and raster calculations in memory. I did not run a fresh browser session, the browser suite, or the TypeScript build. The stroke check used an independent CPU Skia rasterization of the seeded geometry. The preview-pixel check read the committed Chrome PNGs. The last lines of the in-memory test run were:\n\n```text\npalette.test.ts 23 passed, 0 failed\ncolor.test.ts 10 passed\nreview.test.ts 4 passed\nTheme test bodies: 37 passed, 0 failed\n```\n\n**Sound — colour arithmetic.** The sRGB transfer, WCAG luminance and ratio, OKLab/OKLCH, Euclidean scale, and full-severity Machado matrices reproduce the published figures.  \n**Sound — opaque palette measurements.** Both backgrounds, all 16 series rows, four visions, all prefix minima, closest pairs, token distances and hue shifts check out.  \n**Sound — analytic fill measurements.** The published tables correctly describe unquantized encoded-sRGB source-over at both opacities.  \n**Sound — fixed-grid search.** Both final searches complete and reproduce the shipped arrays within their stated domains.  \n**Sound — stroke table.** The reported Canvas percentiles reproduce at their printed precision. The interpretation limits are in finding 6.\n\nEach **yes** below answers the A5 **colour** question for the opaque mark on its scheme background, with full-precision simulation applied to that mark. “Nearest” is the worst distance to any other slot over all four visions. The last two numeric columns give the **weighted stroke p10 / median** and reproduce every colour behind the decision’s grouped stroke rows.\n\n| Scheme · slot | Colour | OKLCH | Contrast | Nearest Δ · other slot, vision | DPR 1 | DPR 2 | A5 colour |\n|---|---|---|---:|---|---|---|---|\n| Light 1 | `#4395ea` | .659 .150 251.8° | 3.1223 | .104942 · 6 tritan | 2.48 / 2.57 | 2.75 / 2.84 | Yes |\n| Light 2 | `#e77412` | .681 .170 52.1° | 3.0414 | .102505 · 5 tritan | 2.47 / 2.55 | 2.73 / 2.79 | Yes |\n| Light 3 | `#085e01` | .419 .140 142.0° | 8.0403 | .101807 · 5 protan | 5.14 / 5.54 | 6.34 / 6.64 | Yes |\n| Light 4 | `#5f2083` | .391 .159 310.0° | 10.3913 | .102990 · 7 protan | 6.33 / 6.89 | 8.08 / 8.47 | Yes |\n| Light 5 | `#ba5f50` | .590 .120 30.7° | 4.3384 | .100643 · 8 deutan | 3.18 / 3.35 | 3.67 / 3.80 | Yes |\n| Light 6 | `#087ca6` | .551 .110 231.4° | 4.7255 | .100387 · 8 protan | 3.42 / 3.60 | 3.99 / 4.15 | Yes |\n| Light 7 | `#a12c63` | .490 .160 356.1° | 6.8721 | .102990 · 4 protan | 4.67 / 5.00 | 5.66 / 5.90 | Yes |\n| Light 8 | `#8f9299` | .660 .011 267.3° | 3.1160 | .100387 · 6 protan | 2.43 / 2.52 | 2.72 / 2.79 | Yes |\n| Dark 1 | `#1c73c5` | .550 .150 251.9° | 3.6430 | .100572 · 8 tritan | 2.82 / 2.95 | 3.19 / 3.29 | Yes |\n| Dark 2 | `#d77939` | .669 .140 52.0° | 5.6519 | .100873 · 5 tritan | 4.13 / 4.36 | 4.80 / 4.97 | Yes |\n| Dark 3 | `#b0e391` | .861 .121 134.3° | 12.0443 | .100668 · 6 tritan | 8.29 / 8.84 | 9.96 / 10.36 | Yes |\n| Dark 4 | `#bf93f1` | .740 .139 304.8° | 7.3225 | .101483 · 7 protan | 5.24 / 5.51 | 6.17 / 6.36 | Yes |\n| Dark 5 | `#bc5145` | .570 .141 28.7° | 3.7219 | .100713 · 8 deutan | 2.84 / 2.99 | 3.23 / 3.33 | Yes |\n| Dark 6 | `#71daff` | .839 .110 223.6° | 11.1413 | .100668 · 3 tritan | 7.66 / 8.17 | 9.24 / 9.62 | Yes |\n| Dark 7 | `#fa93b5` | .780 .129 359.3° | 8.3767 | .101483 · 4 protan | 5.91 / 6.29 | 7.00 / 7.26 | Yes |\n| Dark 8 | `#82828f` | .611 .019 285.8° | 4.6840 | .100572 · 1 tritan | 3.52 / 3.69 | 4.02 / 4.18 | Yes |<!-- Author: Claude Opus 5.5 (requested effort xhigh), Agent-tool subagent abc842fd0288bc675, resumed for the refit. Saved by the coordinator from the hand-back. -->\n\nThe refit only partly meets your brief. The dark scheme's first blue is now an ordinary dark-mode blue (L 0.73, 7.46:1), and every limit now holds on byte-rounded values too. The light scheme is barely more even than before: slot 3's green (L 0.43) is still clearly the heaviest of the first three lines. Nothing is committed.\n\n**New arrays** (slot order unchanged: blue, orange, green, purple, red, cyan, magenta, slate):\n- Light: `#4a94eb #e97200 #136102 #7314a2 #bd585e #007baa #a52265 #9293a5`\n- Dark: `#64acfa #de7b2e #b2e390 #9149b9 #c3513d #71d8ff #f898b5 #7a7f93`\n\n**One token changed:** the dark focus goes from `#79a8ff` to a near-white `#e3e8f0` (14.4:1 on #16181d). The old focus sat exactly where a dark-mode series blue belongs. Keeping it, the best dark blue the exact search found was `#3970c2`, L 0.55 (it timed out, so that is not a proof). The light focus `#1f5fcc` is unchanged, so `keyboard.browser.test.ts` still matches. Any browser test that checks the dark focus colour would now see `#e3e8f0`.\n\n**Lightness, before (51e1be5) and after:**\n\n| | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| Light L range | 0.39–0.68 | 0.43–0.68 |\n| Light, spread of first three | 0.26 | 0.25 |\n| Light, largest chroma | 0.17 | 0.205 |\n| Dark first blue | L 0.55, 3.64:1 | L 0.73, 7.46:1 |\n| Largest light/dark hue shift | 18.5° | 13.6° |\n\n**Measured table (light / dark).** \"Analytic\" is on unrounded values; \"bytes\" is after rounding the simulated colours to 8-bit values.\n\n| Check | Light | Dark | Limit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Lowest contrast | 3.03 | 3.26 | 3 |\n| 2. Pairwise, all 8 (normal / protan / deutan / tritan), analytic | 0.124 / 0.103 / 0.103 / 0.102 | 0.116 / 0.104 / 0.103 / 0.102 | 0.10 |\n| 2. Same, bytes | 0.124 / 0.103 / 0.103 / 0.102 | 0.116 / 0.103 / 0.102 / 0.102 | 0.10 |\n| 3. First four, worst vision (analytic / bytes) | 0.156 / 0.155 | 0.158 / 0.159 | 0.14 |\n| 3. First 3 / 5 / 6, worst vision | 0.156 / 0.102 / 0.102 | 0.158 / 0.103 / 0.103 | 0.10 |\n| 4. Hue shift | 13.6° | same pairs | 30° |\n| 5. Nearest to focus / reference / muted | 0.101 / 0.152 / 0.110 | 0.138 / 0.104 / 0.113 | 0.10 |\n| 6. 25% fills: ideal / both 8-bit models / Chrome canvas / Chrome SVG | 0.064 / 0.063 / 0.063 / 0.063 | 0.064 / 0.064 / 0.063 / 0.063 | 0.06 |\n| 6. 80% fills | 0.201 / 0.202 / 0.202 | 0.179 / 0.179 / 0.179 | 0.06 |\n\nThe decision record lists the full prefix tables (analytic and bytes) with the closest pair for each, plus the tight pairs and their margins. The one-byte hand-edit check, which replaces \"almost any hand edit fails\": 41 of 46 light edits and 46 of 47 dark edits still meet every check.\n\n**Margins (finding 2).** The solver kept pairwise ≥0.102, fills ≥0.063, contrast ≥3.02 and hue shift ≤15°; the tests use the real limits. One stricter margin is recorded but not required: the 25% fill worst case when each channel of both fills is allowed to be off by one byte. It comes out at 0.056 in both schemes, so it binds and misses 0.06 by 0.004. No palette meeting it was found together with the other goals.\n\n**What I tried that failed:**\n- Exact search over wide boxes (light floor 0.44–0.48, chroma ≤0.18–0.22): nothing within 300 s, which is a timeout, not a proof.\n- Annealing from random starts: feasible light sets only with three lightness tiers; nothing with every colour at L ≥0.46.\n- A lighter, calmer slot-3 green (L ≥0.46, chroma ≤0.11): green collides with red under protanopia, and magenta with cyan and slate. The review's palette gets that green by putting magenta at chroma 0.30.\n- Walking from the review's palette toward lower chroma: magenta stayed at chroma 0.28 or more.\n- Red third (Tableau-style) and magenta fourth: nothing usable.\n- Dark searches with the old focus token: no first blue above L 0.55.\nEach of these is recorded as \"not found\", not proved impossible, unless the search completed.\n\n**Previews:** same file names, `research/palette/preview-light.png` and `preview-dark.png` (about 295 KB each).\n\n**Last lines of the theme test run:**\n```\n Test Files  3 passed (3)\n      Tests  46 passed (46)\n```\nThe new byte-rounded and Chrome-pixel tests fail on the old palette, reproducing the review's findings 2 and 3. `npx tsc -p packages/axylo --noEmit` reports one error, in `test/browser/acceptance/a1-names.browser.test.ts`, which is another agent's file; none are in the theme files.\n\n**Other requested fixes:**\n- **Explorer (finding 5):** every order now gets one of three outcomes (feasible, proved infeasible, timed out), all three are printed, and only a completed proof prunes.\n- **Record checks (findings 7, 8):**\n  - The test checks the recorded fields at their written precision, including which pair is closest.\n  - It validates the stroke record, which must name exactly the shipped colours and backgrounds.\n  - `score.mjs` refuses stroke or fill records that do not match.\n  - The \"almost any hand edit fails\" claim is replaced with the tight pairs and margins.\n- **Decision record (findings 4, 6, 9):**\n  - The geometry argument and the necessity claim are corrected.\n  - The thin-line wording is qualified: the core pixel is a 10th percentile, the weighted value is a legibility measure and not the WCAG test.\n  - Palettes recalled from memory are dropped. Tableau 10, Observable 10, Category 10, Dark2 and Set1 are now measured from the installed d3-scale-chromatic 3.1.1.\n  - The first version's family/order statistics came from the faulty explorer and are withdrawn rather than rerun.\n\n**For the owner to judge:**\n- Whether the light set's light-and-deep rhythm is acceptable. Green, purple and magenta sit at L 0.43–0.49 against three colours near 3:1. The more even alternative is louder: its magenta reads hot pink.\n- The near-white dark focus ring, versus keeping the blue ring and a darker dark-mode blue.\n- The dark set's very light green, cyan and pink next to a deep purple (3.26:1) and a mid red (3.86:1).\n\n**Not verified:**\n- Browser suites, since they need `npm run build`.\n- Overlapping 25% fills (one translucent area over another).\n- Severities below 1.0.\n- Rendering outside headless Chrome 157 on Linux.\n- No independent review of the refit itself.\n\nFiles are in /home/canarit/projects/axylo:\n- packages/axylo/src/theme/palette.ts\n- packages/axylo/test/theme/palette.test.ts\n- docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md\n- research/palette/: new scripts anneal.mjs, raster.mjs and references.mjs, with their outputs raster.json and references.json; changes to color.mjs, fit.mjs, explore.mjs, score.mjs and the README; regenerated palette.json, strokes.json, preview.html and the two PNGs; configs/fit-final.json, which reproduces both arrays with complete searches.\n- configs/ was pruned to the searches the record cites; I deleted the old fit-c, f, g, k, l, n and o configs.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/react.review.md reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/react.fix.report.md'","aggregated_output":"<!-- Reviewer: Codex gpt-6.2-astra, effort xhigh (observed in the session rollout, thread 01a1ecae-3654-7253-956b-adcdb3b22b87), read-only, cross-provider. Reviewed commit be123ee. -->\n\n**B for shipping `be123ee`.** The documented `import { Line } from '@axylo/react'` fails. The most consequential runtime defect is the gap between React’s layout and passive effects. A parent can receive a ref whose chart is still `null`, or append to a chart whose new controlled data has committed in React but has yet to reach the core.\n\n### Findings, most severe first\n\n1. **B · The package exports files that the build never produces. Ran and inspected.** Node returned `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND` for `@axylo/react/dist/index.js`. The declaration entry is missing too. The root build targets only `packages/axylo`, and the adapter’s TypeScript project has `noEmit`. The size test nevertheless passes at **984 B gzip** because it bundles `src/index.ts` in memory. With the installed esbuild, that direct entry retained `\"use client\"`; a bundle that re-exported `Line` from another entry lost it. Build the adapter JS and declarations, select the development hint appropriately, then import, type-check, server-render and measure the **packed export**. Assert the directive on the shipped entry. This blocks DX1, DX7, DX8 and DX10 release verification. [Package exports](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/package.json:8) · [Build](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/scripts/build.mjs:5) · [Size test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/size.test.ts:10)\n\n2. **M · The ref becomes observable before creation and before updates. Reasoned from the adapter and installed React 19.3.1.** `useImperativeHandle` publishes during layout. Creation and patching happen in passive effects. On mount, a callback ref or parent layout effect sees `handle.chart === null` and receives no notification when it becomes usable. On update, commit `data=B`, then call `append(row)` in the parent layout effect. The core receives `append` against A, followed by `update({data:B})`, which erases the row. A diagnostics read in that layout effect likewise describes A. The core obeys §1.5 call order throughout. Synchronize creation and accepted props in the child’s client layout phase before publishing the usable handle. Test this sequence without waiting for frames, including on Suspense reveal. This affects §1.7 streaming and DX5. [Adapter](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:53) · [React’s imperative-handle implementation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/node_modules/react-dom/cjs/react-dom-client.development.js:9111)\n\n3. **M · A committed handler change can still call the previous handler. Reasoned.** Commit `onDiagnostic=B` over A, then trigger a synchronous diagnostic through the ref from a parent layout effect. The stable forwarder still reads A. Removing `onHover` has the same window in which the removed callback can fire. Once passive effects run, present → absent → present works, and diagnostics from the adapter’s own `update` see the new handler. Publish the latest handlers in the child’s client layout phase before externally observable chart calls. Add replacement, removal and reinstatement tests that fire during the commit. This affects §1.7 handlers and §1.5 synchronous diagnostic delivery. [Forwarder and ref update](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:51)\n\n4. **M · Explicit `undefined` can fail to restore a default. Ran the diff and modeled the public ref sequence.** Mount with `title` absent, call `chart.update({title:'Live'})` through the ref, then render `title={undefined}`. `diff({}, {title:undefined})` returns `null`, so `Live` survives. The reverse own-key transition also returns `null`. The test explicitly requires both results. Compare own-key presence as well as values and send an own `title: undefined` on the transition. Test against a prior imperative update so the reset has an observable effect. This violates §1.5 patch semantics and §1.7 removal semantics. [Diff](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:28) · [Test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/patch.test.ts:45)\n\n5. **M · Structural comparison can overflow before core validation. Ran.** Two separately allocated field objects with the same `field` and a self-referential metadata property produced `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`. A structurally typed field variable can carry that extra property. Two mistakenly supplied React elements with cyclic props also overflowed; two ordinary ownerless elements compared equal. `diff` runs outside the `update` try block, so the adapter can take down the chart with its own exception. Bound comparison to supported option shapes or track visited pairs and treat unsupported objects, including React elements, as opaque. Add a regression that reaches the update path. This affects §1.7 field comparison and DX4 diagnostic delivery. [Comparator](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:39) · [Call site](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:87)\n\n6. **M · Filling a hole in an option array can silently discard the change. Ran.** `diff({y:Array(1)}, {y:['v']})` returned `null`. Reversing the arguments emitted a patch. `a.every(...)` skips the previous hole, so correcting a sparse wide `y` or `reference` list can leave the chart on its old state. Compare indexed presence and values symmetrically, and test a sparse-to-dense correction in both directions. This violates whole-value replacement in §1.5. [Array comparison](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:43)\n\n7. **M · Container attributes go to chart validation and disappear from the DOM. Ran SSR, `optionsOf` and TypeScript probes.** `id`, `aria-label` and `data-testid` were absent from the server div and present in core options. TypeScript rejected `id` and `onClick` but accepted the hyphenated JSX attributes. Under the specified AX150 behavior, an ordinary `data-*` attribute can therefore reject a strict chart. Omitting `id` also prevents the documented element-id fallback for diagnostic names. This produces the same omission on server and client; it does not require a hydration mismatch to fail. Define the supported host attributes, route them to the div on both sides, and preserve validation of genuine unknown chart options. §1.7 does not promise every div prop, but forwarding these attributes to chart validation has observable DX3/DX4 consequences. [Prop types](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:19) · [Div](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:100) · [Option routing](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:9)\n\n8. **M · Importing only `Line` retains all five chart factories. Ran an in-memory consumer bundle.** The output constructed all five components and imported all five core factories. With distinct stand-in implementations, all five survived bundling. Marking the component factory calls pure in memory reduced the retained implementations to `line`. `sideEffects: false` alone does not eliminate those calls inside a used module. Preserve pure annotations through the emitted distribution or split the component entries, and check a packed `Line`-only consumer. This affects the plan’s per-chart tree-shaking goal and DX7. [Component exports](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:106)\n\n9. **m · An equal replacement can incur a full traversal on every subsequent render. Ran.** Replace a large `colors` record or `reference` list with an equal new object, then keep that new object stable. The null-patch return leaves `state.applied` pointing to the older object. In Node, 20 comparisons of 20,000 entries took about 72 ms for `colors` and 25 ms for `reference`. That is comparator time alone; the cost of a real core update was unmeasured. Record the latest successfully compared props on a no-op, and reserve unbounded structural work for options that require it. Same-reference in-place mutations are also invisible, so the comparison assumes immutable option values. `colors` is a record in the public API. [No-op return](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:87) · [Traversal](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:45)\n\n10. **m · Object enumeration can lose a field change or produce the wrong reset value. Ran.** Two plain field specs with different non-enumerable `field` values compared equal. Removing an own `toString` option produced the inherited function as the patch value. `optionsOf` also copied inherited enumerable props. These paths bypass the intended §1.5 replacement/reset or DX3 unknown-option handling. Compare the documented properties of field specs, enumerate own props when routing, and use own-key checks or prototype-free dictionaries for patch lookup. [Option copying](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:15) · [Lookup](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:30)\n\n11. **m · The memoization hint can advise against a real data update. Ran.** Equal-length arrays with identical endpoints and a changing middle row returned `true` from `looksSame`. Equal dates reconstructed in endpoint rows and freshly allocated typed columns with identical values returned `false`. After three qualifying replacements, the warning asserts that the data was the same. The module-wide `hinted` flag then suppresses hints for other charts. Confirm content before issuing that claim, or word the hint as a sampling heuristic and specify its scope. The §1.7 hint is affected. Actual `data` patches still use identity. [Sampling](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:53) · [Warning](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:79)\n\n12. **m · Deferring an effect error to component state can lose it. Reasoned from React’s commit-error path.** The comment that an effect throw never reaches a boundary is incorrect for a synchronous effect setup. If the chart effect catches a strict error and a parent removes the child in the same passive-effect batch, the child can disappear before it renders `failure`; a surviving boundary then receives nothing from this path. Let synchronous setup/update exceptions propagate to React’s commit-error handling, and test original-object identity with a boundary that stays mounted. In ordinary recovery, the current code does throw the original object, the fallback unmounts the failed instance, and reset mounts a fresh one. Changing props alone cannot clear `failure` on that instance. [Error handling](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:49) · [React’s boundary capture](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/node_modules/react-dom/cjs/react-dom-client.development.js:20217)\n\n13. **m · A setup failure after successful creation has no cleanup path. Reasoned; conditional on core behavior.** If listener registration throws, including through an immediately invoked user listener, the effect exits before returning its disposer. The successfully created chart then has no owner to destroy it. §1.5 promises teardown when `destroy()` is called; it does not specify registration-time event delivery. Destroy the local chart before propagating a setup failure. Separately, a factory that allocates resources and then throws must roll them back itself because the adapter receives no handle. Neither case requires `on` or `destroy` to throw a *strict validation* error. [Creation and listener installation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:61)\n\n14. **m · The teardown test observes an already settled chart. Reasoned from the harness.** `render()` waits for frames and `ready()` before unmount. A mutation that lets a pending draw run after teardown can pass because the draw has already happened. The destroy spy stores a boolean, so double destruction also passes. The listener probe excludes detached nodes and all `once` listeners, and the spy retains chart elements. Count destroys, unmount before the pending frame, and assert against the saved handle and scheduled callback. The current test establishes less than the DX5 teardown and leak criteria. [Waits](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/react.browser.test.ts:55) · [Spy](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/spy-axylo.ts:26) · [Probe](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/probe.ts:69)\n\n### Test coverage and mutations the seven reported checks missed\n\n| Tests examined | What their assertions distinguish, and what can still pass |\n|---|---|\n| `optionsOf`, `diff`, `equal`, `looksSame` | The examples distinguish ordinary additions, changes, removal of defined values, whole nested replacements, function changes and handler filtering. Deep-equality assertions can accept a copied `data` value. The property uses production `equal` as its oracle and compares indexed values, so it can share the comparator’s defect and miss own-key differences. Add independent presence, identity and symmetry assertions. [Patch tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/patch.test.ts:13) |\n| Five browser lifecycle cases, StrictMode, independence | The cases check factory kind, title updates, destruction and isolation between two charts. A render-phase write of `latest.current`, a duplicate destroy, or a stale saved handle can pass. Exercise an abandoned transition, an actual key change, and a saved getter across cleanup. [Lifecycle cases](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/react.browser.test.ts:82) |\n| Inline options, removed title, removed tooltip, memoization hint | The inline test counts updates. Title checks the own reset key and generated name. Tooltip checks restored behavior. None exercises removal of required fields. The hint test would accept a warning one or two replacements late because it checks only after three more renders. Assert on each replacement and include changing middle rows. [Prop cases](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/react.browser.test.ts:135) |\n| Handlers, ref, strict errors | Hover is exercised after settling. A broken `select` or `zoom` forwarder, stale layout-phase callback, or broken diagnostic removal can pass. The ref test always appends to a ready chart. The boundary test checks message text and remount counts; wrapping the error or advancing `applied` before a rejected update can pass because recovery creates a new instance. [Handler, ref and error cases](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/react.browser.test.ts:213) |\n| Server, hydration and size | The five SSR cases establish empty markup and zero factory calls. The class/style case checks exact HTML. The directive assertion reads source. The hydration control detects deliberately altered markup, but successful hydration never checks reuse of the original div and both sides use source. All these tests can pass with a broken package entry. Save the server node for an identity assertion and run the distribution checks through the package name. [Server cases](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/server.test.ts:23) · [Hydration](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/react.browser.test.ts:312) · [Size](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/size.test.ts:12) |\n| Type fixtures | I removed all 11 suppressions **in memory**. Every marked line failed. The misspellings, text value, donut options and scatter category produced the intended diagnostics. The two wide-`y` cases first complain about widened `string[]`; using `as const` isolates the forbidden combination. Every directive can mask another error on its line. Pair each negative case with the same otherwise-valid expression. JSX inferred rows and typed columns. `createElement(Line, …)` and `memo(Line)` lost row inference; specializing `Line<Row>` worked. [Type fixtures](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/types.check.tsx:32) |\n\n### Verification and contract limits\n\n`npm run typecheck` passed in the current checkout, including `test/types.check.tsx`. I ran the adapter’s Node suite with filesystem writes disabled, native config loading, threads, and both Vitest caches disabled. Its final lines were:\n\n```text\nTest Files  3 passed (3)\n     Tests  25 passed (25)\n  Start at  04:25:38\n  Duration  518ms (transform 65%, import 24%, tests 10%, worker 2%)\n```\n\nI changed no files. Browser behavior, Fast Refresh and React 18 were assessed by reading. Only React 19.3.1 ran. I did not inspect the changing core implementation. The adapter assumes the target’s `axylo` class is sufficient when React rewrites `className`. The browser spy also calls extracted `update` and `destroy` methods without their receiver; §1.5 allows a conforming implementation to use `this`. Preserve the receiver in the spy. The manifest has no direct `react-dom` peer, which matches the adapter’s imports; the workspace lockfile still lists that peer and should be synchronized. A consumer still needs a DOM renderer to mount or server-render the components. [Host class](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:98) · [Spy](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/spy-axylo.ts:26) · [Lockfile](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/package-lock.json:2271)\n\nNo core contract change is needed for these fixes. The fixed public `Chart` exposes `zoom()` and `on('zoom', …)` for **every** chart. I verified that a donut ref accepts both, while donut JSX rejects `zoom` and `onZoom`, and `donutRef.chart.update({zoom:true})` is rejected. A chart-specific handle that excludes those methods would require a deliberate public API decision. [Public handle](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/public.ts:137)\n\n### Areas examined and found sound\n\n- **Committed renders.** Reasoned. Creation, diffing and writes to `applied` occur in effects. An abandoned render publishes no options, callback change or patch.\n- **Normal lifecycle.** Reasoned. StrictMode deliberately creates twice with cleanup between setups. A key change disposes the old instance. A Fast Refresh remount or effect replay pairs successful setup with cleanup. The adapter’s normal path clears `live` before destruction. A caller who explicitly destroys the raw chart can still cause later adapter updates to reach it; §1.5 makes those calls no-ops.\n- **Suspense and ref identity.** Reasoned for installed React 19. A Suspense fallback disconnects the layout ref while the existing chart’s passive effect can remain connected. Reveal can publish a new wrapper around that chart. With an unchanged ref, ordinary rerenders keep the wrapper stable. A saved getter reads `null` after actual passive cleanup; during StrictMode replay it can subsequently see the replacement chart.\n- **Strict rejection and recovery.** Reasoned. A thrown `update` leaves `applied` at the last successful options, consistent with §1.5 rollback. The latest handler ref can already reflect the rejected render. Boundary remount gets fresh state. A strict `append` through the raw ref throws synchronously to its caller, and an event-handler caller must catch it there.\n- **Ordinary patches.** Ran. Removing previously defined `data`, `x`, `y` and `onDiagnostic` emitted own keys with `undefined`. Missing required fields then reach core validation. Event props use subscriptions, with absent handlers becoming no-ops after synchronization. The diagnostic forwarder is installed for a supplied callback and cleared on removal.\n- **Ordinary comparison.** Ran. Dense nested values replace whole options. Dates compare by timestamp, including two invalid dates. `NaN` compares equal and signed zeros differ. Functions, maps, typed arrays and class instances compare by identity. Data replacement uses identity; only the development hint inspects row endpoints or column references.\n- **Source SSR and option types.** Ran. All five components rendered an empty div with deterministic class/style, a null server ref and zero factory calls. Each JSX prop type uses its corresponding public options type, including area stacking, bar layout, scatter field restrictions and donut fields. By inspection, the hooks and `forwardRef` used here are available in React 18.<!-- Author: Claude Opus 5.5 (requested effort high), Agent-tool subagent a5529f23e59e816c4, resumed for the fix pass. Saved by the coordinator from the hand-back. -->\n\n## @axylo/react fix pass: report\n\nI fixed all 14 findings. For findings 1–8 and 10–14 I wrote a test first, saw it fail against the be123ee adapter for the reviewer's reason, then fixed the code. Finding 9 has a test but no one-line fix. All tests in packages/react now pass. The adapter now ships from a build: the production `dist/index.js` is **1427 B gzipped** (gzip level 9; 3377 B before gzip). Nothing is committed.\n\n**Command for the root build script**, after the core build because declarations need `packages/axylo/dist/index.d.ts`:\n```\nnode packages/react/build.mjs\n```\nThe root `typecheck` script already ends with `&& tsc -p packages/react`, and it passes. `tsc -p packages/react` works without dist.\n\n### Runs I made\n\n**Before the fix:**\n- New `patch.test.ts` cases against the old patch.ts: `Tests 8 failed | 14 passed (22)`.\n  - Errors seen: `{ title: undefined }` expected but got null; `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`; `Array(1)` to `['v']` gave a null patch; inherited props copied (`[ 'data', 'inherited' ]`); host props reached options.\n- New browser tests against the be123ee adapter: `Tests 13 failed | 18 passed (31)`.\n  - Errors seen: `expected 10 to be 11` (append erased); `Set{ 'diagnostic:A' }` instead of `'diagnostic:B'`; `[ 'zoom:A' ]` instead of `'zoom:B'`; `expected 5 to be 2` (repeated traversal); container attributes missing; `[0,0,0,1,1,1]` instead of `[0,0,0,2,2,2]` (hint per instance); `caught.length 0` instead of 1; `destroys [0]` instead of `[1]`.\n\n**After the fix:**\n- `npx tsc -p packages/react`: clean. It is also clean with dist removed.\n- `npm run typecheck`: passes.\n- `npx vitest run packages/react`: `Test Files 2 passed (2)`, `Tests 34 passed (34)`, Duration 1.15s.\n- `AXYLO_BROWSER=1 npx vitest run packages/react`, run twice: `Test Files 1 passed (1)`, `Tests 31 passed (31)`, Duration 16.76s.\n- Full `npm test`: `Tests 7 failed | 620 passed (627)`. All 7 are outside my package: six in `packages/axylo/test/theme/palette.test.ts` and one timing test in `packages/axylo/test/data/normalize.test.ts`.\n- **Finding 8 mutation:** with the production file built using `minify: true`, \"keeps only the line chart (conditions: default)\" fails. Restored afterwards, and the file passes again.\n\n### Per finding\n\n1. **No dist (B): fixed.**\n   - New `packages/react/build.mjs` and `tsconfig.build.json` produce:\n     - `dist/index.js`: production, `__DEV__` false, so the hint is removed.\n     - `dist/index.dev.js`: development, with the hint.\n     - `dist/index.d.ts`.\n   - This follows the core's convention: the files are chosen through a `development` export condition, not `NODE_ENV`. package.json exports are now `types`, `development` and `default`.\n   - Both files start with exactly one `'use client';` and contain no `__DEV__`.\n   - Tests in `test/dist.test.ts`, which builds first: \"starts with the 'use client' directive and has no bare __DEV__\" (both files); \"the production file drops the development hint…\"; \"the production file is at most 2 KiB gzipped\"; \"the declarations are what a consumer type-checks against\" (runs tsc on `test/consumer/consumer.tsx`, which imports `@axylo/react`; it fails when dist is absent); and SSR through `@axylo/react` and `dist/index.js`.\n   - I deleted the old `server.test.ts` and `size.test.ts`, which tested src.\n2. **Ref before create / call order (M): fixed.**\n   - Create, patch and handler publication now run in `useLayoutEffect` on the client. They are declared before `useImperativeHandle`, so they run before it.\n   - On the server `useEffect` is used, so React 18 gives no SSR warning (not verified: React 18 is not installed).\n   - Destroy runs in a passive cleanup, so a Suspense hide keeps the chart.\n   - Tests: \"on mount, a callback ref and the parent layout effect see a created chart\"; \"a parent layout effect that appends after a data change appends to the new data\"; \"diagnostics read in a parent layout effect describe the props of that commit\"; \"after a Suspense fallback, the revealed chart is the same one and matches the revealing commit\".\n3. **Stale handler during commit (M): fixed.** `latest` is written in the layout effect.\n   - Tests: \"onDiagnostic replaced, removed and reinstated takes effect within the commit\" and \"onZoom replaced, removed and reinstated…\". Both fire through the ref from the parent's layout effect.\n   - Also \"an abandoned transition applies nothing…\", a guard against writes during render.\n4. **Explicit undefined (M): fixed.** Presence now counts, and the patch carries an own `title: undefined`.\n   - I corrected the wrong-requirement test into \"sends the transition between an absent prop and an explicit undefined, as an own key\".\n   - Browser test: \"title={undefined} after a title set through the ref restores the generated name\".\n5. **Comparator overflow (M): fixed.**\n   - Comparison now tracks the pairs it has already visited.\n   - React elements (`$$typeof`), class instances and anything not plain are compared by identity.\n   - `equal` catches internally, so a throwing getter means \"unequal\".\n   - Test: \"never throws, and treats values it does not understand by identity\". It covers cyclic field specs, mutually recursive objects, a throwing getter and cyclic React elements.\n6. **Sparse arrays (M): fixed.** Index presence is compared, and the comparison is symmetric. Test: \"sees a hole filled or opened in an option array, in both directions\".\n   - The property test now uses an oracle independent of `equal`, which tells holes, undefined and absence apart and checks symmetry.\n7. **Host attributes (M): fixed as you decided.**\n   - `id`, `className`, `style` and `data-*` go to the div on server and client and never reach the options.\n   - `aria-*` reaches core validation; the reason is in a comment in `patch.ts`.\n   - Types: `ContainerProps` with `id` and `` `data-${string}` ``.\n   - Tests: the optionsOf unit test; dist SSR \"id, className, style and data-* render on the container…\"; browser \"id, className, style and data-* reach the container, never the chart; aria-* is reported as an unknown option\" (a strict chart with `data-testid` is not rejected, and `aria-label` produces AX150).\n8. **Tree-shaking (M): fixed.** The five definitions carry `/* @__PURE__ */`.\n   - esbuild's whitespace minification drops those marks. So the production file minifies identifiers and syntax only, which is why it is 1427 B rather than 1203 B.\n   - Test: \"keeps only the line chart\", for the default and `development` conditions. It bundles a consumer that imports only `Line`, with marker stand-ins for the core.\n9. **Repeated traversal (m): fixed.** `applied` now advances on a no-op too. Test: \"an equal replacement object is compared once, not on every later render\", using a proxy that counts key reads.\n   - I did not limit how deep structural comparison goes for any option. Options are assumed immutable, and a comment in `diff` says so.\n10. **Enumeration (m): fixed.** Comparison uses own keys including non-enumerable ones (`Reflect.ownKeys`), `optionsOf` copies own props only, and patch lookups check own keys. Tests: \"compares own properties, enumerable or not, and never reads inherited ones\" and \"copies own props only\".\n11. **Hint (m): fixed.** It is now per chart instance and worded as a sample (\"same length and the same first and last rows (only those are compared)\").\n    - Endpoint dates are compared by time, and typed columns by length and endpoints.\n    - Tests: the looksSame unit test, and browser \"the memoization hint: once per chart, on the third endpoint-equal replacement, worded as a sample\". That test asserts after each replacement, with a middle row changing.\n12. **Error path (m): fixed.**\n    - **Observed on React 19.3.1:** a throw from the layout effect reaches the boundary as the original object (`caught === spy.errors` holds, by identity), on create and on update. So I removed the state-and-rethrow path.\n    - Test: \"a strict rejection reaches the boundary even when the parent removes the chart right after the commit\". The old code lost the error there.\n13. **Setup failure (m): fixed.** The chart is destroyed before the error from `on` propagates. Test: \"a setup failure after the chart exists destroys it before the error propagates\".\n14. **Teardown test strength (m): fixed.**\n    - The spy now counts destroys, holds charts and elements through `WeakRef`, and calls `update` and `destroy` with the handle as `this`.\n    - The probe now tracks `once` listeners too.\n    - New tests: \"unmounting before the first frame cancels it: nothing is drawn after destroy, destroy runs once\"; \"a key change destroys the old chart once and creates a new one\"; a saved handle reads `null` after unmount; hydration checks that the server-rendered div is reused.\n    - Also added \"the latest onSelect is called on click\".\n\n### For you to decide or relay\n- **Core (not mine):** after destroy, the core leaves listeners on its own removed elements (keyboard proxy `input`, toolbar, legend and control buttons). They cannot fire. The StrictMode test lists them and asserts that every such element is one of the core's `axylo-*` elements, none of them React's container. Strict cleanliness would need a change in `src/dom`.\n- **Core behaviour (not mine):** a click right after hovering lands on the floating tooltip, so no `select` fires. The select test uses `tooltip: false` for that reason.\n- **Trade-off:** the Suspense decision means the chart survives being hidden. An Activity or Offscreen hide, which disconnects passive effects, destroys it, and the reveal creates a new chart from the latest props.\n- **Not verified:** React 18 (only 19.3.1 is installed) and Fast Refresh.\n- `package-lock.json` still lists the `react-dom` peer. That file is not mine.\n- `packages/react/test/browser/.out/` (the generated test bundle) is still not covered by `.gitignore`; dist is covered by the existing `dist/` rule.\n\nAll files are in /home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/:\n- new: `build.mjs`, `tsconfig.build.json`, `test/dist.test.ts`, `test/consumer/consumer.tsx`, `test/consumer/tsconfig.json`\n- changed: `src/index.ts`, `src/patch.ts`, `package.json`, `tsconfig.json` (excludes `test/consumer`), `test/patch.test.ts`, `test/types.check.tsx` (each negative case now has a valid twin; wide `y` uses `as const`), `test/browser/app.tsx`, `test/browser/probe.ts`, `test/browser/spy-axylo.ts`, `test/browser/react.browser.test.ts`\n- deleted: `test/server.test.ts`, `test/size.test.ts`\n\nThe core under `packages/axylo/src` was being edited during my runs. None of my browser failures came from the core.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
