{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md | sed -n '1,255p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Decision 0003: the default categorical palette\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: **proposed, refitted after review.** The first version (commit 51e1be5) was reviewed by Codex gpt-6.2-astra (`reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/palette.review.md`). The review confirmed the arithmetic and showed four things wrong: the uneven lightness was claimed to be forced and is not; simulated screenshots and 8-bit fills fell below the limits the record promised; the order explorer could prune a feasible order after a timeout; and the record overclaimed in several places. This version is a refit with byte-level checks and corrected text. The hard constraints are measured and tested. Whether the result is good to look at is the owner's call (see \"For the owner\"). The refit itself has not been reviewed.\n     4\t\n     5\tSpec: A5 (palette thresholds, provisional), L2 (3:1 for marks), F9 (eight series). Finding R7, docs/01-landscape.md.\n     6\t\n     7\t## The palette\n     8\t\n     9\tSlot order: blue, orange, green, purple, red, cyan, magenta, slate. That is the first version's order. Hue families are kept per slot.\n    10\t\n    11\t| Slot | Family | Light | OKLCH | Contrast on #ffffff | Dark | OKLCH | Contrast on #16181d | Hue shift |\n    12\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    13\t| 1 | blue | `#4a94eb` | 0.66 0.150 254 | 3.12 | `#64acfa` | 0.73 0.135 252 | 7.46 | 2° |\n    14\t| 2 | orange | `#e97200` | 0.68 0.175 52 | 3.06 | `#de7b2e` | 0.68 0.150 54 | 5.90 | 2° |\n    15\t| 3 | green | `#136102` | 0.43 0.140 141 | 7.67 | `#b2e390` | 0.86 0.121 133 | 12.08 | 8° |\n    16\t| 4 | purple | `#7314a2` | 0.43 0.205 310 | 8.93 | `#9149b9` | 0.54 0.177 312 | 3.26 | 2° |\n    17\t| 5 | red | `#bd585e` | 0.59 0.130 18 | 4.45 | `#c3513d` | 0.58 0.151 32 | 3.86 | 14° |\n    18\t| 6 | cyan | `#007baa` | 0.55 0.115 234 | 4.76 | `#71d8ff` | 0.83 0.110 225 | 10.97 | 9° |\n    19\t| 7 | magenta | `#a52265` | 0.49 0.175 355 | 6.96 | `#f898b5` | 0.79 0.119 1 | 8.58 | 6° |\n    20\t| 8 | slate | `#9293a5` | 0.67 0.026 283 | 3.03 | `#7a7f93` | 0.60 0.031 274 | 4.47 | 9° |\n    21\t\n    22\t**One token changes.** The dark focus token goes from `#79a8ff` to a near-white `#e3e8f0`, which is 14.4:1 on #16181d. The old focus blue sat at OKLab L 0.735, exactly where a dark-mode series blue belongs. With it kept, no dark set I found had a first blue above L 0.55. The exact search with the blue asked for at 0.62 ended at `#3970c2`, L 0.55 (`configs/dark-n2.json`, best within 300 s). The light focus `#1f5fcc` is unchanged; `keyboard.browser.test.ts` hardcodes it. Every other token is unchanged.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Before and after.**\n    25\t\n    26\t| | 51e1be5 | Now |\n    27\t|---|---|---|\n    28\t| Light OKLab L, lowest to highest | 0.39 to 0.68 | 0.43 to 0.68 |\n    29\t| Light, spread across the first three | 0.26 | 0.25 |\n    30\t| Light, largest chroma | 0.17 | 0.205 (purple) |\n    31\t| Dark first blue | `#1c73c5`, L 0.55, 3.64:1 | `#64acfa`, L 0.73, 7.46:1 |\n    32\t| Dark, largest chroma | 0.15 | 0.177 (purple) |\n    33\t| Largest light/dark hue shift | 18.5° (slate), 8° chromatic | 13.6° (red) |\n    34\t| Byte-rounded simulation, worst pair | 0.098 (light protan 6–8) | 0.102 |\n    35\t| 25% fills on an 8-bit raster, worst neighbours | 0.0590 (review's raster) | 0.063 (Chrome, canvas and SVG) |\n    36\t\n    37\tThe dark blue now has ordinary dark-mode lightness. The light set did not get much more even. Slot 3's green is still the heaviest of the first three lines on white (L 0.43 against 0.66 and 0.68). \"Tried and failed\" below says what I searched for that and why I stopped.\n    38\t\n    39\t![Light scheme preview](../../research/palette/preview-light.png)\n    40\t\n    41\t![Dark scheme preview](../../research/palette/preview-dark.png)\n    42\t\n    43\tEach preview shows:\n    44\t- the swatches, with hex, contrast and nearest neighbour;\n    45\t- lines with 3, 5 and 8 series at 2 px, with Axylo's end markers;\n    46\t- three overlapping areas at 25%;\n    47\t- eight stacked areas at 80%;\n    48\t- an eight-slice donut;\n    49\t- grouped bars.\n    50\t\n    51\tThe same charts are repeated under the three simulations (the Machado matrices as SVG color matrices in linear RGB) and in grayscale. Source: `research/palette/preview.html`.\n    52\t\n    53\t## What is measured, and on what\n    54\t\n    55\tThere are three forms of measurement:\n    56\t\n    57\t- **Analytic.** The 8-bit hex is decoded, then simulated or composited in floating point, then converted to OKLab.\n    58\t- **Bytes.** Values are rounded where an 8-bit image would round them. A simulated color is encoded and rounded to the nearest byte before the OKLab conversion; that is what the simulated rows of the preview PNGs hold. A translucent fill is quantised as a raster would quantise it: alpha at round(α×255), each channel rounded. That reproduces Chrome 157's canvas and SVG fills on white exactly and is within one byte on #16181d. The second variant, alpha at floor(α×255), is what the review's CPU Skia raster did.\n    59\t- **Chrome.** The fill pixels Chrome actually drew, canvas and SVG (`research/palette/raster.mjs`).\n    60\t\n    61\tThe unit test requires the analytic and the byte forms. It checks the recorded Chrome pixels against the 0.06 limit, and checks that each recorded pixel is within one byte of the model. A stricter margin is the worst case over every byte color within one byte per channel of both fills. It is recorded but not required, because I found no palette that meets it together with the rest (0.054–0.056 is the best I saw; see below).\n    62\t\n    63\t## Constraints, measured\n    64\t\n    65\tThese come from `research/palette/palette.json`, which `score.mjs` writes. The unit test recomputes each value with its own code and compares it at the written precision, including which pair is closest.\n    66\t\n    67\t| Constraint | Check | Limit | Light | Dark |\n    68\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n    69\t| 1 | lowest contrast on background | ≥ 3 | 3.03 (slate) | 3.26 (purple) |\n    70\t| 2 | min pairwise ΔE, all 8, normal / protan / deutan / tritan | ≥ 0.10 | 0.124 / 0.103 / 0.103 / 0.102 | 0.116 / 0.104 / 0.103 / 0.102 |\n    71\t| 2, bytes | the same after the simulation is rounded to bytes | ≥ 0.10 | 0.124 / 0.103 / 0.103 / 0.102 | 0.116 / 0.103 / 0.102 / 0.102 |\n    72\t| 3 | first 4, worst vision (analytic; bytes) | ≥ 0.14 | 0.156 protan; 0.155 | 0.158 tritan; 0.159 |\n    73\t| 3 | first 3, 5, 6, worst vision (analytic) | ≥ 0.10 | 0.156 / 0.102 / 0.102 | 0.158 / 0.103 / 0.103 |\n    74\t| 4 | largest light/dark OKLCH hue shift | ≤ 30° | 13.6° (red) | same pairs |\n    75\t| 5 | nearest series color to focus / reference / muted | ≥ 0.10 | 0.101 (cyan) / 0.152 (green) / 0.110 (cyan) | 0.138 (cyan) / 0.104 (cyan) / 0.113 (slate) |\n    76\t| 6 | neighbour fills at 25%: ideal / byte models / Chrome canvas / Chrome SVG | ≥ 0.06 | 0.064 / 0.063 / 0.063 / 0.063 | 0.064 / 0.064 / 0.063 / 0.063 |\n    77\t| 6 | neighbour fills at 80%: ideal / byte models / Chrome | ≥ 0.06 | 0.201 / 0.202 / 0.202 | 0.179 / 0.179 / 0.179 |\n    78\t| 6, recorded only | 25% fills, worst case within one byte | (0.06) | 0.056 | 0.056 |\n    79\t\n    80\t\"Muted\" is my addition to constraint 5: the donut draws its \"Other\" slice in the muted token, next to series slices.\n    81\t\n    82\t### Prefix minima (constraint 3)\n    83\t\n    84\tEach cell is analytic / bytes, with the closest slots.\n    85\t\n    86\t| Prefix | Vision | Light | Dark |\n    87\t|---|---|---|---|\n    88\t| first 3 | normal | 0.319 / 0.319 (1–2) | 0.251 / 0.251 (1–3) |\n    89\t| first 3 | protan | 0.156 / 0.155 (2–3) | 0.247 / 0.246 (1–3) |\n    90\t| first 3 | deutan | 0.270 / 0.271 (2–3) | 0.178 / 0.177 (2–3) |\n    91\t| first 3 | tritan | 0.243 / 0.241 (1–3) | 0.158 / 0.159 (1–3) |\n    92\t| first 4 | normal | 0.284 / 0.284 | 0.246 / 0.246 |\n    93\t| first 4 | protan | 0.156 / 0.155 | 0.247 / 0.246 |\n    94\t| first 4 | deutan | 0.217 / 0.217 | 0.178 / 0.177 |\n    95\t| first 4 | tritan | 0.157 / 0.157 | 0.158 / 0.159 |\n    96\t| first 5 | normal / protan / deutan / tritan | 0.135 / 0.103 / 0.136 / 0.102 | 0.116 / 0.126 / 0.109 / 0.103 |\n    97\t| first 6 | normal / protan / deutan / tritan | 0.124 / 0.103 / 0.117 / 0.102 | 0.116 / 0.124 / 0.109 / 0.103 |\n    98\t\n    99\tThe full tables with pair identities for every row are printed by `node research/palette/score.mjs --md`.\n   100\t\n   101\t### Tight pairs and margins\n   102\t\n   103\tThese are every measured value within 5% of its limit (`palette.json`, `tight`):\n   104\t\n   105\t- **Light:**\n   106\t  - contrast 3.026 (slate; the solver keeps 3.02);\n   107\t  - all-eight pairwise 0.102–0.103 under the three deficiencies, analytic and in bytes;\n   108\t  - first five and six 0.102–0.103;\n   109\t  - focus distance 0.101 (cyan against `#1f5fcc`).\n   110\t- **Dark:**\n   111\t  - all-eight pairwise 0.102–0.104;\n   112\t  - first five and six 0.102–0.103;\n   113\t  - reference distance 0.104 (cyan against `#bcc0c9`).\n   114\t- **Fills, both schemes:** the 25% neighbours have about 0.003–0.004 of room; the 80% fills have a lot.\n   115\t\n   116\tHow fragile is that to a hand edit? I tried every one-byte change of one channel of one color: 41 of 46 light edits and 46 of 47 dark edits still meet every required check (`palette.json`, `oneByteEdits`). The first version of this record said that \"almost any hand edit fails\". That was wrong; the review measured 36/48 and 32/47 for 51e1be5. A changed hex still fails the record test until `score.mjs` is rerun.\n   117\t\n   118\tThe solver kept margins above the tested limits: pairwise 0.102, fills 0.063, contrast 3.02, hue shift 15° for chromatic slots (`configs/fit-final.json`, `solverLimits`). In the final boxes it proved 0.065/0.103 and 0.066/0.102 infeasible, so the margins cannot be much larger near this palette.\n   119\t\n   120\t## Thin lines\n   121\t\n   122\t`strokes.mjs` draws each color as a 2 px random-walk polyline in headless Chrome 157 (canvas and SVG, device pixel ratio 1 and 2) and reads the pixels back. For each pixel column it records two values:\n   123\t\n   124\t- **Core:** the most covered pixel.\n   125\t- **Weighted:** the coverage-weighted mean of the touched pixels, bg + (Σc²/Σc)(ink − bg), with coverage at or below 0.02 dropped.\n   126\t\n   127\tThis is a legibility measurement of this rendering path, not the WCAG test. WCAG 1.4.11 allows the specified stroke color to be assessed despite antialiasing, and A5 is met on the swatch.\n   128\t\n   129\t| | Swatch | Core p10 | Weighted p10 / median, DPR 1 | Weighted p10 / median, DPR 2 |\n   130\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n   131\t| light blue `#4a94eb` | 3.12 | 3.12 | 2.47 / 2.57 | 2.75 / 2.84 |\n   132\t| light orange `#e97200` | 3.06 | 3.06 | 2.50 / 2.59 | 2.75 / 2.81 |\n   133\t| light slate `#9293a5` | 3.03 | 3.03 | 2.38 / 2.47 | 2.65 / 2.71 |\n   134\t| light red, cyan | 4.45, 4.76 | same | 3.27 / 3.44, 3.46 / 3.64 | 3.75 / 3.91, 4.03 / 4.18 |\n   135\t| light green, purple, magenta | 6.96–8.93 | same | 4.86 and up | 5.80 and up |\n   136\t| dark purple `#9149b9` | 3.26 | 3.26 | 2.57 / 2.65 | 2.87 / 2.95 |\n   137\t| dark red `#c3513d` | 3.86 | 3.86 | 2.93 / 3.09 | 3.35 / 3.43 |\n   138\t| dark slate `#7a7f93` | 4.47 | 4.47 | 3.36 / 3.55 | 3.88 / 3.99 |\n   139\t| dark blue, orange, green, cyan, pink | 5.90–12.08 | same | 4.27 and up | 5.00 and up |\n   140\t\n   141\tThe core column is the 10th percentile. In at least 90% of pixel columns the most covered pixel has the swatch's contrast. The review found that a few columns, about one in 576 at DPR 1, have no fully covered pixel (for the first version's orange, the least-covered core was 96.6%). Canvas and SVG agree within 0.05. Every series also has a full-color marker at its last point and at sparse intervals (spec A5).\n   142\t\n   143\t## Why lightness spreads on white (corrected)\n   144\t\n   145\tThe first version said that each simulation \"removes one chromatic axis\", and that three deep colors were therefore forced. Both statements were wrong.\n   146\t\n   147\tThe Machado matrices act in linear RGB, not in OKLab. In OKLab they mostly compress the red–green direction (protanopia, deuteranopia) or the blue–yellow direction (tritanopia), but they also move lightness. For example, the first version's light blue has L 0.659 unsimulated and between 0.643 and 0.691 across the simulations. The compression is a design heuristic, not a proof.\n   148\t\n   149\tThe review then exhibited a two-scheme palette meeting all six constraints with light L from 0.48 to 0.69:\n   150\t\n   151\t```text\n   152\tlight  #3791ff #d28204 #2b6d48 #881bbd #c70f09 #1f7fae #fe1ce0 #959396\n   153\tdark   #63d2fc #f9c309 #78aa6f #bf37be #e42129 #4574fa #ee5ec3 #7e7c80\n   154\t```\n   155\t\n   156\tIt lay outside the boxes my what-if runs had searched. What it costs is chroma: its magenta has chroma 0.30, its red 0.21 and its purple 0.23, and a hue shift sits at 29.97°.\n   157\t\n   158\t## How the refit was done\n   159\t\n   160\t1. **Wider boxes, exact search.** `fit.mjs` with light floors at L 0.44–0.48 and chroma up to 0.18–0.22 (`configs/even-a.json`, `even-c.json`). Both now require analytic and byte-rounded pairwise distances and both byte models for fills. No palette was found within 300 s. That is a timeout, not a proof.\n   161\t2. **Annealing.** `anneal.mjs` searches the same grids by minimising the total shortfall. I checked it first on a known-feasible neighbourhood: it recovered the review's palette. From random starts it found feasible light sets only once the boxes were split into tiers: blue, orange and slate near 3:1, red and cyan in the middle, green, purple and magenta lower (`configs/tier-44-free.json`). It found nothing with every color at L 0.46 or above (`configs/tier-46-cr.json`).\n   162\t3. **Dark against that light set.** The searches with the old focus token failed: `dark-a2.json` (annealing) and `dark-n2.json` (exact; best blue L 0.55). Moving the dark focus to near-white let annealing find a set with a light dark-mode blue and a deeper purple (`configs/dark-c.json`).\n   163\t4. **Polish.** `configs/fit-final.json` puts small boxes around both results and adds the solver margins. Both exact searches complete, so the run is deterministic and reproduces the shipped arrays: `node research/palette/fit.mjs --config=research/palette/configs/fit-final.json --seconds=300`.\n   164\t5. **Explorer fixed.** `explore.mjs orders` now keeps three outcomes per order (feasible, proved infeasible, timed out). It prunes only on a completed proof and prints all three. The first version's family and order statistics came from the faulty explorer, and I did not rerun them in full, so they are withdrawn.\n   165\t\n   166\t## Tried and failed\n   167\t\n   168\tEach line below means \"not found\". Only searches that complete prove anything, and those are marked.\n   169\t\n   170\t- **A lighter, calmer slot-3 green** (L 0.46 or above, chroma 0.11 or below), with restrained chroma elsewhere (`configs/green-a.json`; `green-b.json`, which also allowed a saturated red): nothing. The shortfalls were green against red under protanopia, and magenta against cyan and slate. The review's palette does have such a green (`#2b6d48`). It pays for it with a near-3:1 magenta at chroma 0.30, which the walk below could not bring down.\n   171\t- **Walking from the review's palette toward lower chroma** while staying feasible (`configs/walk-b.json`): the green and red calmed down, but the magenta stayed at chroma 0.28 or more (`#fd26e0`).\n   172\t- **The review's tier structure with chroma held at 0.22 or less** (`configs/cx-a.json`): nothing; magenta was always the shortfall.\n   173\t- **Red third**, as in Tableau 10 (`configs/red3-a.json`): nothing; red was short against green under protanopia.\n   174\t- **Magenta fourth instead of purple**, to relieve the dark blue (`configs/mag4-a.json`): feasible on white. Its dark set was proved infeasible with the old focus token (exact search, `configs/dark-n2-m4.json`), and annealing found none with the new one (`configs/dark-b2-m4.json`).\n   175\t- **Fills at the worst case within one byte per channel**: never met together with the other goals. The shipped palette's worst case is 0.056 in both schemes, short by 0.004.\n   176\t- **A palette with every light color at L 0.48 or above that also keeps chroma at 0.22 or less**: none found. As far as I can tell, the lightness-for-chroma trade the review's palette makes is real. What is not proved is that a better point does not exist; the searches above were limited in time and in boxes.\n   177\t\n   178\t## Additions beyond the six constraints\n   179\t\n   180\t- **Muted token** in constraint 5 (tested). The donut's \"Other\" slice.\n   181\t- **Byte-level checks** (tested): byte-rounded simulation; fills on both 8-bit raster models; Chrome's drawn pixels within one byte of the model and 0.06 apart.\n   182\t- **Ring closure and deficiency-simulated fills** (recorded only). At 25% the donut's closing pairs go down to 0.032 (light 8–1) and 0.037 (dark 6–1). Neighbours under simulation go down to 0.024 (light, deuteranopia). Slices are opaque in Axylo, so constraint 2 covers them.\n   183\t- **Solver margins** as listed above; the tests use the decision's limits.\n   184\t\n   185\t## For the owner\n   186\t\n   187\t- **Is the light set's weight acceptable?** Three colors (green, purple, magenta at L 0.43–0.49) are much darker than the three at 3:1. In the 3-series chart the green line is clearly the heaviest. I could not remove that at restrained chroma. The review's palette is more even and noticeably louder; its magenta reads as hot pink.\n   188\t- **The dark focus ring becomes near-white `#e3e8f0`** (light stays blue). This is a visible UI change, made to free the dark blue. The alternative is to keep `#79a8ff` and accept a dark blue near L 0.55.\n   189\t- **The dark set mixes very light green, cyan and pink** (L 0.79–0.86) with a deep purple (0.54) and a mid red (0.58). Purple and red are the dimmest (3.26 and 3.86:1).\n   190\t- **Slot hues moved within each family.** The light red is a coral (hue 18), the dark red a brick (hue 32). That 13.6° shift is the largest.\n   191\t\n   192\t## Reference palettes (measured)\n   193\t\n   194\tThese are read from the installed d3-scale-chromatic 3.1.1 (`research/harness/node_modules`) by `research/palette/references.mjs` and scored with the same code (`research/palette/references.json`). Minima are over the first eight colors.\n   195\t\n   196\t| Scheme | Below 3:1 on white | Min ΔE normal / protan / deutan / tritan (closest slots) |\n   197\t|---|---|---|\n   198\t| schemeTableau10 | 5 of 10 | 0.129 / 0.016 / 0.007 (3–5, red–green) / 0.064 |\n   199\t| schemeObservable10 | 6 of 10 | 0.113 / 0.059 / 0.020 / 0.046 |\n   200\t| schemeCategory10 (D3's default, Plotly's) | 4 of 10 | 0.113 / 0.007 (2–3) / 0.039 / 0.042 |\n   201\t| schemeDark2 | 2 of 8 | 0.099 / 0.018 / 0.017 / 0.049 |\n   202\t| schemeSet1 | 5 of 9 | 0.130 / 0.031 / 0.035 / 0.066 |\n   203\t\n   204\tLibrary defaults as rendered are in R7 (docs/01-landscape.md). That table has eight displayed six-slice rows. Recharts' row uses its documentation's authored colors, the D3 chart is authored, uPlot has no default palette, and ECharts was read from its options. What I took from these: the blue-then-orange opening (Tableau 10, Category 10), a quiet neutral last, and the evidence that the familiar sets put adjacent hues that deuteranopia merges (Tableau 10's red and green at 0.007). The first version also drew on palettes recalled from memory (IBM Carbon, Okabe–Ito, Paul Tol). No arrays for them are installed here, so they are not cited.\n   205\t\n   206\t## Not verified\n   207\t\n   208\t- The browser suites that sample palette colors were not run, because they need `npm run build`.\n   209\t  - `render.browser.test.ts` and `theme.browser.test.ts` read the colors from `palette.ts`, so they should follow; that is inferred.\n   210\t  - `keyboard.browser.test.ts` checks the light focus only, and that token is unchanged.\n   211\t  - Any browser test that checks the dark focus ring's color would see `#e3e8f0` now.\n   212\t- Raster checks cover isolated fills over the background. A 25% area over another 25% area (overlapping areas) is not scored.\n   213\t- The simulation is Machado at severity 1.0 only.\n   214\t- Strokes and fills were measured in headless Chrome 157 on Linux only.\n   215\t- Nobody with a color-vision deficiency has looked at the previews.\n   216\t\n   217\t## Files\n   218\t\n   219\t- `packages/axylo/src/theme/palette.ts`: both series sets and the dark focus token. Exported names and shapes are unchanged.\n   220\t- `packages/axylo/test/theme/palette.test.ts`: constraints 1–6, analytic and in bytes; Chrome fill pixels; the record at written precision; stroke-record validation.\n   221\t- `research/palette/`: `color.mjs`, `fit.mjs`, `anneal.mjs`, `explore.mjs`, `score.mjs`, `raster.mjs`, `strokes.mjs`, `preview.mjs`, `references.mjs`, `configs/`, `palette.json`, `raster.json`, `strokes.json`, `references.json`, `preview.html` and the two PNGs. See the README there.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba research/palette/color.mjs; nl -ba research/palette/anneal.mjs; nl -ba research/palette/explore.mjs'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba research/palette/color.mjs; nl -ba research/palette/anneal.mjs; nl -ba research/palette/explore.mjs'","aggregated_output":"     1\t// Pure color math and the palette constraints of decision 0003, shared by the solver, the scorer and the\n     2\t// preview. No dependencies, no I/O. The formulas match research/harness/palette.mjs, which scored the ten\n     3\t// libraries in finding R7: WCAG 2 relative luminance and contrast; OKLab (Ottosson 2020); color-vision\n     4\t// deficiency simulated with the Machado, Oliveira and Fernandes (2009) matrices at severity 1.0, applied in\n     5\t// linear RGB and clamped to [0, 1] before the OKLab conversion.\n     6\t\n     7\texport const lin = v => (v <= 0.04045 ? v / 12.92 : ((v + 0.055) / 1.055) ** 2.4);\n     8\texport const gam = v => (v <= 0.0031308 ? 12.92 * v : 1.055 * v ** (1 / 2.4) - 0.055);\n     9\tconst clamp01 = v => (v < 0 ? 0 : v > 1 ? 1 : v);\n    10\t\n    11\t/** '#rrggbb' -> gamma-encoded sRGB channels in [0, 1]. */\n    12\texport const srgb = hex => [1, 3, 5].map(i => parseInt(hex.slice(i, i + 2), 16) / 255);\n    13\t/** Gamma-encoded sRGB in [0, 1] -> '#rrggbb' (clamped, rounded). */\n    14\texport const toHex = rgb => '#' + rgb.map(v => Math.round(255 * clamp01(v)).toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');\n    15\texport const linear = hex => srgb(hex).map(lin);\n    16\t\n    17\texport const luminance = hex => { const [r, g, b] = linear(hex); return 0.2126 * r + 0.7152 * g + 0.0722 * b; };\n    18\texport const contrast = (a, b) => { const [x, y] = [luminance(a), luminance(b)].sort((p, q) => q - p); return (x + 0.05) / (y + 0.05); };\n    19\t\n    20\t/** Linear sRGB -> OKLab [L, a, b]. */\n    21\texport function oklab([r, g, b]) {\n    22\t  const l = Math.cbrt(0.4122214708 * r + 0.5363325363 * g + 0.0514459929 * b);\n    23\t  const m = Math.cbrt(0.2119034982 * r + 0.6806995451 * g + 0.1073969566 * b);\n    24\t  const s = Math.cbrt(0.0883024619 * r + 0.2817188376 * g + 0.6299787005 * b);\n    25\t  return [0.2104542553 * l + 0.793617785 * m - 0.0040720468 * s,\n    26\t    1.9779984951 * l - 2.428592205 * m + 0.4505937099 * s,\n    27\t    0.0259040371 * l + 0.7827717662 * m - 0.808675766 * s];\n    28\t}\n    29\t\n    30\t/** OKLab -> linear sRGB (may be out of gamut). */\n    31\texport function oklabToLinear([L, a, b]) {\n    32\t  const l = (L + 0.3963377774 * a + 0.2158037573 * b) ** 3;\n    33\t  const m = (L - 0.1055613458 * a - 0.0638541728 * b) ** 3;\n    34\t  const s = (L - 0.0894841775 * a - 1.291485548 * b) ** 3;\n    35\t  return [4.0767416621 * l - 3.3077115913 * m + 0.2309699292 * s,\n    36\t    -1.2684380046 * l + 2.6097574011 * m - 0.3413193965 * s,\n    37\t    -0.0041960863 * l - 0.7034186147 * m + 1.707614701 * s];\n    38\t}\n    39\t\n    40\t/** OKLCH (hue in degrees) -> '#rrggbb', or null when outside the sRGB gamut. */\n    41\texport function lchToHex([L, C, h]) {\n    42\t  const rad = h * Math.PI / 180;\n    43\t  const rgb = oklabToLinear([L, C * Math.cos(rad), C * Math.sin(rad)]);\n    44\t  if (!rgb.every(v => v >= -1e-4 && v <= 1 + 1e-4)) return null;\n    45\t  return toHex(rgb.map(v => gam(clamp01(v))));\n    46\t}\n    47\t\n    48\t/** '#rrggbb' -> OKLCH [L, C, h°]. */\n    49\texport function lch(hex) {\n    50\t  const [L, a, b] = oklab(linear(hex));\n    51\t  const h = (Math.atan2(b, a) * 180 / Math.PI + 360) % 360;\n    52\t  return [L, Math.hypot(a, b), h];\n    53\t}\n    54\t\n    55\texport const hueDiff = (h1, h2) => { const d = Math.abs(h1 - h2) % 360; return d > 180 ? 360 - d : d; };\n    56\t\n    57\texport const CVD = {\n    58\t  normal: [[1, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0], [0, 0, 1]],\n    59\t  protan: [[0.152286, 1.052583, -0.204868], [0.114503, 0.786281, 0.099216], [-0.003882, -0.048116, 1.051998]],\n    60\t  deutan: [[0.367322, 0.860646, -0.227968], [0.280085, 0.672501, 0.047413], [-0.011820, 0.042940, 0.968881]],\n    61\t  tritan: [[1.255528, -0.076749, -0.178779], [-0.078411, 0.930809, 0.147602], [0.004733, 0.691367, 0.303900]],\n    62\t};\n    63\texport const VISIONS = Object.keys(CVD);\n    64\t\n    65\t/** Linear RGB as seen under a vision (clamped), for previews and grayscale. */\n    66\texport const simulateLinear = (rgbLin, vision) => CVD[vision].map(row => clamp01(row[0] * rgbLin[0] + row[1] * rgbLin[1] + row[2] * rgbLin[2]));\n    67\t/** OKLab of a hex color under a vision. */\n    68\texport const labUnder = (hex, vision) => oklab(simulateLinear(linear(hex), vision));\n    69\texport const dist = (p, q) => Math.hypot(p[0] - q[0], p[1] - q[1], p[2] - q[2]);\n    70\texport const distUnder = (h1, h2, vision) => dist(labUnder(h1, vision), labUnder(h2, vision));\n    71\t\n    72\t/** Source-over compositing of `hex` at `alpha` over an opaque background, in gamma-encoded sRGB as browsers do. */\n    73\texport function compositeHex(hex, alpha, bgHex) {\n    74\t  const f = srgb(hex), b = srgb(bgHex);\n    75\t  return toHex(f.map((v, i) => v * alpha + b[i] * (1 - alpha)));\n    76\t}\n    77\t/** The same without rounding to 8 bits, so measurements do not depend on the rounding of the composite. */\n    78\texport function compositeLinear(hex, alpha, bgHex) {\n    79\t  const f = srgb(hex), b = srgb(bgHex);\n    80\t  return f.map((v, i) => lin(v * alpha + b[i] * (1 - alpha)));\n    81\t}\n    82\t\n    83\t// --- 8-bit output. The analytic measures above work on unrounded values; what a screen shows is bytes.\n    84\t\n    85\texport const bytes = hex => [1, 3, 5].map(i => parseInt(hex.slice(i, i + 2), 16));\n    86\texport const bytesToHex = b => '#' + b.map(v => v.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');\n    87\texport const labOfBytes = b => oklab(b.map(v => lin(v / 255)));\n    88\t\n    89\t/** A simulated color as an 8-bit image holds it: simulate in linear RGB, clamp, encode, round to the nearest byte. */\n    90\texport const simulateBytes = (hex, vision) => simulateLinear(linear(hex), vision).map(v => Math.round(255 * gam(v)));\n    91\t/** OKLab of the byte-rounded simulation. */\n    92\texport const labUnderBytes = (hex, vision) => labOfBytes(simulateBytes(hex, vision));\n    93\t\n    94\t/**\n    95\t * Byte model of a translucent fill on an 8-bit raster: alpha quantised to `quantise(alpha × 255)`, each channel\n    96\t * rounded. With Math.round it reproduces Chrome 157's canvas and SVG fills on white exactly and is within one byte\n    97\t * on #16181d (raster.mjs); the CPU Skia raster of the review of 51e1be5 quantised alpha down (63 at 25%), which\n    98\t * Math.floor models. The scoring uses both.\n    99\t */\n   100\texport function rasterFill(hex, alpha, bgHex, quantise = Math.round) {\n   101\t  const k = quantise(alpha * 255), f = bytes(hex), b = bytes(bgHex);\n   102\t  return f.map((v, i) => Math.round((v * k + b[i] * (255 - k)) / 255));\n   103\t}\n   104\t\n   105\t/** The lattice of byte colors within ±r of `b` per channel (clamped to 0–255). */\n   106\texport function byteBox(b, r = 1) {\n   107\t  const out = [];\n   108\t  for (let i = -r; i <= r; i++) for (let j = -r; j <= r; j++) for (let k = -r; k <= r; k++) {\n   109\t    out.push([b[0] + i, b[1] + j, b[2] + k].map(v => Math.min(255, Math.max(0, v))));\n   110\t  }\n   111\t  return out;\n   112\t}\n   113\t\n   114\t/** Smallest OKLab distance between any byte color within ±r of `a` and any within ±r of `b`: the worst case of rounding. */\n   115\texport function boxDistance(a, b, r = 1) {\n   116\t  const la = byteBox(a, r).map(labOfBytes), lb = byteBox(b, r).map(labOfBytes);\n   117\t  let m = Infinity;\n   118\t  for (const p of la) for (const q of lb) { const d = dist(p, q); if (d < m) m = d; }\n   119\t  return m;\n   120\t}\n   121\t\n   122\t/** Smallest pairwise OKLab distance under one vision, with the pair. `labOf` picks analytic or byte-rounded. */\n   123\texport function minPair(colors, vision, labOf = labUnder) {\n   124\t  const labs = colors.map(c => labOf(c, vision));\n   125\t  let min = Infinity, pair = null;\n   126\t  for (let i = 0; i < labs.length; i++) for (let j = i + 1; j < labs.length; j++) {\n   127\t    const d = dist(labs[i], labs[j]);\n   128\t    if (d < min) { min = d; pair = [i, j]; }\n   129\t  }\n   130\t  return { min, pair };\n   131\t}\n   132\t\n   133\t/** Adjacent pairs of a stacked area or donut with `n` parts: k and k+1, and the closing pair (n, 1) of a ring. */\n   134\texport function neighbourPairs(n) {\n   135\t  const pairs = [];\n   136\t  for (let k = 0; k + 1 < n; k++) pairs.push([k, k + 1]);\n   137\t  return pairs;\n   138\t}\n   139\texport function ringClosures(n) {\n   140\t  const pairs = [];\n   141\t  for (let m = 3; m <= n; m++) pairs.push([m - 1, 0]);\n   142\t  return pairs;\n   143\t}\n   144\t\n   145\t// Tokens no series color may come within THRESHOLDS.token of: focus and reference (constraint 5) and muted,\n   146\t// which paints the donut's \"Other\" slice beside series slices.\n   147\texport const TOKEN_NAMES = ['focus', 'reference', 'muted'];\n   148\t\n   149\t// box: 1 also requires the fill neighbours at the worst case within one byte per channel of the model; 0 (the\n   150\t// default, what the shipped palette meets) records that worst case without requiring it. No palette meeting it was\n   151\t// found together with the decision's other goals (decision 0003, \"Tried and failed\").\n   152\texport const OPTIONS = { box: 0 };\n   153\t\n   154\texport const THRESHOLDS = {\n   155\t  contrast: 3,\n   156\t  minDelta: 0.10,\n   157\t  prefix4: 0.14,\n   158\t  hueMatch: 30,\n   159\t  token: 0.10,\n   160\t  composite: 0.06,\n   161\t  alphas: [0.25, 0.8],\n   162\t  prefixes: [3, 4, 5, 6],\n   163\t};\n   164\t\n   165\t/**\n   166\t * Every measured quantity of constraints 1–6 for one scheme. `scheme` is { background, focus, reference, series }.\n   167\t * `other` is the other scheme, for the hue match (constraint 4), optional.\n   168\t */\n   169\texport function measureScheme(scheme, other) {\n   170\t  const { background: bg, series } = scheme;\n   171\t  const contrastBy = series.map(c => contrast(c, bg));\n   172\t  const full = Object.fromEntries(VISIONS.map(v => [v, minPair(series, v)]));\n   173\t  const prefixes = Object.fromEntries(THRESHOLDS.prefixes.map(n => [n, Object.fromEntries(VISIONS.map(v => [v, minPair(series.slice(0, n), v)]))]));\n   174\t  const tokens = {};\n   175\t  for (const t of TOKEN_NAMES) {\n   176\t    const d = series.map(c => distUnder(c, scheme[t], 'normal'));\n   177\t    const k = d.indexOf(Math.min(...d));\n   178\t    tokens[t] = { min: d[k], slot: k };\n   179\t  }\n   180\t  const composites = {};\n   181\t  for (const alpha of THRESHOLDS.alphas) {\n   182\t    const labs = Object.fromEntries(VISIONS.map(v => [v, series.map(c => oklab(simulateLinear(compositeLinear(c, alpha, bg), v)))]));\n   183\t    const at = (pairs, v) => pairs.map(([i, j]) => ({ pair: [i, j], d: dist(labs[v][i], labs[v][j]) }));\n   184\t    composites[alpha] = Object.fromEntries(VISIONS.map(v => {\n   185\t      const nb = at(neighbourPairs(series.length), v), ring = at(ringClosures(series.length), v);\n   186\t      const lo = list => list.reduce((m, x) => (x.d < m.d ? x : m), { d: Infinity, pair: null });\n   187\t      return [v, { neighbours: nb, ring, minNeighbour: lo(nb), minRing: lo(ring) }];\n   188\t    }));\n   189\t  }\n   190\t  // The same distances after the simulated colors are rounded to bytes (what a simulated screenshot holds).\n   191\t  const fullBytes = Object.fromEntries(VISIONS.map(v => [v, minPair(series, v, labUnderBytes)]));\n   192\t  const prefixesBytes = Object.fromEntries(THRESHOLDS.prefixes.map(n => [n, Object.fromEntries(VISIONS.map(v => [v, minPair(series.slice(0, n), v, labUnderBytes)]))]));\n   193\t  // Fills on an 8-bit raster: the byte model, and the worst case within one byte of it.\n   194\t  const raster = {};\n   195\t  for (const alpha of THRESHOLDS.alphas) {\n   196\t    const px = series.map(c => rasterFill(c, alpha, bg)), pxFloor = series.map(c => rasterFill(c, alpha, bg, Math.floor));\n   197\t    const nb = neighbourPairs(series.length).map(([i, j]) => ({ pair: [i, j], model: dist(labOfBytes(px[i]), labOfBytes(px[j])), modelFloor: dist(labOfBytes(pxFloor[i]), labOfBytes(pxFloor[j])), worst: boxDistance(px[i], px[j]) }));\n   198\t    raster[alpha] = { pixels: px.map(bytesToHex), pixelsFloor: pxFloor.map(bytesToHex), neighbours: nb, minModel: Math.min(...nb.map(x => Math.min(x.model, x.modelFloor))), minWorst: Math.min(...nb.map(x => x.worst)) };\n   199\t  }\n   200\t  const hue = other ? series.map((c, k) => hueDiff(lch(c)[2], lch(other.series[k])[2])) : null;\n   201\t  return { contrast: contrastBy, full, prefixes, tokens, composites, fullBytes, prefixesBytes, raster, hue };\n   202\t}\n   203\t\n   204\t/** The hard constraints as a list of { id, ok, value, limit, label } for one scheme. */\n   205\texport function checkScheme(scheme, other) {\n   206\t  const m = measureScheme(scheme, other), T = THRESHOLDS, out = [];\n   207\t  out.push({ id: 1, label: 'lowest contrast on background', value: Math.min(...m.contrast), limit: T.contrast });\n   208\t  for (const v of VISIONS) out.push({ id: 2, label: `min pairwise ΔE (all 8), ${v}`, value: m.full[v].min, limit: T.minDelta });\n   209\t  for (const n of T.prefixes) for (const v of VISIONS) out.push({ id: 3, label: `min pairwise ΔE (first ${n}), ${v}`, value: m.prefixes[n][v].min, limit: n === 4 ? T.prefix4 : T.minDelta });\n   210\t  if (m.hue) out.push({ id: 4, label: 'largest light/dark hue difference per slot (°)', value: Math.max(...m.hue), limit: T.hueMatch, upper: true });\n   211\t  for (const t of TOKEN_NAMES) out.push({ id: 5, label: `nearest series to ${t} token`, value: m.tokens[t].min, limit: T.token });\n   212\t  for (const a of T.alphas) out.push({ id: 6, label: `min neighbour ΔE at ${a * 100}% opacity, normal`, value: m.composites[a].normal.minNeighbour.d, limit: T.composite });\n   213\t  for (const v of VISIONS) out.push({ id: '2b', label: `min pairwise ΔE (all 8), ${v}, simulated then rounded to bytes`, value: m.fullBytes[v].min, limit: T.minDelta });\n   214\t  for (const n of T.prefixes) for (const v of VISIONS) out.push({ id: '3b', label: `min pairwise ΔE (first ${n}), ${v}, rounded to bytes`, value: m.prefixesBytes[n][v].min, limit: n === 4 ? T.prefix4 : T.minDelta });\n   215\t  for (const a of T.alphas) out.push({ id: '6b', label: `min neighbour ΔE at ${a * 100}%, 8-bit raster models (alpha rounded and floored)`, value: m.raster[a].minModel, limit: T.composite });\n   216\t  if (OPTIONS.box) for (const a of T.alphas) out.push({ id: '6b', label: `min neighbour ΔE at ${a * 100}%, worst case within one byte`, value: m.raster[a].minWorst, limit: T.composite });\n   217\t  for (const c of out) c.ok = c.upper ? c.value <= c.limit : c.value >= c.limit;\n   218\t  return { checks: out, measured: m };\n   219\t}\n     1\t// Feasibility search over wide boxes (decision 0003). fit.mjs's branch and bound is exact but finds nothing in\n     2\t// reasonable time when the feasible region is small and far from the targets; this script searches the same\n     3\t// grids (fit.mjs `candidates`) with simulated annealing on the amount of violation, so it can find palettes the\n     4\t// exact search cannot reach, but it proves nothing. Its output is meant to be polished: a small box around each\n     5\t// color, solved to completion by fit.mjs (see configs/fit-final.json).\n     6\t//\n     7\t// State: one grid color per slot. Score: 100 × total violation + target cost, where violation sums, over every pair\n     8\t// and vision, how far a difference falls short of its limit (analytic and after byte rounding), and over neighbours\n     9\t// how far the fills fall short (ideal, and worst case within one byte of the 8-bit model). Moves re-pick one slot.\n    10\t//\n    11\t//   node research/palette/anneal.mjs --config=configs/x.json [--scheme=light] [--light=#…,…] [--seed=1] [--seeds=4]\n    12\t//        [--steps=400000] [--out=file.json]\n    13\timport fs from 'node:fs';\n    14\timport path from 'node:path';\n    15\timport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n    16\timport { THRESHOLDS as T, VISIONS, dist, lch } from './color.mjs';\n    17\timport { OPTIONS, SCHEME_TOKENS, candidates } from './fit.mjs';\n    18\t\n    19\tconst here = path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\n    20\tconst args = Object.fromEntries(process.argv.slice(2).map(a => a.replace(/^--/, '').split('=')).map(([k, v]) => [k, v ?? 'true']));\n    21\tconst cfg = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(args.config, 'utf8'));\n    22\tconst scheme = args.scheme ?? 'light';\n    23\tif (args.box !== undefined) OPTIONS.box = +args.box;   // --box=0: fills on the byte model only (what-if)\n    24\tconst tokens = { ...SCHEME_TOKENS[scheme], ...(cfg.tokens?.[scheme] ?? {}) };\n    25\tconst hueClip = +(args.hueClip ?? cfg.hueClip ?? T.hueMatch - 2);\n    26\tconst lightSet = args.light ? args.light.split(',') : null;\n    27\tif (scheme === 'dark' && !lightSet) throw new Error('--light=#…,… is required for the dark scheme');\n    28\tconst clips = lightSet ? lightSet.map((h, k) => ({ h: lch(h)[2], max: cfg.slots[k].neutral ? T.hueMatch - 2 : hueClip })) : null;\n    29\t// A neutral's hue is barely defined, so its dark box is centred on the light result's hue rather than its own target.\n    30\tconst centred = (s, k) => (s.neutral && clips?.[k] ? { target: [s[scheme].target[0], s[scheme].target[1], clips[k].h] } : {});\n    31\tconst domains = cfg.slots.map((s, k) => candidates(k, { ...s[scheme], neutral: !!s.neutral, ...centred(s, k) }, tokens, clips?.[k]));\n    32\t// --start=#…,… begins from a given palette (each color is added to its slot's grid, priced by that slot's target);\n    33\t// with it, the walk keeps every accepted state feasible once it is, so it moves along the feasible region.\n    34\tconst start = args.start ? args.start.split(',') : null;\n    35\tconst startIndex = start ? start.map((hex, k) => {\n    36\t  const [l, c, h] = lch(hex), box = cfg.slots[k][scheme];\n    37\t  const one = candidates(k, { ...box, neutral: !!cfg.slots[k].neutral, L: [l, l], C: [c, c], hr: 0, target: box.target, step: [1, 1, 1], at: h }, tokens, null, hex);\n    38\t  if (!one.length) throw new Error(`start color ${hex} fails on its own (contrast or tokens)`);\n    39\t  domains[k].push(one[0]);\n    40\t  return domains[k].length - 1;\n    41\t}) : null;\n    42\tconsole.log(scheme, 'domains', domains.map(d => d.length).join(' '));\n    43\tconst n = domains.length;\n    44\t\n    45\tconst short = (d, limit) => (d < limit ? limit - d : 0);\n    46\tfunction fillShort(fa, fb) {\n    47\t  const c = dist(fa.centre, fb.centre);\n    48\t  let s = short(dist(fa.ideal, fb.ideal), T.composite) + short(c, T.composite) + short(dist(fa.floor, fb.floor), T.composite);\n    49\t  if (!OPTIONS.box || c - fa.radius - fb.radius >= T.composite) return s;\n    50\t  let m = Infinity;\n    51\t  for (const p of fa.box) for (const q of fb.box) { const d = dist(p, q); if (d < m) m = d; }\n    52\t  return s + short(m, T.composite);\n    53\t}\n    54\tfunction violation(i, a, j, b) {\n    55\t  const limit = i < 4 && j < 4 ? T.prefix4 : T.minDelta;\n    56\t  let s = 0;\n    57\t  for (let v = 0; v < VISIONS.length; v++) s += short(dist(a.labs[v], b.labs[v]), limit) + short(dist(a.labsBytes[v], b.labsBytes[v]), limit);\n    58\t  if (Math.abs(i - j) === 1) for (let k = 0; k < T.alphas.length; k++) s += fillShort(a.fills[k], b.fills[k]);\n    59\t  return s;\n    60\t}\n    61\tfunction rng(seed) { let s = seed >>> 0; return () => { s = (s + 0x6D2B79F5) | 0; let t = Math.imul(s ^ (s >>> 15), 1 | s); t = (t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), 61 | t)) ^ t; return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296; }; }\n    62\t\n    63\tfunction run(seed, steps) {\n    64\t  const r = rng(seed);\n    65\t  const pick = k => domains[k][Math.floor(r() * domains[k].length)];\n    66\t  let state = startIndex ? startIndex.map((i, k) => domains[k][i]) : domains.map((d, k) => pick(k));\n    67\t  const V = Array.from({ length: n }, () => new Array(n).fill(0));\n    68\t  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) for (let j = i + 1; j < n; j++) V[i][j] = V[j][i] = violation(i, state[i], j, state[j]);\n    69\t  let viol = 0; for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) for (let j = i + 1; j < n; j++) viol += V[i][j];\n    70\t  let cost = state.reduce((a, x) => a + x.cost, 0);\n    71\t  let best = null;\n    72\t  for (let step = 0; step < steps; step++) {\n    73\t    const Tm = 2 * Math.pow(1e-3, step / steps);\n    74\t    const k = Math.floor(r() * n), x = pick(k);\n    75\t    let dv = 0; const row = new Array(n).fill(0);\n    76\t    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) if (j !== k) { row[j] = violation(k, x, j, state[j]); dv += row[j] - V[k][j]; }\n    77\t    const dc = x.cost - state[k].cost, dScore = 100 * dv + dc;\n    78\t    if (startIndex && viol < 1e-12 && viol + dv > 1e-12) continue;   // stay feasible once feasible\n    79\t    if (dScore <= 0 || r() < Math.exp(-dScore / Tm)) {\n    80\t      state[k] = x; viol += dv; cost += dc;\n    81\t      for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) if (j !== k) V[k][j] = V[j][k] = row[j];\n    82\t      if (viol < 1e-12 && (!best || cost < best.cost)) best = { cost, hex: state.map(s => s.hex) };\n    83\t    }\n    84\t  }\n    85\t  const worst = [];\n    86\t  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) for (let j = i + 1; j < n; j++) if (V[i][j] > 0) worst.push(`${i + 1}-${j + 1}:${V[i][j].toFixed(4)}`);\n    87\t  return { best, finalViolation: viol, worst: worst.join(' '), final: state.map(s => s.hex) };\n    88\t}\n    89\t\n    90\tconst seeds = +(args.seeds ?? 4), steps = +(args.steps ?? 400000), first = +(args.seed ?? 1), results = [];\n    91\tfor (let s = first; s < first + seeds; s++) {\n    92\t  const { best, finalViolation, worst, final } = run(s * 7919 + 13, steps);\n    93\t  console.log('seed', s, best ? `feasible, cost ${best.cost.toFixed(2)}: ${best.hex.join(' ')}` : `no feasible palette (final violation ${finalViolation.toFixed(4)}; short pairs ${worst}; ${final.join(' ')})`);\n    94\t  if (best) results.push({ seed: s, ...best });\n    95\t}\n    96\tresults.sort((a, b) => a.cost - b.cost);\n    97\tconst out = { config: path.basename(args.config, '.json'), scheme, light: lightSet, results };\n    98\tfs.writeFileSync(args.out ?? path.join(here, 'candidates', `anneal-${out.config}-${scheme}.json`), JSON.stringify(out, null, 1) + '\\n');\n     1\t// Which hue families, and which orders of them, can meet the hard constraints of decision 0003 at all?\n     2\t// Uses the grid and branch-and-bound search of fit.mjs on the light scheme, which is the tighter one.\n     3\t//\n     4\t//   node research/palette/explore.mjs sets [--part=0 --parts=1]\n     5\t//       Every set of eight families (blue plus seven of POOL) that has an orange, a purple, a green or teal and a\n     6\t//       red, pink or magenta: can the eight be placed at least 0.10 apart under all four visions, with 3:1 on white\n     7\t//       and clear of the tokens? (No order yet: no prefix or neighbour constraints.)\n     8\t//   node research/palette/explore.mjs orders --set=blue,orange,... [--part=0 --parts=1 --seconds=1]\n     9\t//       Every order of a set with blue first. Orders whose first four are proved unable to reach 0.14 are pruned\n    10\t//       (a timed-out first-four check prunes nothing); the others get the full search (prefixes and neighbour\n    11\t//       fills included) with a time limit.\n    12\t//\n    13\t// Prints one line per set or order, with one of three outcomes: OK with the cost and colors of the cheapest\n    14\t// palette found, \"no\" when the search proved that none exists on the grid, \"??\" when it ran out of time without\n    15\t// either. A summary of the three counts goes to stderr.\n    16\timport { candidates, compatible, search, SCHEME_TOKENS } from './fit.mjs';\n    17\timport { THRESHOLDS as T, dist } from './color.mjs';\n    18\t\n    19\tconst args = Object.fromEntries(process.argv.slice(3).map(a => a.replace(/^--/, '').split('=')).map(([k, v]) => [k, v ?? 'true']));\n    20\tconst mode = process.argv[2], part = +(args.part ?? 0), parts = +(args.parts ?? 1);\n    21\tconst tokens = SCHEME_TOKENS.light, LW = [0.38, 0.70];\n    22\t\n    23\t// Target and box per family, light scheme. Targets are where a designer would put the color if nothing else\n    24\t// mattered; the boxes are wide so that the search, not the target, decides what is possible.\n    25\texport const POOL = {\n    26\t  blue: { target: [0.63, 0.14, 252], L: [0.58, 0.70], C: [0.10, 0.17], hr: 10 },\n    27\t  orange: { target: [0.64, 0.15, 52], L: LW, C: [0.10, 0.18], hr: 10 },\n    28\t  green: { target: [0.50, 0.12, 145], L: LW, C: [0.08, 0.16], hr: 14 },\n    29\t  teal: { target: [0.55, 0.10, 185], L: LW, C: [0.07, 0.14], hr: 15 },\n    30\t  cyan: { target: [0.60, 0.11, 222], L: LW, C: [0.07, 0.15], hr: 12 },\n    31\t  red: { target: [0.55, 0.15, 25], L: LW, C: [0.10, 0.18], hr: 10 },\n    32\t  pink: { target: [0.60, 0.14, 0], L: LW, C: [0.09, 0.17], hr: 10 },\n    33\t  magenta: { target: [0.50, 0.15, 335], L: LW, C: [0.10, 0.18], hr: 12 },\n    34\t  purple: { target: [0.52, 0.15, 300], L: LW, C: [0.10, 0.18], hr: 12 },\n    35\t  gold: { target: [0.62, 0.12, 82], L: LW, C: [0.07, 0.15], hr: 12 },\n    36\t  brown: { target: [0.48, 0.09, 58], L: LW, C: [0.05, 0.12], hr: 14 },\n    37\t  slate: { target: [0.60, 0.03, 255], L: LW, C: [0.0, 0.045], hr: 35, neutral: true },\n    38\t};\n    39\tconst domain = name => candidates(0, { ...POOL[name], step: [0.01, 0.01, 3] }, tokens, null);\n    40\t// At least `limit` apart under all four visions, analytically and after rounding the simulation to bytes.\n    41\tconst apart = (a, b, limit) => { for (let v = 0; v < 4; v++) if (dist(a.labs[v], b.labs[v]) < limit || dist(a.labsBytes[v], b.labsBytes[v]) < limit) return false; return true; };\n    42\tconst line = (r, names) => console.log(r.best ? 'OK ' : (r.complete ? 'no ' : '?? '), r.best ? r.cost.toFixed(2).padStart(6) : '     -', names.join(','), r.best ? r.best.map(x => x.hex).join(' ') : '', r.best && !r.complete ? '(best within time limit)' : '');\n    43\t\n    44\tif (mode === 'sets') {\n    45\t  const doms = Object.fromEntries(Object.keys(POOL).map(k => [k, domain(k)]));\n    46\t  const uniform = (i, a, j, b) => apart(a, b, T.minDelta);\n    47\t  const others = Object.keys(POOL).filter(k => k !== 'blue'), combos = [];\n    48\t  const pick = (start, acc) => { if (acc.length === 7) { combos.push(acc.slice()); return; } for (let i = start; i < others.length; i++) { acc.push(others[i]); pick(i + 1, acc); acc.pop(); } };\n    49\t  pick(0, []);\n    50\t  const required = c => c.includes('orange') && c.includes('purple') && (c.includes('green') || c.includes('teal')) && (c.includes('red') || c.includes('pink') || c.includes('magenta'));\n    51\t  combos.filter(required).forEach((c, i) => {\n    52\t    if (i % parts !== part) return;\n    53\t    const fams = ['blue', ...c];\n    54\t    line(search(fams.map(f => doms[f]), +(args.seconds ?? 3), uniform), fams);\n    55\t  });\n    56\t} else if (mode === 'orders') {\n    57\t  const fams = args.set.split(','), doms = Object.fromEntries(fams.map(k => [k, domain(k)]));\n    58\t  const four = (i, a, j, b) => apart(a, b, T.prefix4);\n    59\t  // First-four outcome per set of four families: 'feasible', 'infeasible' (search completed without a palette) or\n    60\t  // 'unknown' (timed out without one). Only a completed proof prunes the orders that start with that set.\n    61\t  const firstFour = new Map(), perms = [], counts = { OK: 0, no: 0, '??': 0, pruned: 0 };\n    62\t  const permute = (a, k = 0) => { if (k === a.length) { perms.push(a.slice()); return; } for (let i = k; i < a.length; i++) { [a[k], a[i]] = [a[i], a[k]]; permute(a, k + 1); [a[k], a[i]] = [a[i], a[k]]; } };\n    63\t  permute(fams.slice(1));\n    64\t  for (let p = part; p < perms.length; p += parts) {\n    65\t    const order = [fams[0], ...perms[p]], key = order.slice(0, 4).sort().join();\n    66\t    if (!firstFour.has(key)) {\n    67\t      const r = search(order.slice(0, 4).map(f => doms[f]), +(args.fourSeconds ?? 2), four);\n    68\t      firstFour.set(key, r.best ? 'feasible' : r.complete ? 'infeasible' : 'unknown');\n    69\t    }\n    70\t    if (firstFour.get(key) === 'infeasible') { counts.no++; counts.pruned++; console.log('no      -', order.join(','), '(first four proved infeasible)'); continue; }\n    71\t    const r = search(order.map(f => doms[f]), +(args.seconds ?? 1), compatible);\n    72\t    counts[r.best ? 'OK' : r.complete ? 'no' : '??']++;\n    73\t    line(r, order);\n    74\t  }\n    75\t  const by = o => [...firstFour].filter(([, v]) => v === o).map(([k]) => k).join(' | ') || '(none)';\n    76\t  console.error(`orders: ${counts.OK} feasible, ${counts.no} proved infeasible (${counts.pruned} by their first four), ${counts['??']} timed out`);\n    77\t  console.error(`first four feasible: ${by('feasible')}`);\n    78\t  console.error(`first four proved infeasible: ${by('infeasible')}`);\n    79\t  console.error(`first four timed out: ${by('unknown')}`);\n    80\t} else {\n    81\t  console.error('usage: explore.mjs sets | orders --set=a,b,...');\n    82\t  process.exitCode = 2;\n    83\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
