{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba bench/run.mjs | sed -n '220,400p'; nl -ba docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md | sed -n '1,135p'; nl -ba docs/04-plan-performance.md | sed -n '42,108p'\"","aggregated_output":"   220\tasync function main() {\n   221\t  const die = msg => { console.error(`run.mjs: ${msg}`); process.exit(2); };\n   222\t  let a;\n   223\t  try {\n   224\t    ({ values: a } = parseArgs({\n   225\t      options: {\n   226\t        baseline: { type: 'string' }, candidate: { type: 'string' }, aa: { type: 'string' }, adapter: { type: 'string' }, base: { type: 'string' },\n   227\t        workload: { type: 'string', default: 'first' }, size: { type: 'string' }, fixture: { type: 'string' }, gaps: { type: 'boolean', default: false }, clustered: { type: 'boolean', default: false },\n   228\t        charts: { type: 'string' }, window: { type: 'string' }, samples: { type: 'string' }, toggles: { type: 'string' }, moves: { type: 'string' }, cycles: { type: 'string' },\n   229\t        blocks: { type: 'string' }, settle: { type: 'string', default: '500' }, timeout: { type: 'string', default: '120000' },\n   230\t        seed: { type: 'string' }, 'bootstrap-seed': { type: 'string' }, dpr: { type: 'string', default: '1' }, headed: { type: 'boolean', default: false },\n   231\t        noise: { type: 'string' }, 'history-dir': { type: 'string' }, artifact: { type: 'string' }, source: { type: 'string' },\n   232\t        screen: { type: 'boolean', default: false }, tolerance: { type: 'string' }, 'allow-harness-difference': { type: 'boolean', default: false },\n   233\t        quiet: { type: 'boolean', default: false },\n   234\t      },\n   235\t    }));\n   236\t  } catch (e) { die(e.message); }\n   237\t  const workload = a.workload;\n   238\t  if (!WORKLOADS.includes(workload)) die(`unknown workload ${workload} (${WORKLOADS.join(', ')})`);\n   239\t  if (a.fixture && a.fixture !== 'trend') die(`unknown fixture ${a.fixture} (trend)`);\n   240\t  let params;\n   241\t  try { params = workloadParams(workload, a); } catch (e) { die(e.message); }\n   242\t  const blocks = a.blocks === undefined ? DEFAULT_BLOCKS : Number(a.blocks);\n   243\t  let seed, bootstrapSeed;\n   244\t  try {\n   245\t    seed = a.seed !== undefined ? parseSeed(a.seed, '--seed') : crypto.randomInt(1, 2 ** 31);\n   246\t    bootstrapSeed = a['bootstrap-seed'] !== undefined ? parseSeed(a['bootstrap-seed'], '--bootstrap-seed') : DEFAULT_BOOTSTRAP_SEED;\n   247\t    balancedOrder(blocks, seed);\n   248\t  } catch (e) { die(e.message); }\n   249\t  const settleMs = Number(a.settle), timeoutMs = Number(a.timeout), dpr = Number(a.dpr);\n   250\t  if (!(dpr === 1 || dpr === 2)) die(`--dpr must be 1 or 2, got ${a.dpr}`);\n   251\t  const historyDir = path.resolve(a['history-dir'] ?? path.join(BENCH, 'history'));\n   252\t\n   253\t  let mode, A, B, artifacts;\n   254\t  try {\n   255\t    if (a.base) {\n   256\t      if (!a.candidate || !a.adapter) die('two-build mode needs --adapter <ref> --base <artifact dir> --candidate <artifact dir>');\n   257\t      mode = 'builds';\n   258\t      parseAdapterRef(a.adapter);\n   259\t      A = { ref: a.adapter, slot: 'A' }; B = { ref: a.adapter, slot: 'B' };\n   260\t      artifacts = { A: readArtifact(a.base), B: readArtifact(a.candidate) };\n   261\t      if (artifacts.A.id === artifacts.B.id) die('--base and --candidate are the same artifact; use --aa for A-versus-A');\n   262\t      // Only the library may differ: a different page or adapter would confound the comparison.\n   263\t      const diff = Object.keys({ ...artifacts.A.files, ...artifacts.B.files }).filter(HARNESS).filter(p => artifacts.A.files[p]?.sha256 !== artifacts.B.files[p]?.sha256);\n   264\t      if (diff.length && !a['allow-harness-difference']) die(`the two artifacts differ in harness files, not only in the library: ${diff.join(', ')} (rebuild both with the same bench/, or pass --allow-harness-difference)`);\n   265\t    } else {\n   266\t      const aa = a.aa ?? null;\n   267\t      const baseline = aa ?? a.baseline, candidate = aa ?? a.candidate;\n   268\t      if (!baseline || !candidate) die('give --baseline and --candidate, or --aa <adapter>, or --adapter with --base and --candidate');\n   269\t      parseAdapterRef(baseline); parseAdapterRef(candidate);\n   270\t      mode = aa ? 'aa' : 'compare';\n   271\t      A = { ref: baseline, slot: 'A' }; B = { ref: candidate, slot: 'A' };\n   272\t      artifacts = { A: a.artifact ? readArtifact(a.artifact) : readArtifact((await build({ source: a.source })).dir) };\n   273\t      if (!a.artifact && !a.quiet) console.log(`built session artifact ${artifacts.A.dir}`);\n   274\t    }\n   275\t  } catch (e) { die(e.message); }\n   276\t  if (a.screen && mode !== 'builds') die('--screen applies to two-build mode (--base/--candidate)');\n   277\t  let tolerance = null;\n   278\t  if (a.screen) { tolerance = Number(a.tolerance); if (a.tolerance === undefined || !Number.isFinite(tolerance)) die('--screen needs --tolerance <largest regression tolerated, in the primary metric\\'s unit>; the plan has it declared per workload before measuring'); }\n   279\t\n   280\t  const cfg = { mode, A, B, artifacts, workload, params, blocks, seed, bootstrapSeed, settleMs, timeoutMs, dpr, headed: a.headed, noiseIds: a.noise ? a.noise.split(',') : null, historyDir, quiet: a.quiet, command: process.argv.slice(2) };\n   281\t  try {\n   282\t    const first = await runSession(cfg);\n   283\t    if (!a.screen) return;\n   284\t    const v1 = screeningVerdict(first, tolerance);\n   285\t    console.log(`screening, first job ${first.runId}: ${v1.exceeds ? 'worse than tolerance' : 'within tolerance or inconclusive'} (${v1.why})`);\n   286\t    if (!v1.exceeds) return;\n   287\t    const again = await runSession({ ...cfg, seed: crypto.randomInt(1, 2 ** 31), command: [...cfg.command, '(screening rerun)'] });\n   288\t    const v2 = screeningVerdict(again, tolerance);\n   289\t    const flagged = v2.exceeds;\n   290\t    console.log(`screening, rerun ${again.runId}: ${v2.exceeds ? 'worse than tolerance' : 'within tolerance or inconclusive'} (${v2.why})`);\n   291\t    console.log(flagged ? 'FLAGGED: worse than the tolerance in two jobs in a row' : 'not flagged: the rerun did not confirm the first job');\n   292\t    const screenFile = path.join(historyDir, `${again.runId}.screening.json`);\n   293\t    fs.writeFileSync(screenFile, JSON.stringify({ schema: 'axylo-bench-screening/1', tolerance, jobs: [first.runId, again.runId], verdicts: [v1, v2], flagged }, null, 1));\n   294\t    if (flagged) process.exitCode = 3;\n   295\t  } catch (e) {\n   296\t    console.error(e?.message ?? e);\n   297\t    process.exit(1);\n   298\t  }\n   299\t}\n   300\t\n   301\tif (process.argv[1] && path.resolve(process.argv[1]) === fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)) await main();\n     1\t# Decision 0002: SVG paths draw the marks by default (provisional)\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: **provisional working default**, set by the coordinator on 2026-11-18 from the M1 measurements and weakened the same day after an independent review of the benchmark harness (`reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/bench.review.md`, Codex gpt-6.2-sol at effort max). The owner asked for this to be settled by measurement. It is not settled: the measurements support using SVG while the line-chart slice is built and keeping canvas behind the internal switch. They do not establish a shipping winner or a reason to remove either renderer.\n     4\t\n     5\t## What the review changed\n     6\t\n     7\tThe table below is kept as measured. These statements made from it in the first version are withdrawn or narrowed:\n     8\t\n     9\t- **Withdrawn: \"nothing measured favours canvas except all-thread CPU during resize.\"** The recorded heap, DOM-node and layout counts also favour canvas.\n    10\t- **Withdrawn: the resize CPU gap is raster work.** The number is CPU for the whole renderer process; no stage was traced.\n    11\t- **Withdrawn: simplification bounds every chart by its width.** It was shown for line series only; point marks and many-series charts were not measured.\n    12\t- **Withdrawn: an SVG theme change needs no run-time work.** Both renderers recorded six DOM mutations per toggle; \"no canvas drawing call\" is all that was observed.\n    13\t- **Withdrawn: normalization and garbage collection explain the gap to uPlot.** About 71–73% of busy time in the production profiles is unmapped; the normalization share comes from development builds. It is a hypothesis to test, not a finding.\n    14\t- **Narrowed: \"half the renderer memory.\"** The halving is of the increase above a data-only page at pixel ratio 2 (34.2 → 17.7 MiB). Absolute renderer memory was 74.7 → 56.2 MiB, and at pixel ratio 1 the increase was 23.1 → 18.4 MiB.\n    15\t- **Narrowed: \"no frame later than 25 ms.\"** True of the recorded animation-frame callbacks; the window can miss an interval that spans its boundary, and a callback is not a presented frame.\n    16\t- **Void: every 24-chart page row** (page at pixel ratio 1 and 2, resize of the 24-chart page, and the memory row). The harness fix pass found that these runs drew no marks: at 230 × 115 px each tile is below the 120 × 80 px plot minimum of spec L8, so every chart showed its text fallback. The SVG records carry `svgPaths: 0`; the canvas runs held empty canvases. The numbers compare two empty charts and say nothing about the renderers. The harness now rejects a run whose chart drew no marks, and the page workload uses 340 × 200 px tiles.\n    17\t- **Narrowed: verdicts.** 149 metric comparisons were judged without adjustment for choosing among them, on six pairs or fewer per workload, with noise bands from one session and two workload shapes. Two \"SVG lower\" rows allow a gain as small as 0.16 ms (10,000 points) and 6.3 ms (dashboard at pixel ratio 2).\n    18\t\n    19\tEvidence: [bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md](../../bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md). One session of 39 runs, production build, headless Chrome 157, machine under load from other work, A-versus-A noise bands for two workloads only.\n    20\t\n    21\t## What was measured\n    22\t\n    23\tCanvas → SVG, medians of main-thread task time unless stated. \"Lower\" means the 95% interval cleared zero and the noise band where one existed.\n    24\t\n    25\t| Workload | Canvas | SVG | Verdict |\n    26\t|---|---|---|---|\n    27\t| Streaming, per update | 2.26 ms | 1.45 ms | SVG lower |\n    28\t| Theme change, per toggle | 2.68 ms and 27 drawing calls | 2.01 ms and no drawing call | SVG lower |\n    29\t| Resize, one chart of 100,000 points | 915 ms | 472 ms | SVG lower on the main thread |\n    30\t| Same, CPU across all threads | 1,035 ms | 1,770 ms | **SVG higher** |\n    31\t| Resize, 24-chart page | 730 ms | 525 ms | Void: no marks were drawn |\n    32\t| First chart, 10,000 points | 34.0 ms | 28.4 ms | SVG lower |\n    33\t| 24-chart page at pixel ratio 2 | 199 ms | 158 ms | Void: no marks were drawn |\n    34\t| First chart, 3 × 120 | 23.6 ms | 19.8 ms | Inconclusive |\n    35\t| First chart, 100,000 points | 81.8 ms | 83.5 ms | Inconclusive |\n    36\t| First chart, 1,000,000 points | 622 ms | 652 ms | Inconclusive (4 blocks) |\n    37\t| Memory, 24-chart page at pixel ratio 2 | 34.2 MiB renderer, 11.5 MiB GPU, 7.7 MiB backing stores | 17.7 MiB, 2.2 MiB, none | Void: no marks were drawn |\n    38\t\n    39\tNo frame was later than 25 ms in any configuration, so frame pacing does not separate the two.\n    40\t\n    41\t## Why SVG for now\n    42\t\n    43\t1. On the line workloads measured, main-thread task time was lower with SVG or the two could not be told apart.\n    44\t2. Line series are simplified to a few vertices per pixel column before either renderer sees them, so for line charts the path size follows the plot width, not the data.\n    45\t3. SVG follows the page with less code: forced colors and print work through CSS, browser zoom stays sharp, and there is no backing store to size, cap or lose. This is a design argument, not a measurement.\n    46\t\n    47\t## What this does not establish\n    48\t\n    49\t- **GPU rasterization was not measured.** Headless Chrome here does not use a GPU. The resize, pixel-ratio-2 and process-memory results need a headed, GPU-backed rerun before they transfer.\n    50\t- **Dense point marks were not measured.** A scatter chart with tens of thousands of markers is the case most likely to favour canvas.\n    51\t- **One browser, one machine, one session.** Firefox and Safari may order the two differently.\n    52\t- **Many series, pan and zoom, and the budget workloads P4, P7 and P8 were not measured**, and the million-point comparison has four pairs.\n    53\t- Both renderers are far from uPlot in the configurations measured (about 80 ms against 12 ms at 100,000 points; uPlot draws no tooltip or accessibility layer). Where that time goes is not established.\n    54\t\n    55\t## Consequences\n    56\t\n    57\t- The working default is SVG. The canvas renderer stays in the code behind the same internal switch, with the pixel-agreement tests between the two kept.\n    58\t- Before the choice is final: fix the harness defects the review found (renderer identity in the readback, noise bands on replay, failed runs excluded from speed verdicts, gap-aware admission, the frame window), declare the primary endpoints and thresholds in advance, then rerun on a quiet machine with scatter at 10,000, 50,000 and 200,000 points, ten series of 100,000 points, pan and zoom, and the byte cost of keeping both renderers. Removing canvas from the bundle needs that evidence too.\n    59\t- When a real GPU and a second browser are available: rerun the resize and million-point workloads headed. If canvas wins there by more than the shipping thresholds in the performance plan, flip the switch back.\n    60\t- The plan's sections 2.2 and 2.3 are read with SVG in the \"marks\" position: a theme change no longer needs a redraw, and device pixel ratio matters only to simplification.\n    42\t\n    43\tEvery workload has one primary metric with a defined start, a defined end state that can be verified, and a statistic.\n    44\t\n    45\t| Metric | Start | End | How |\n    46\t|---|---|---|---|\n    47\t| Time to first correct chart | The create call | The first presented frame in which the chart shows the fixture's data: readiness is signalled by the adapter (Axylo exposes a revision counter for this in benchmark builds), and the signal is validated on sampled runs against a screenshot compared with a reference | `performance.mark`, a `requestAnimationFrame` after readiness; screenshot sampling in separate, non-timed runs |\n    48\t| Main-thread task time | Same | A fixed settle window after readiness | Chrome DevTools Protocol `Performance.getMetrics` (task, script, layout and style durations). This counts work wherever the library schedules it: synchronous, microtask, timer or animation frame |\n    49\t| Update cost when streaming | First append | Last append displayed | Main-thread task time divided by updates; plus, per sequence number, the delay until the frame that displays it, the backlog, and the count of coalesced updates |\n    50\t| Frame pacing | – | – | Intervals between animation-frame callbacks, summarized per run: 95th and 99th percentile, and the count over 1.5 refresh intervals (25 ms at 60 Hz, 12.5 ms at 120 Hz). The refresh rate is recorded |\n    51\t| Input to display | A real pointer or key event dispatched at a known datum, timestamped | The first presented frame in which the readout shows that datum | A lightweight hook in benchmark builds, validated against Chrome traces on sampled runs. The Event Timing API is not used for this: it does not report `pointermove` or `wheel` |\n    52\t| Long stalls | – | – | Long Animation Frames entries, which report frames of 50 ms and more. Used for stalls only, not for the one-frame budgets |\n    53\t| GPU and raster time | – | – | Chrome tracing on selected headed runs |\n    54\t| Memory | – | – | Four separate numbers, never mixed: bytes in buffers Axylo owns (counted directly); JavaScript heap after forced collection (CDP); process memory including canvas backing stores (browser task manager figures via CDP where available); peak during a data replacement. Baselines: a blank page, and the page holding only the input data. Leaks: heap after 100, 300 and 1,000 create–destroy cycles following warm-up, gated on slope, plus a count of remaining listeners and observers |\n    55\t| Bytes | – | – | Minified, gzip and brotli size of each entry point from the packed distribution, CSS included; parse and compile time from a trace |\n    56\t\n    57\tWhere a browser lacks an instrument, the result file says \"not available\" for that browser. It is never filled with a number from another metric.\n    58\t\n    59\t### 3.2 Environment\n    60\t\n    61\t| Dimension | Values |\n    62\t|---|---|\n    63\t| Browsers | Chrome and Edge stable, Firefox stable, headed, with a real GPU. Safari on macOS by hand at releases. Playwright's WebKit build in CI, reported under its own name, not as Safari. |\n    64\t| Hardware profiles | \"Desktop\": the reference machine, recorded with CPU, GPU, OS, display refresh rate and power settings. \"Throttled\": 4× CPU throttling through CDP, Chromium only. \"Phone\": one real mid-range Android phone, used at baseline calibration and at releases, when the device is available; when it is not, the result says so. |\n    65\t| Display | Fixed 1,440×900 window; device pixel ratio 1 and 2. |\n    66\t| Build | Production build of every library; pinned versions; adapter and fixture hashes in the result file. |\n    67\t| Isolation | Fresh browser context per run; no extensions; machine otherwise idle. |\n    68\t| Runnable combinations | Desktop × {Chrome, Firefox, WebKit}; Throttled × Chrome; Phone × Chrome. Nothing else is claimed. |\n    69\t\n    70\tThe reference machine restarts often and has no permanent temporary storage, so every run writes its raw samples and environment record to `bench/history/` in the repository as it goes. A run cut short by a restart is kept, labelled interrupted, and never merged with a later one.\n    71\t\n    72\t### 3.3 Statistics\n    73\t\n    74\t- **Design:** baseline and candidate run in randomized, balanced blocks (AB and BA in equal numbers) inside one session, so drift and order effects fall on both.\n    75\t- **Unit of analysis:** the run. Frames inside one run share a browser, a thermal state and a history, so they are summarized per run before any interval is computed.\n    76\t- **Summary:** median of per-run values with a paired bootstrap 95% interval for the difference; tail and count metrics get their own intervals from the same per-run values.\n    77\t- **Noise:** an A-versus-A distribution, built from repeated blocks across at least three sessions, gives the noise band for each workload. A single A–A difference is not a noise floor; it can be near zero by chance.\n    78\t- **Sample sizes** are set from a pilot in M0 that measures each workload's variance and run time. Until then: 20 complete pairs for a verdict, that is 20 runs of each configuration. With fewer, the result is reported as exploratory, with no verdict. (The first harness used six pairs; a reviewer showed that six can give a confident answer in the wrong direction about one time in ten on a skewed metric.)\n    79\t- **Three outcomes:** better, worse, or inconclusive. \"No significant difference\" is reported as inconclusive, never as \"no regression\".\n    80\t- **Scope of a number:** a result holds for its commit, browser version and machine. Comparisons against stored history are valid only on the same machine and browser version; otherwise both sides are rebuilt and rerun together.\n    81\t\n    82\t### 3.4 Comparison libraries\n    83\t\n    84\t| Library | Configurations | Role |\n    85\t|---|---|---|\n    86\t| uPlot | Default | Speed reference. |\n    87\t| Chart.js | Starter default; feature-equivalent tuned | The most deployed. |\n    88\t| ECharts | Starter default; feature-equivalent tuned | Canvas with built-in large-data modes. |\n    89\t| Highcharts | Default; Boost | SVG with a WebGL path; evaluation use only, subject to its license terms for publishing benchmarks. |\n    90\t| Recharts | Starter default; tuned | What most React users have. |\n    91\t| Nivo | SVG line; Canvas line | A node-per-point library with a canvas alternative. |\n    92\t| D3 | One SVG path per series, with current axes | A control for \"SVG without per-point nodes\". |\n    93\t\n    94\tFor each library, up to three configurations are published where they exist: the starter default, the least configuration that completes the same reader task, and a tuned configuration that keeps the same visible features (axes, legend, tooltip). Every option that differs from the default is listed with the documentation page that recommends it. A causal claim about one option needs a run that changes only that option. Node counts and the active renderer are recorded during the run.\n    95\t\n    96\tAxylo is compared with a comparably equipped library as well as with uPlot, since uPlot does less. Versions are pinned and bumped once a quarter in a commit of their own. Comparison numbers are published with method, versions and configuration files, including the ones Axylo loses.\n    97\t\n    98\t## 4. The improvement loop\n    99\t\n   100\tOne iteration, written up as a short record in `bench/experiments/NNNN-title.md`:\n   101\t\n   102\t1. **Pick the target.** A workload failing its budget comes first. Otherwise rank by what readers meet most: missed frame deadlines in frequent interactions (hover, streaming, resize) before a rare wait, and a 40 ms stall that happens on every dashboard before a second saved on a million-point chart.\n   103\t2. **Find the bottleneck.** Record a profile of that workload on the production build with source maps. Attribute time to stages (normalize, lookup, layout, scene, mark drawing, DOM text, raster) with `performance.measure` spans available in benchmark builds. Record each stage's share of the workload's critical path.\n   104\t3. **State a hypothesis that can fail,** with a number: \"Layout takes 41% of W-page because every tick label is measured twice; caching by font and string will cut W-page by at least 25%.\"\n   105\t4. **Build the smallest experiment** behind a flag or on a branch, with no refactoring mixed in.\n   106\t5. **Measure** candidate against baseline on the target workload and on every other tracked workload.\n   107\t6. **Decide** by section 5 and write the record, including when the hypothesis was wrong. Negative results stay in the repository.\n   108\t7. **Review.** Someone other than the author of the change checks the measurement and the record before it merges.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
