{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_96","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'version|react-dom' package-lock.json | rg '\"'^22[56-79][0-9]'\"'; nl -ba docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md | sed -n '1,66p'; nl -ba scripts/size.mjs | sed -n '1,174p'; rg -n 'marks.*range|10x100000|\\\\/100' bench/README.md\"","aggregated_output":"2251:      \"version\": \"3.2.1\",\n2264:      \"version\": \"0.0.0\",\n2269:      \"version\": \"0.0.0\",\n2274:        \"react-dom\": \"^18.0.0 || ^19.0.0\"\n     1\t# Decision 0002: SVG paths draw the marks by default (provisional)\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: **provisional working default**, set by the coordinator on 2026-11-18 from the M1 measurements and weakened the same day after an independent review of the benchmark harness (`reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/bench.review.md`, Codex gpt-6.2-sol at effort max). The owner asked for this to be settled by measurement. It is not settled: the measurements support using SVG while the line-chart slice is built and keeping canvas behind the internal switch. They do not establish a shipping winner or a reason to remove either renderer.\n     4\t\n     5\t## What the review changed\n     6\t\n     7\tThe table below is kept as measured. These statements made from it in the first version are withdrawn or narrowed:\n     8\t\n     9\t- **Withdrawn: \"nothing measured favours canvas except all-thread CPU during resize.\"** The recorded heap, DOM-node and layout counts also favour canvas.\n    10\t- **Withdrawn: the resize CPU gap is raster work.** The number is CPU for the whole renderer process; no stage was traced.\n    11\t- **Withdrawn: simplification bounds every chart by its width.** It was shown for line series only; point marks and many-series charts were not measured.\n    12\t- **Withdrawn: an SVG theme change needs no run-time work.** Both renderers recorded six DOM mutations per toggle; \"no canvas drawing call\" is all that was observed.\n    13\t- **Withdrawn: normalization and garbage collection explain the gap to uPlot.** About 71–73% of busy time in the production profiles is unmapped; the normalization share comes from development builds. It is a hypothesis to test, not a finding.\n    14\t- **Narrowed: \"half the renderer memory.\"** The halving is of the increase above a data-only page at pixel ratio 2 (34.2 → 17.7 MiB). Absolute renderer memory was 74.7 → 56.2 MiB, and at pixel ratio 1 the increase was 23.1 → 18.4 MiB.\n    15\t- **Narrowed: \"no frame later than 25 ms.\"** True of the recorded animation-frame callbacks; the window can miss an interval that spans its boundary, and a callback is not a presented frame.\n    16\t- **Void: every 24-chart page row** (page at pixel ratio 1 and 2, resize of the 24-chart page, and the memory row). The harness fix pass found that these runs drew no marks: at 230 × 115 px each tile is below the 120 × 80 px plot minimum of spec L8, so every chart showed its text fallback. The SVG records carry `svgPaths: 0`; the canvas runs held empty canvases. The numbers compare two empty charts and say nothing about the renderers. The harness now rejects a run whose chart drew no marks, and the page workload uses 340 × 200 px tiles.\n    17\t- **Narrowed: verdicts.** 149 metric comparisons were judged without adjustment for choosing among them, on six pairs or fewer per workload, with noise bands from one session and two workload shapes. Two \"SVG lower\" rows allow a gain as small as 0.16 ms (10,000 points) and 6.3 ms (dashboard at pixel ratio 2).\n    18\t\n    19\tEvidence: [bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md](../../bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md). One session of 39 runs, production build, headless Chrome 157, machine under load from other work, A-versus-A noise bands for two workloads only.\n    20\t\n    21\t## What was measured\n    22\t\n    23\tCanvas → SVG, medians of main-thread task time unless stated. \"Lower\" means the 95% interval cleared zero and the noise band where one existed.\n    24\t\n    25\t| Workload | Canvas | SVG | Verdict |\n    26\t|---|---|---|---|\n    27\t| Streaming, per update | 2.26 ms | 1.45 ms | SVG lower |\n    28\t| Theme change, per toggle | 2.68 ms and 27 drawing calls | 2.01 ms and no drawing call | SVG lower |\n    29\t| Resize, one chart of 100,000 points | 915 ms | 472 ms | SVG lower on the main thread |\n    30\t| Same, CPU across all threads | 1,035 ms | 1,770 ms | **SVG higher** |\n    31\t| Resize, 24-chart page | 730 ms | 525 ms | Void: no marks were drawn |\n    32\t| First chart, 10,000 points | 34.0 ms | 28.4 ms | SVG lower |\n    33\t| 24-chart page at pixel ratio 2 | 199 ms | 158 ms | Void: no marks were drawn |\n    34\t| First chart, 3 × 120 | 23.6 ms | 19.8 ms | Inconclusive |\n    35\t| First chart, 100,000 points | 81.8 ms | 83.5 ms | Inconclusive |\n    36\t| First chart, 1,000,000 points | 622 ms | 652 ms | Inconclusive (4 blocks) |\n    37\t| Memory, 24-chart page at pixel ratio 2 | 34.2 MiB renderer, 11.5 MiB GPU, 7.7 MiB backing stores | 17.7 MiB, 2.2 MiB, none | Void: no marks were drawn |\n    38\t\n    39\tNo frame was later than 25 ms in any configuration, so frame pacing does not separate the two.\n    40\t\n    41\t## Why SVG for now\n    42\t\n    43\t1. On the line workloads measured, main-thread task time was lower with SVG or the two could not be told apart.\n    44\t2. Line series are simplified to a few vertices per pixel column before either renderer sees them, so for line charts the path size follows the plot width, not the data.\n    45\t3. SVG follows the page with less code: forced colors and print work through CSS, browser zoom stays sharp, and there is no backing store to size, cap or lose. This is a design argument, not a measurement.\n    46\t\n    47\t## What this does not establish\n    48\t\n    49\t- **GPU rasterization was not measured.** Headless Chrome here does not use a GPU. The resize, pixel-ratio-2 and process-memory results need a headed, GPU-backed rerun before they transfer.\n    50\t- **Dense point marks were not measured.** A scatter chart with tens of thousands of markers is the case most likely to favour canvas.\n    51\t- **One browser, one machine, one session.** Firefox and Safari may order the two differently.\n    52\t- **Many series, pan and zoom, and the budget workloads P4, P7 and P8 were not measured**, and the million-point comparison has four pairs.\n    53\t- Both renderers are far from uPlot in the configurations measured (about 80 ms against 12 ms at 100,000 points; uPlot draws no tooltip or accessibility layer). Where that time goes is not established.\n    54\t\n    55\t## Consequences\n    56\t\n    57\t- The working default is SVG. The canvas renderer stays in the code behind the same internal switch, with the pixel-agreement tests between the two kept.\n    58\t- Before the choice is final: fix the harness defects the review found (renderer identity in the readback, noise bands on replay, failed runs excluded from speed verdicts, gap-aware admission, the frame window), declare the primary endpoints and thresholds in advance, then rerun on a quiet machine with scatter at 10,000, 50,000 and 200,000 points, ten series of 100,000 points, pan and zoom, and the byte cost of keeping both renderers. Removing canvas from the bundle needs that evidence too.\n    59\t- When a real GPU and a second browser are available: rerun the resize and million-point workloads headed. If canvas wins there by more than the shipping thresholds in the performance plan, flip the switch back.\n    60\t- The plan's sections 2.2 and 2.3 are read with SVG in the \"marks\" position: a theme change no longer needs a redraw, and device pixel ratio matters only to simplification.\n     1\t// What a consumer downloads, minified and gzipped, measured from the packed tarballs (spec DX7, P6; plan M7).\n     2\t//\n     3\t//   node scripts/size.mjs           pack packages/*/dist (scripts/pack.mjs --no-build), measure, print, write bench/size-latest.json\n     4\t//   node scripts/size.mjs --check   the same, and exit 1 when a row exceeds its target: total gzip bytes\n     5\t//                                   compared as integers against targetKiB * 1024 (40 KiB means 40,960 B)\n     6\t//   node scripts/size.mjs --no-pack measure the fixtures already in tests/dist/fixtures\n     7\t//\n     8\t// Every row is built the way a consumer's bundler would build it: esbuild, from inside a fixture that installed\n     9\t// the tarball, minified, with the `production` export condition, so only the packed files and their `exports`\n    10\t// are read. The script-tag row is the two files the documented snippet loads, as shipped.\n    11\t//\n    12\t// Stylesheet: the JavaScript carries the stylesheet as a string and applies it at run time, so a bundler page\n    13\t// downloads it inside its JavaScript and needs no axylo.css; the script-tag snippet also links axylo.css, so\n    14\t// that row adds the file. \"total\" is what each page downloads. The targets are provisional (spec DX7) and are\n    15\t// under review, so CI runs this script without --check for now: the flag is correct, but it is not a gate.\n    16\t//\n    17\t// The per-module breakdown cannot come from the packed files, which are one pre-bundled file each: it rebuilds\n    18\t// packages/axylo/src with the release build's production settings (scripts/build.mjs) and reads esbuild's\n    19\t// metafile, then states how far that rebuild is from the packed axylo.js.\n    20\timport { build } from 'esbuild';\n    21\timport fs from 'node:fs';\n    22\timport path from 'node:path';\n    23\timport { gzipSync } from 'node:zlib';\n    24\timport { fixtureGuard, pack } from './pack.mjs';\n    25\t\n    26\tconst root = path.resolve(import.meta.dirname, '..');\n    27\tconst fixtures = path.join(root, 'tests/dist/fixtures');\n    28\tconst LEVEL = 9;\n    29\tconst gzip = bytes => gzipSync(bytes, { level: LEVEL }).length;\n    30\tconst kib = bytes => +(bytes / 1024).toFixed(2);\n    31\t\n    32\t/** Whether `totalBytes` is over a target given in KiB: integer bytes against targetKiB * 1024, never rounded KiB. */\n    33\texport const exceeds = (totalBytes, targetKiB) => targetKiB !== null && totalBytes > targetKiB * 1024;\n    34\t\n    35\tasync function main() {\n    36\t  if (!process.argv.includes('--no-pack')) pack({ build: false, log: () => {} });\n    37\t  const manifest = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.join(root, 'tests/dist/.packed/manifest.json'), 'utf8'));\n    38\t\n    39\t  async function consumer({ cwd, contents, external = [], metafile = false }) {\n    40\t    const r = await build({\n    41\t      stdin: { contents, resolveDir: cwd, loader: 'ts', sourcefile: 'consumer.ts' }, absWorkingDir: cwd, bundle: true, minify: true,\n    42\t      format: 'esm', target: 'es2022', conditions: ['production'], external, write: false, metafile, logLevel: 'silent',\n    43\t      plugins: [fixtureGuard([cwd])],\n    44\t    });\n    45\t    const inputs = Object.keys(r.metafile?.inputs ?? {});\n    46\t    return { bytes: r.outputFiles[0].contents, inputs };\n    47\t  }\n    48\t\n    49\t  const npm = path.join(fixtures, 'npm');\n    50\t  const react = path.join(fixtures, 'react');\n    51\t  const axyloFile = file => fs.readFileSync(path.join(npm, 'node_modules/axylo/dist', file));\n    52\t  const css = axyloFile('axylo.css');\n    53\t\n    54\t  const cases = [\n    55\t    {\n    56\t      entry: 'line only (examples/first-chart/npm.ts)', targetKiB: 25,\n    57\t      build: () => consumer({ cwd: npm, contents: fs.readFileSync(path.join(npm, 'example/npm.ts'), 'utf8') }), cssFile: false,\n    58\t    },\n    59\t    { entry: 'everything (export * from axylo)', targetKiB: 40, build: () => consumer({ cwd: npm, contents: \"export * from 'axylo';\" }), cssFile: false },\n    60\t    { entry: 'script tag (axylo.iife.min.js + axylo.css)', targetKiB: null, build: async () => ({ bytes: axyloFile('axylo.iife.min.js') }), cssFile: true },\n    61\t    {\n    62\t      entry: 'React adapter alone (react, axylo external)', targetKiB: 2, cssFile: false,\n    63\t      build: () => consumer({ cwd: react, contents: \"export * from '@axylo/react';\", external: ['react', 'react/*', 'react-dom', 'axylo'] }),\n    64\t    },\n    65\t  ];\n    66\t\n    67\t  const rows = [];\n    68\t  for (const c of cases) {\n    69\t    const { bytes } = await c.build();\n    70\t    const jsGzip = gzip(bytes);\n    71\t    const cssGzip = c.cssFile ? gzip(css) : 0;\n    72\t    const total = jsGzip + cssGzip;\n    73\t    rows.push({\n    74\t      entry: c.entry, minBytes: bytes.length, jsGzipBytes: jsGzip, cssFileGzipBytes: cssGzip, totalGzipBytes: total,\n    75\t      totalKiB: kib(total), targetKiB: c.targetKiB, targetBytes: c.targetKiB === null ? null : c.targetKiB * 1024,\n    76\t      over: exceeds(total, c.targetKiB),\n    77\t    });\n    78\t  }\n    79\t\n    80\t  console.log(`Download size from the packed tarballs (${manifest.packages.axylo.tarball}, ${manifest.packages['@axylo/react'].tarball}, packed ${manifest.packedAt}); esbuild minify, gzip level ${LEVEL}`);\n    81\t  console.table(rows.map(r => ({\n    82\t    entry: r.entry, 'min B': r.minBytes, 'js gzip B': r.jsGzipBytes, 'css file gzip B': r.cssFileGzipBytes || '—',\n    83\t    'total KiB': r.totalKiB, 'target KiB': r.targetKiB ?? '—', status: r.targetKiB === null ? 'no target' : r.over ? 'OVER' : 'ok',\n    84\t  })));\n    85\t  console.log(`The stylesheet is ${css.length} B, ${gzip(css)} B gzipped as a file; the same text is inside every JavaScript row above.`);\n    86\t\n    87\t  // Where the bytes of \"everything\" are: the source rebuilt as scripts/build.mjs builds dist/axylo.js.\n    88\t  const src = path.join(root, 'packages/axylo/src');\n    89\t  const rebuilt = await build({\n    90\t    entryPoints: [path.join(src, 'index.ts')], bundle: true, target: 'es2022', legalComments: 'none', format: 'esm', minify: true,\n    91\t    define: { __DEV__: 'false' }, write: false, metafile: true, logLevel: 'silent', absWorkingDir: root,\n    92\t  });\n    93\t  const packedProd = axyloFile('axylo.js').length;\n    94\t  const out = Object.values(rebuilt.metafile.outputs)[0];\n    95\t  const modules = Object.entries(out.inputs).map(([file, v]) => ({ file: path.relative(src, path.resolve(root, file)), bytes: v.bytesInOutput }))\n    96\t    .sort((a, b) => b.bytes - a.bytes);\n    97\t  const sum = modules.reduce((n, m) => n + m.bytes, 0);\n    98\t  const byDir = new Map();\n    99\t  for (const m of modules) { const d = m.file.includes(path.sep) ? m.file.split(path.sep)[0] : '(root)'; byDir.set(d, (byDir.get(d) ?? 0) + m.bytes); }\n   100\t  const dirs = [...byDir].sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]).map(([dir, bytes]) => ({ directory: dir, 'min B': bytes, share: `${((100 * bytes) / sum).toFixed(1)}%` }));\n   101\t  console.log(`\\nPer-module breakdown of \"everything\" (source rebuilt with the release settings: ${rebuilt.outputFiles[0].contents.length} B minified; the packed axylo.js is ${packedProd} B, ${rebuilt.outputFiles[0].contents.length === packedProd ? 'identical in size' : `a difference of ${rebuilt.outputFiles[0].contents.length - packedProd} B, so dist was built from different source`}). Minified bytes before gzip; gzip does not split by module.`);\n   102\t  console.table(dirs);\n   103\t  console.table(modules.slice(0, 25).map(m => ({ module: m.file, 'min B': m.bytes, share: `${((100 * m.bytes) / sum).toFixed(1)}%` })));\n   104\t\n   105\t  fs.mkdirSync(path.join(root, 'bench'), { recursive: true });\n   106\t  fs.writeFileSync(path.join(root, 'bench/size-latest.json'), JSON.stringify({\n   107\t    date: new Date().toISOString(),\n   108\t    method: 'esbuild consumer bundles from tests/dist/fixtures (packed tarballs), minify, production condition; gzip level 9',\n   109\t    packed: { at: manifest.packedAt, axylo: manifest.packages.axylo.integrity, react: manifest.packages['@axylo/react'].integrity },\n   110\t    cssFileBytes: css.length, cssFileGzipBytes: gzip(css), rows,\n   111\t    breakdown: { rebuiltMinBytes: rebuilt.outputFiles[0].contents.length, packedMinBytes: packedProd, directories: dirs, modules },\n   112\t  }, null, 1));\n   113\t\n   114\t  if (process.argv.includes('--check') && rows.some(r => r.over)) {\n   115\t    console.error(`size target exceeded: ${rows.filter(r => r.over).map(r => `${r.entry} ${r.totalGzipBytes} B > ${r.targetBytes} B (${r.targetKiB} KiB)`).join('; ')}`);\n   116\t    process.exit(1);\n   117\t  }\n   118\t}\n   119\t\n   120\tif (path.resolve(process.argv[1] ?? '') === import.meta.filename) await main();\n140:| `first` | P1 (`--fixture trend`), P2 (`--size 1x100000`, `1x1000000`) | Create one line chart; any `SxN` (`10x100000` for the renderer decision), `--gaps` | one 800×400 |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
