{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_120","type":"agent_message","text":"The benchmark harness cannot yet make the renderer decision in §5.1. The palette clears its six numerical constraints, but the new dark focus token falls below 3:1 against five series colours. The React adapter fixes ordinary mount and update ordering and produces usable distribution files. Its distribution test has a clean-checkout dependency, and its setup cleanup can replace the original error.\n\nI reviewed the three commits and checked their integration with the core through `df6dc52`, including the in-progress benchmark hook where noted. All checks were read-only. Browser behaviour was assessed from source and committed records. No public contract change is needed for the fixes below.\n\n## A. Benchmark harness · `d83dee1`\n\n### Findings\n\n1. **B · The runner can report a winner without implementing §5.1. Ran Node and traced the decision path.** Twenty identical pairs at 10.0 versus 9.7 ms produce `better` for P1, although its smallest worthwhile improvement is 1 ms. The runner applies a signed noise band or zero. It lacks the declared improvement and regression thresholds for P1, both P2 sizes, P3 task time, P4, P5, P8 and P7 memory. It also lacks the upper-noise-edge floor on both thresholds and the “too noisy to gate” result. P3’s second endpoint needs a comparison of runs with any interval over **25 ms**, and lifecycle needs the two specified windows and the **1%** rule. The 20-complete-pair minimum itself works. **Fix:** predeclare and record the nine endpoint configurations, including **50k scatter first draw**, **10×100k create** and **100k zoom task time per step**. Freeze the latter three thresholds at **10% and 5% of the slower pilot median**. Compute **99.4%** intervals for the family. Apply all three decision branches: loss on an out-of-tolerance regression with no worthwhile win, everyday-workload win counts and the **4×** retention test when both win somewhere, and the SVG tie rule with the **0.5 KiB gzipped** Canvas cost check. Keep resize, DPR 2 and process memory open outside that decision. The plan still needs a rule for equal everyday win counts and an explicit treatment of P3’s two metrics in that count. [Performance plan](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/04-plan-performance.md:139) · [Classification](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/stats.js:78) · [Analysis](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/stats.js:415)\n\n2. **B · An ineligible session can supply a verdict or a supposedly calibrated noise band. Ran the replay CLI and synthetic controls.** The old DPR 1 band is now refused for a DPR 2 replay. I supplied three matching-host, matching-file uPlot A/A records for a Canvas–SVG comparison. Only one had 20 pairs for `taskMs`, yet the loader returned `band from 3 sessions` and used the one available interval. The artifact contains several adapters and both Axylo builds, so matching files alone admits an unrelated control. The runner samples load at session start and end, so it misses the required **one-minute load below 1.0 at the start of every block**. A job uses one Chrome process, which fixes its version within that job. There is no eligibility record to enforce version and environment consistency across the endpoint jobs and their controls. **Fix:** validate each block, pin the decision family to one eligible environment, match the actual adapter/build and refresh conditions, and count qualifying independent sessions per metric. Keep missing or exploratory bands out of gating. [Noise compatibility](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/refs.mjs:51) · [Calibration](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/refs.mjs:85) · [Block loop](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/run.mjs:175)\n\n3. **B · Stratification preserves a false carry-over result at the verdict sample size. Ran the earlier statistical counterexamples.** The earlier AB/BA example has no adapter effect. At eight pairs the current result is `inconclusive`. At 20 the same pattern gives `better`, **[−4, −4] ms**, with **−6 ms AB** and **−2 ms BA**. On the earlier two-point distribution, five 5 ms candidate runs and one 11 ms run still give **[−5, −2] ms**, now withheld at six pairs. Enumerating the stratum counts with the seeded 10,000-resample bootstrap gave about **4.83%** wrong-direction `better` verdicts at 20 pairs on that distribution, whose true median difference is **+1 ms**. **Fix:** use the pilot to test carry-over and interval coverage for tied, skewed and binary endpoints. Standardize starting state or isolate browser processes where order changes the estimate, and withhold renderer conclusions when that effect remains. [Bootstrap](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/stats.js:51) · [Per-order estimates](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/stats.js:425)\n\n4. **B · Admission can still certify a drawing that lost fixture features. Ran the installed Chart.js decimator and synthetic readbacks.** The old 100k gappy fixture still gives **2,981 retained points, 60 retained NaNs out of 5,000, and 144 actual bridges**. It is now rejected. But Chart.js rewrites the `x` of **1,380** retained points, so looking up those coordinates as source indices reports **185** bridges and **43** points at missing data. Separately, Axylo’s pixel check accepted a gap crossed by several short segments and let one stroke satisfy two series. A 50k scatter readback with correct held extents and an empty renderer shell passed the geometry probe because scatter declares `not-applicable`. Its `--clustered` fixture clusters only **y**. **Fix:** carry Chart.js source indices through decimation, match Axylo strokes one-to-one, verify continuous paths across gaps, and admit scatter against actual point geometry and the injected extreme. Use a genuine two-dimensional clustered fixture. The current worktree hook exposes renderer identity and `kept` indices. The adapter discards both, and a flat kept list still needs run boundaries to verify gap topology. [Chart.js readback](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/drawn.js:32) · [Pixel admission](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/admission.js:87) · [Scatter readback](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/axylo-common.js:194) · [Fixture](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/fixtures.js:131) · [Core hook](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:1680)\n\n5. **B · P4 can time a stale readout as a match and drop its slowest moves. Ran a Node adapter probe and percentile counterexample.** The initial create, settle and pointer entry are outside `hover:start` → `hover:end`. Inside are 200 serialized real pointer dispatches, readout polling and waits. Per-move latency starts at `pointermove.timeStamp` and ends in the first rAF callback that accepts the chart’s own picked index. `readoutShows` accepted text `10.24` for an expected `1`. The sweep checks direction, span and at least half as many distinct picks as moves, so it never independently checks the intended datum for each move. With 189 moves at 8 ms, one at 100 ms and ten misses, the harness reports **8 ms p95**. The nearest-rank p95 with misses retained is **100 ms**. **Fix:** target independently known data coordinates, verify the exact committed datum and visible readout, count misses as deadline failures, and validate the presentation boundary with sampled traces. The pointer-transparent, datum-anchored tooltip removes the old interception mechanism by source inspection. The 800×400 sweep does not exercise the reserved strip, though both DOM variants expose the value class. Give each move its own waiter so a callback left over after a timeout cannot release the next move. [Hover window](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:407) · [Axylo match](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/axylo-common.js:153) · [Percentile](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/stats.js:313) · [Tooltip CSS](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/theme/css.ts:176)\n\n6. **B · P7 reports an isolate delta as Axylo-owned memory. Read the CDP fields and ran the summaries against stored and synthetic records.** `mem` samples after forced GC at `blank`, `data` and `chart`. The last sample follows first readiness, one rAF and settle. The claimed `ownedBytes` is the chart-minus-data delta in **isolate-wide JS heap plus backing storage**. Backing storage includes external strings as well as buffers. The metric cannot identify Axylo-owned bytes. `lifecycle` runs 20 warm-up cycles, then waits for readiness and destroys each chart. It collects at 0, 100, 300 and 1,000 by default, reports a fitted slope and listener/node deltas, and admits a new chart after the loop plus an empty container after the last destroy. There is no observer count, replacement peak, slope gate or §5.1 comparison between cycles 1,500–2,000 and 2,000–2,500. **Fix:** count owned buffers directly, keep each memory quantity separate, and sample both lifecycle windows with a gate. The historical DPR 2 PSS figures were **34.23→17.71 MiB incremental** and **74.65→56.21 MiB absolute** for a void dashboard configuration. Whole-renderer CPU and the WebGL probe cannot attribute raster cost. [Memory collection](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/driver.mjs:189) · [Memory summaries](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/stats.js:331) · [Lifecycle](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:506) · [P7 method](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/04-plan-performance.md:54)\n\n7. **B · P3 can still miss a stall, and an acknowledgement is weaker than a displayed update. Ran the old watchdog, lateness and survivor probes.** The boundary helper now counts the old **150 ms** overlap. But `lateWindow([160,177,194,211], 40, 3)` reports three 17 ms intervals and silently starts at 160 ms. The real loop can lack the preceding sample if its first callback stalls. `stream:start` precedes worker dispatch and the first due time, and `stream:end` follows a callback acknowledging the final revision. The region includes dispatch, append, chart work and harness tracking. A coalesced revision credits earlier sequences at the same callback, and the late count uses **1.5× observed refresh** rather than the §5.1 fixed threshold. The old unfinished eight-run candidate now yields zero pairs. The 14-of-20 timeout case yields six conditional pairs and `inconclusive`. Two late candidate runs block a speed win even though the median late-frame count stays zero. With equally many failures on different blocks, 22 survivors can still get a conditional `better`. **Fix:** require samples on both sides of the measured window, separate a revision acknowledgement from presentation, and gate endpoint claims on paired completion and deadline reliability in both directions. [Stream window](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:167) · [Lateness](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/stats.js:195) · [Completion and reliability](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/stats.js:258)\n\n8. **M · The create and zoom paths expose useful measurements with mismatched defaults and incomplete admission. Ran fixture checks and traced the windows.** `first`, `page`, `compact`, `scatter` and `mem` share `create:start` → a fresh task containing adapter conversion and create → readiness → one rAF → settle → `create:end`. Fixture creation, empty-container layout, forced GC and final readback are outside. This is the task-time shape for P1, P2, P5 and P8. The scatter primary is also settle-inclusive task time, while §5.1 names **first draw**. `10×100k` does feed ten typed y columns and exercises the core’s over-eight-series path. The P1/P5 named data matches the research fixture, but requires `--fixture trend`, and Axylo gets a synthetic `Benchmark` title. The generic `axylo` adapter still forces **Canvas**, so it measures a different configuration from the current public SVG default. P8 has the right tile size, count and single series, but admission does not verify marks-only output, `role=\"img\"`, description or zero tab stops. Zoom times 60 API calls plus harness scheduling and readiness through a following rAF per step. Only the final view is admitted, with 10% x-range slack. **Fix:** pin the budget recipes, follow the public default on the budget path, use the first verified presentation for scatter, check P8’s compact semantics, and verify representative intermediate zoom windows as well as the final one. Time-axis epoch milliseconds remain valid zoom inputs. The new half-device-pixel **scatter** tie rule is exercised by none of these runs. [Create window](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:121) · [Zoom window](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:471) · [Axylo defaults](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/axylo-common.js:36) · [Workload defaults](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/run.mjs:29) · [Scatter tie rule](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/lookup/scatter-grid.ts:233)\n\n9. **M · Resize and theme probes still admit incomplete transitions. Reasoned from the current code.** At the narrow end of the 24-chart resize, four columns in 400 px give each tile at most **95.5 px**, below the **120 px** minimum even before margins. `fillReasons` measures the outer SVG or Canvas width, so fallback shells can pass at the narrow end and valid strokes can return by the final probe. The timed resize log begins at the first step callback and stops before the final readiness await, so its improved overlap helper cannot recover intervals it never sampled. Theme compares marks to tokens reported by the chart and checks for an actual change only on the **first** toggle. If both freeze in the dark state, the second toggle can pass. **Fix:** separate responsive fallback from renderer resize timing, check committed drawable geometry and the actual mode at intermediate sizes, bracket the window with frames, and derive expected scheme colours independently on every toggle. The new strip changes layout work for eligible small full charts, including dashboard tiles near the threshold. It bypasses compact charts and does not rescue the 95.5 px resize tiles. [Resize sizing](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.html:15) · [Resize probe](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:343) · [Theme probe](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:297) · [Readout layout](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:1225)\n\n10. **M · Regression screening can clear a candidate that never finishes. Ran a synthetic screen verdict and inspected CI.** A candidate that times out in every block returns `exceeds: false` because its primary metric is unavailable. The CLI consequently skips the retry and prints `within tolerance or inconclusive`. Two-build comparison exists, but PR baseline construction, scheduled confirmations, last-release drift and size enforcement remain unwired. The packed-size record has **101,760 B** for line-only against **25,600 B**, and **101,858 B** for everything against **40,960 B**. It does not measure the marginal Canvas cost. **Fix:** give failed or unavailable screens their own outcome and retry policy, wire the paired jobs and size check, and measure matched packed builds with and without Canvas. [Screening verdict](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/run.mjs:208) · [Retry path](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/run.mjs:280) · [CI](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/.github/workflows/ci.yml:44) · [Packed size record](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/size-latest.json:13)\n\n11. **M · Profile attribution can turn an unmapped segment into a stage sample. Ran a source-map counterexample.** In `AAAA,U`, column 10 begins an explicitly unmapped segment. The mapper discards it and attributes column 11 to the preceding normalization source. New profiles do separate leaf and inclusive samples and separate active and settle intervals. Those changes cannot retrospectively attribute the old unmapped production profiles, recover raster work or explain the uPlot gap. **Fix:** preserve unmapped segments in lookup and add sampled stage/presentation traces plus a matched uPlot profile before making a bottleneck attribution. [Source-map lookup](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/profile-summary.mjs:49) · [Profile windows](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/driver.mjs:178) · [M1 decision](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md:7)\n\n### Disposition of the 18 earlier findings\n\n| Earlier finding | Status | What the current path establishes |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Shipping decision | **Partly** | Decision 0002 is provisional and the dashboards are void. §5.1 is unwired, and the decision still contains an unqualified no-late-frame sentence. |\n| 2. Frame lateness | **Partly** | Overlap calculation and 300-interval accounting improved. Boundary sampling and presentation remain open. |\n| 3. Production profiles | **Fixed with a new defect** | Maps, separate accounting and separate settle profiles exist. Explicit unmapped segments are misattributed. Stage, raster and uPlot evidence remains open. |\n| 4. Drawn fidelity | **Partly** | Missing counts and probe geometry are now checked. Source-index, connected-gap, series matching and scatter holes remain. |\n| 5. CPU and memory claims | **Partly** | The record narrows the attribution and denominator. P7 ownership, peak and lifecycle gates remain open. |\n| 6. Replay noise | **Fixed** | Stored replay preserves `inconclusive` and its **[−63.97, 51.54] ms** task band. Reanalysis validates controls or requires explicit zero-only mode to discard them. |\n| 7. Failed work wins | **Partly** | All three original examples are blocked as speed wins. Reliability handling remains one-sided, and all-timeout screening can clear. |\n| 8. P1/P2/P5 proxies | **Partly** | Named data, 20-block defaults and constant-time stream acknowledgement are in place. Default options and verified presentation remain open. |\n| 9. Renderer identity | **Fixed** | The two explicit adapters pass their requested renderer and compare it with committed renderer elements. The generic adapter’s Canvas default is a separate budget issue. |\n| 10. Noise matching and contention | **Partly** | DPR and several environment mismatches now fail. Adapter, per-metric calibration and block validity are incomplete. |\n| 11. Six-pair inference | **Partly** | Both seeds are validated and recorded. Six pairs cannot win, and the discrete counterexample still wins in the wrong direction at 20. |\n| 12. AB/BA carry-over | **Partly** | Balanced sampling, warm-up and order estimates are present. The false 20-pair aggregate win remains. |\n| 13. Resize and theme | **Partly** | Fixed width steps, narrow-width probe, per-mark checks and separate counters exist. The probes can still accept incomplete transitions. |\n| 14. Missing workloads | **Partly** | The new entry points exist. P4/P7 and scatter admission have the gaps above. Packed gzip sizing is separate. The planned row/irregular-x, mixed page, replace, keyboard/table, busy-page, cold-load and broader chart/browser cases remain open. |\n| 15. Cross-library equivalence | **Partly** | The README feature matrix describes the starter differences. A matched reader task and contemporaneous no-op control are still needed for an equivalence claim. |\n| 16. Regression detection | **Partly** | Immutable two-build mode and retry exist. Failure handling and the scheduled gates remain open. |\n| 17. Provenance | **Fixed** | The runner pins per-session artifacts and checks served bytes. The legacy generator reads pinned history/profile IDs and flags profile hash mismatches. |\n| 18. Overstated tests | **Fixed** | The earlier test gaps gained direct cases. The new workload smoke uses smaller hover, memory and lifecycle cases, so it establishes wiring only. |\n\n**Ran:** The six file suites finished `tests 47 / pass 47 / fail 0`. Six additional inference tests finished `tests 6 / pass 6 / fail 0`. The inference suite’s remaining CLI test hit `spawnSync EPERM` while capturing stderr in this sandbox. Invoking both invalid-seed cases directly produced the expected errors and exit status 2. The write-dependent suites and browser workloads were outside this run.\n\n- **Sound · Replay.** The legacy stored result reproduces its original verdict and noise band.\n- **Sound · Renderer selection.** The explicit Canvas and SVG flags and the identity predicate passed the Node adapter checks.\n- **Sound · Fixtures and scheduling.** The named trend columns match the research fixture, the worker clock is independent, and scheduled blocks balance AB and BA.\n- **Sound · Artifact provenance.** The source and report paths pin bytes and identify legacy evidence by run and profile ID.\n\n## B. Palette refit · `52bbdf0`\n\n### Findings\n\n1. **B · The new dark focus token fails the adjacent-colour target. Ran WCAG contrast arithmetic and traced the focus geometry.** `#e3e8f0` gives **14.433:1** on `#16181d`. Against dark slots 1–8 it gives **1.934, 2.446, 1.195, 4.424, 3.739, 1.316 and 1.682 and 3.230:1**, respectively. Blue, orange, green, cyan and pink fail. The square datum outline can cross neighbouring marks and uses a single focus ink. Its OKLab separation cannot establish this contrast. In fact, the background and purple force a light single ink, and even white cannot reach 3:1 against the green. **Fix:** use a visible two-tone outline or a guaranteed background surround, for both the plot and datum indicators where they can cross marks. Check the actual adjacent colours. WCAG **1.4.11** supplies the adjacent-colour test. **2.4.11** is Focus Not Obscured and requires a layout check. **2.4.13** separately compares the same pixels in focused and unfocused states. [Token](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/theme/palette.ts:45) · [Datum outline](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/focus.ts:252) · [WCAG 1.4.11](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/non-text-contrast.html) · [WCAG 2.4.11](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/focus-not-obscured-minimum.html) · [WCAG 2.4.13](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/focus-appearance.html)\n\n2. **m · The simulated preview has a smaller margin than the byte model. Ran pixel extraction on the committed PNGs.** Light tritan slot 7 is `#b30f41` in the preview and `#b20f41` in the independently rounded model. The light tritan 5–7 distance is **0.100475** in the image versus **0.102175** in the model. The PNG still clears 0.10, and every other sampled channel matched. **Fix:** distinguish the image from the rounding model in the decision and retain a PNG-interior check. [Decision’s byte claim](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:55) · [Preview filters](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/research/palette/preview.html:18)\n\n3. **m · The record checks cover fewer fields than they claim. Ran mutations in memory.** A wrong closest pair, a **0.01** contrast change, zeroed OKLCH coordinates and a stroke median of 99 each failed. All **32** palette tests still passed with a plausible but false stroke p10/median of **1.01/1.02**, and with false stored token, simulated-fill, lightness and edit-count fields. The scorer checks browser-record colours and backgrounds before importing them, but it rounds its Chrome distance before its pass/fail comparison. **Fix:** check the published fields against their independent calculation or the pinned raw measurement record, and apply the limit before rounding. Narrow the test’s “every measured field” claim to the fields it verifies. [Record test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/theme/palette.test.ts:234) · [Stroke assertions](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/theme/palette.test.ts:287) · [Scorer](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/research/palette/score.mjs:68)\n\n4. **m · Two corrected passages still overstate their evidence. Ran the record comparison and reasoned from the stroke sampler.** The dark green DPR 1 weighted p10 is **8.32 Canvas / 8.23 SVG**, a **0.09** difference against the stated maximum of 0.05. Dark cyan differs by 0.07. Rounded core p10 equality does not recover the full-coverage count. The dark first-three normal closest pair is **2–3** at **0.251291**. The JSON is correct and the decision says **1–3**. The source palette comment also retains “left no room” for the former focus blue after the decision qualified that result as a timed search. **Fix:** label the stroke rows by renderer, retain raw coverage if claiming a count, correct the pair, and use the bounded search claim in the source comment. [Prefix table](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:86) · [Stroke conclusion](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:122) · [Palette comment](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/theme/palette.ts:11)\n\n### Independent measurements\n\nI used linear sRGB → D65 XYZ → LMS → OKLab, WCAG luminance and full-severity Machado matrices. I rescored the recorded fill pixels directly. The independently rounded JSON values and pair identities agreed for the series, prefixes, token distances and fill rows I recomputed.\n\n| Slot | Light OKLCH | Light contrast | Dark OKLCH | Dark contrast | Hue shift |\n|---|---|---:|---|---:|---:|\n| 1 | .65984 / .14968 / 254.09° | 3.12186 | .73054 / .13525 / 251.85° | 7.46150 | 2.24° |\n| 2 | .67998 / .17508 / 51.91° | 3.05603 | .68092 / .15018 / 53.98° | 5.90148 | 2.07° |\n| 3 | .43000 / .14017 / 141.05° | 7.66859 | .86186 / .12085 / 133.26° | 12.07884 | 7.79° |\n| 4 | .43462 / .20512 / 310.08° | 8.93329 | .54393 / .17690 / 311.58° | 3.26215 | 1.50° |\n| 5 | .58678 / .13026 / 18.31° | 4.44794 | .57968 / .15057 / 31.95° | 3.86024 | 13.64° |\n| 6 | .54943 / .11522 / 234.15° | 4.75798 | .83482 / .11017 / 225.24° | 10.96692 | 8.91° |\n| 7 | .48859 / .17504 / 354.96° | 6.96149 | .78591 / .11937 / 0.71° | 8.58329 | 5.75° |\n| 8 | .66837 / .02645 / 283.13° | 3.02637 | .59924 / .03095 / 274.34° | 4.46895 | 8.79° |\n\nPrefix minima below are **analytic / byte-rounded**, in normal, protan, deutan and tritan order.\n\n| Prefix | Light | Dark |\n|---|---|---|\n| First 3 | .31936/.31936 · .15557/.15541 · .26996/.27056 · .24313/.24141 | .25129/.25129 · .24686/.24606 · .17800/.17750 · .15849/.15906 |\n| First 4 | .28436/.28436 · .15557/.15541 · .21746/.21728 · .15730/.15747 | .24553/.24553 · .24686/.24606 · .17800/.17750 · .15849/.15906 |\n| First 5 | .13533/.13533 · .10289/.10281 · .13599/.13716 · .10227/.10300 | .11640/.11640 · .12566/.12503 · .10852/.10869 · .10337/.10214 |\n| First 6 | .12427/.12427 · .10289/.10281 · .11728/.11720 · .10227/.10290 | .11640/.11640 · .12392/.12411 · .10852/.10869 · .10337/.10214 |\n| All 8 | .12403/.12403 · .10289/.10281 · .10342/.10345 · .10227/.10217 | .11640/.11640 · .10370/.10304 · .10301/.10217 · .10233/.10214 |\n\n| Other check | Light | Dark |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Nearest focus / reference / muted | .100772 / .152348 / .110171 | .138042 / .104214 / .113348 |\n| 25% fill, ideal / round-alpha / floor-alpha / Chrome Canvas / Chrome SVG | .063696 / .063379 / .063351 / .063379 / .063379 | .063836 / .063524 / .064321 / .062652 / .062652 |\n| 80% fill, same order | .201393 / .201561 / .201561 / .201561 / .201561 | .178653 / .179037 / .179037 / .179152 / .179152 |\n| 25% fill, worst within one byte per channel | .056008 | .056477 |\n\nI also imported the installed `d3-scale-chromatic` **3.1.1** exports directly. The white-background counts below 3:1 reproduced as **5/10, 6/10, 4/10, 2/8 and 5/9** for Tableau 10, Observable 10, Category 10, Dark2 and Set1. All five first-eight distance rows reproduced at published precision, including Tableau’s red–green deutan distance of **0.006640**. [Reference record](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/research/palette/references.json)\n\n### More even light scheme\n\nI found the following pair through a wider hue search and independently rescored the resulting hex bytes. I allowed the dark partner to change while preserving `#64acfa`, both backgrounds and all tokens. The search holding **all eight** shipped dark colours fixed did not find a match. The example uses a bluer sixth slot and a green-grey neutral.\n\n```text\nLight  #1e97fa #c7872b #0f6f48 #8636c1 #c61405 #4f81b7 #921184 #8c9695\nDark   #64acfa #fbab2d #96e8c6 #9f46ae #fe573f #387bf0 #d571aa #5f6866\n```\n\n| Independently rescored | Light | Dark |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| OKLab L range | .460259–.672961 | .508900–.868833 |\n| Maximum OKLCH chroma | .207247 | .206878 |\n| Minimum background contrast | 3.030786 | 3.093900 |\n| All-eight minimum, analytic / bytes | .102381 / .102584 | .104403 / .105158 |\n| First-four minimum, analytic / bytes | .147095 / .146050 | .142679 / .141610 |\n| Nearest focus / reference / muted | .116345 / .129962 / .113856 | .112977 / .114918 / .120400 |\n| Minimum 25% fill across ideal and both byte models | .063153 | .064058 |\n| Minimum 80% fill across the same models | .209047 | .168102 |\n\nMaximum cross-scheme hue shift is **14.984°**. This meets the published solver margins. Browser rasterisation of the alternate pair is unverified.\n\n### Disposition of the nine earlier findings\n\n| Earlier finding | Status | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Area swatches lose shape | **Fixed in the core** | Ran eight real swatch paths in Node and got eight distinct markers over the chips. Legend and tooltip share that path. The full rendered A5 suite was outside this run. |\n| 2. 25% fill raster failure | **Fixed for isolated fills** | Both recorded renderers clear 0.06 on both backgrounds. Overlap is outside the declared test. |\n| 3. Simulated preview pairs below 0.10 | **Fixed with a new defect** | Sampled PNG pairs pass. The claimed equality to the rounding model is off by one byte. |\n| 4. Lightness spread claimed necessary | **Fixed in the decision** | The text distinguishes finite search from proof. The source comment needs the same qualification. |\n| 5. Explorer timeout prunes orders | **Fixed** | Ran timeout and completed-prune cases. Traced the feasible-incumbent branch. Only a completed proof prunes the full order. |\n| 6. Stroke claims | **Partly** | The weighted statistic is now scoped to legibility. The numerical renderer-agreement and coverage wording still need correction. |\n| 7. Record cross-check | **Partly** | Pair identities, OKLCH and precision checks caught the old mutations. Plausible false stroke and unverified record fields passed. |\n| 8. Hand edits claimed fragile | **Fixed** | Independent enumeration reproduced **41/46 light** and **46/47 dark** passing edits. |\n| 9. Reference palettes | **Fixed** | The installed arrays and all five measured rows reproduced. |\n\n**Ran:** The in-memory palette mutation baseline finished `32 passed, 0 failed`. Both final fixed-grid searches completed and reproduced the refit, in **78 light** and **17,539 dark** nodes.\n\n- **Sound · Opaque palette.** Both schemes pass the contrast and separation limits under the four requested vision models.\n- **Sound · Isolated fills.** The ideal, byte-model and committed Chrome pixel rows clear 0.06 at both opacities.\n- **Sound · Feasibility.** The wider search produced a pair satisfying the requested lightness floor, chroma cap and solver margins.\n- **Sound · References.** The versioned D3 inputs and their recorded results match.\n\n## C. React adapter · `a63bbee`\n\n### Findings\n\n1. **B · The distribution test assumes the core has already been built. Reasoned from CI and the build path.** On a clean checkout CI calls `npm test` before `npm run build`. The adapter’s `dist.test.ts` immediately builds the adapter, whose declaration build resolves `axylo` through the core’s `dist/index.d.ts`. My declaration and consumer typechecks passed with that file present. **Fix:** run distribution tests after the root build, or have the suite build both packages in the required order. Synchronize the stale `react-dom` peer in the workspace lockfile as part of the package fix. [CI order](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/.github/workflows/ci.yml:28) · [Distribution setup](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/dist.test.ts:19) · [Declaration build](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/build.mjs:38) · [Lockfile](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/package-lock.json:2268)\n\n2. **M · A saved ref can expose a chart between ref detachment and passive teardown. Ran the adapter effects with hook doubles and inspected React’s traversal.** In an ordinary visible commit React lays out descendants first. The adapter creates or patches and publishes handlers before its imperative handle and the ancestor’s layout effect. On concurrent Suspense hide, React detaches the layout ref while this adapter keeps the chart alive. Reveal reapplies props before republishing the ref. A caller outside the hidden boundary can run earlier, however, and a saved wrapper still points to the hidden chart with its previous props. The same wrapper remains live after deletion’s ref detachment until passive cleanup. A parent that actually runs before the child’s layout effect has no guarantee of a current chart. **Fix:** tie external getter availability to layout attachment and queue imperative work until the ref is published. Keep the internal chart alive across an ordinary Suspense hide if retaining its appended data and zoom is desired. Normal document-free React 18 SSR selects the passive hook, and its server dispatcher also treats the imperative handle as a no-op. The hook choice is made at module import, so a document installed later in the same runtime leaves it on the passive path. [Adapter lifecycle](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:53) · [React 18 commit traversal](https://github.com/facebook/react/blob/v18.2.0/packages/react-reconciler/src/ReactFiberCommitWork.new.js#L2354) · [React 18 server dispatcher](https://github.com/facebook/react/blob/v18.2.0/packages/react-server/src/ReactFizzHooks.js#L504) · [Suspense semantics](https://react.dev/reference/react/Suspense)\n\n3. **M · Setup cleanup can replace the exception the boundary receives. Ran the actual adapter setup with a chart double.** When `chart.on()` throws object A and `destroy()` succeeds, A escapes unchanged. When `destroy()` also throws object B, only B escapes. Ordinary create and update exceptions already go straight to React’s commit-error handling, so a surviving boundary can get the original object. Passive unmount clears `live` before calling `destroy()`, so a passive disposal error is a separate commit error and the saved getter is null after that cleanup. **Fix:** preserve the setup exception and report a disposal failure separately. Test both throws and a throwing passive teardown. [Setup and cleanup](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:68)\n\n4. **m · Structural comparison and option copying still lose edge-case changes. Ran Node counterexamples.** A one-object cycle and a two-object cycle under an otherwise identical field spec compare equal and yield a null patch. The visited-pair guard terminates recursion but allows one object to correspond to several. This matters for opaque extra or invalid fields. Ordinary hole-to-value updates work, though `i in x` treats an inherited index as present. An own enumerable `__proto__` disappears during option copying into `{}`, before core unknown-option validation. **Fix:** compare documented option shapes or treat cycles as opaque once detected, check own array indices, and construct option and patch records without prototype setters. [Option copying and diff](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:26) · [Comparator](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:65)\n\n5. **m · The new development hint can throw on data the core accepts. Ran the core validator and adapter sampler.** Replace typed columns with equivalent x/y columns and a new unused `ArrayLike` column whose first entry has a throwing getter. Core `validate('line', …)` returns no diagnostics. The hint reads that unplotted endpoint and throws before `diff` or `update`. The older column hint compared identities and skipped the endpoint read. **Fix:** make the sampler best-effort so an unreadable sample skips the hint and the data replacement still reaches the core. [Hint call](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:85) · [Endpoint sampling](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:93)\n\n6. **m · Forwarding the host `id` does not restore the documented diagnostic name fallback. Reasoned from the adapter and core.** The React div now correctly gets `id=\"sessions\"`. Without a title the controller uses `host.id`, supplied by the core’s internal ID allocator. A diagnostic can therefore still name the generated ID rather than `sessions`. The rest of the ARIA routing is appropriate for the current API. `labelledBy` and `description` reach the inner figure, whose `aria-describedby` points to the generated description and preserves required disclosures. Arbitrary `aria-*` is validated as an unknown chart option. **Fix:** use the author’s element ID for diagnostic naming in the core while keeping internal IDs for its DOM relationships. [React host props](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:100) · [Allocated ID](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/chart.ts:61) · [Diagnostic name](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts:334) · [Figure semantics](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/figure.ts:193)\n\n### Disposition of the 14 earlier findings\n\n| Earlier finding | Status | What the current path establishes |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Missing distribution | **Fixed with a new defect** | Both JS exports and the declaration entry work with dist present. Clean-checkout test ordering is broken. |\n| 2. Ref and patch call order | **Partly** | Ordinary visible commits and Suspense reveal have the intended ordering. Saved wrappers can expose an unavailable or stale chart before its layout effect or passive cleanup. |\n| 3. Stale handlers at commit | **Fixed** | The layout effect publishes handlers before chart calls in ordinary descendant-first commits. Hidden or earlier callers have the same attachment limit as finding 2. |\n| 4. Explicit `undefined` | **Fixed** | Adding or removing the own key emits an own `undefined` reset. |\n| 5. Comparator overflow | **Fixed with a new defect** | The cycle and exception guards stop overflow. Distinct cyclic graphs can compare equal. |\n| 6. Sparse arrays | **Fixed** | Ordinary holes and explicit entries compare symmetrically. Inherited indices are an edge case. |\n| 7. Host attributes | **Fixed** | `id`, `className`, `style` and `data-*` reach the div without entering chart options. The remaining diagnostic ID fallback is in the core. |\n| 8. Line-only tree-shaking | **Fixed** | Both emitted adapter entries retain pure annotations. I bundled each against the actual core dist and confirmed the four unused adapter components were dropped. This verifies adapter pruning. The shared core and the packed P6 byte result are separate. |\n| 9. Repeat traversal | **Fixed** | Successful no-op comparisons advance `applied`, so stable replacement identity is cheap thereafter. |\n| 10. Enumeration | **Partly** | Ordinary own and inherited lookups are corrected. Own `__proto__` is lost during copying. |\n| 11. Memoization hint | **Fixed with a new defect** | The warning is per instance and accurately describes sampling. The new column endpoint read can throw. |\n| 12. Deferred effect errors | **Fixed** | Create and update errors now escape directly from the layout effect. No child state update is needed to deliver them. |\n| 13. Setup failure disposal | **Fixed with a new defect** | Disposal is attempted after registration fails. A throwing disposer replaces the original error. |\n| 14. Teardown test strength | **Fixed** | The test source now counts destroys, unmounts before the first frame, tracks pending work and checks saved handles after cleanup. It does not cover the interval before passive cleanup. |\n\nThe adapter derives its chart props from the current core types. `labelledBy`, `description`, `state`, `messages`, `zoom: 'touch'`, chart-specific compact and layout options, and whole-option resets flow through without adapter-specific conversion. `hover`, `select` and `zoom` payloads are passed through unchanged, including `Date`, calendar and wall-clock strings, category or numeric x, and null zoom resets. Donut JSX still excludes `onZoom`, and the shared raw `Chart` ref still has the core’s `zoom()` method and its applicability validation. [Adapter types and forwarding](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:30) · [Current core events and handle](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/public.ts:127)\n\n**Ran:** The React patch and core theme suites finished `Test Files 4 passed (4) / Tests 70 passed (70)`. `npm run typecheck` and the adapter consumer typecheck exited 0. The in-memory production build matched the existing dist byte-for-byte at **1,427 B gzip**, with one leading `'use client'` directive. Both package conditions rendered in Node. React 18 client execution, browser hydration, Fast Refresh and a clean packed install were outside this verification.\n\n- **Sound · SSR and distribution exports.** Both entry conditions resolve, and all five React 19 server renders produced an empty host div and a null ref.\n- **Sound · Ordinary commit order and patches.** The child layout effect applies props and publishes handlers before its ref on visible commits.\n- **Sound · Core API forwarding.** The public option and event types flow through without retagging x values or changing their representation.\n- **Sound · Adapter tree-shaking.** Both emitted entries retain the annotations required for a `Line`-only consumer to discard the other four adapters."}}
